ount Balance; High and Low Temperatures","Practice problems for Lesson 13","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 7.13 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/13.html"},{"label":"Lesson 13 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-13"}],"questions":["An account balance of -$25 means what?","A point (3, -8) shows day 3 and temperature. What is the temperature?"],"answers":["You owe $25","-8 degrees"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-7-14","title":"Lesson 14: Distances on a Coordinate Plane","steps":["Warm-up: Coordinate Patterns","Activities: Signs of Numbers in Coordinates; Finding Distances on a Coordinate Plane","Practice problems for Lesson 14","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 7.14 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/14.html"},{"label":"Lesson 14 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-14"}],"questions":["What is the distance between (5, 2) and (-5, 2)?","What is the distance between (1, -3) and (1, 4)?"],"answers":["10 units","7 units"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-7-15","title":"Lesson 15: Shapes on the Coordinate Plane","steps":["Warm-up: Figuring Out The Coordinate Plane","Activities: Plotting Polygons; Four Quadrants of A-Maze-ing","Practice problems for Lesson 15","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 7.15 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/15.html"},{"label":"Lesson 15 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-15"}],"questions":["A rectangle has corners (-2, 2), (3, 2), (3, -1), (-2, -1). What is its perimeter?","What is that rectangle''s area?"],"answers":["16 units","15 square units"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-7-16","title":"Lesson 16: Common Factors","steps":["Warm-up: Figures Made of Squares","Activities: Diego’s Bake Sale; Greatest Common Factor","Practice problems for Lesson 16","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 7.16 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/16.html"},{"label":"Lesson 16 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-16"}],"questions":["What is the greatest common factor of 12 and 18?","You have 24 cookies and 36 brownies to put equally into bags with nothing left. What is the most bags?"],"answers":["6","12"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-7-17","title":"Lesson 17: Common Multiples","steps":["Warm-up: Notice and Wonder: Multiples","Activities: The Florist’s Order; Least Common Multiple; Prizes on Grand Opening Day","Practice problems for Lesson 17","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 7.17 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/17.html"},{"label":"Lesson 17 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-17"}],"questions":["What is the least common multiple of 6 and 8?","Rolls come in 4s and hot dogs in 6s. Least of each to have equal numbers?"],"answers":["24","12 of each"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-7-18","title":"Lesson 18: Using Common Multiples and Common Factors","steps":["Warm-up: Keeping a Steady Beat","Activities: Factors and Multiples; More Factors and Multiples; Factors and Multiples Bingo","Practice problems for Lesson 18","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 7.18 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/18.html"},{"label":"Lesson 18 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/7/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-18"}],"questions":["One drum beats every 3 seconds, another every 4. When do they first beat together?","What is the GCF of 30 and 45?"],"answers":["At 12 seconds","15"]}]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-u8","title":"Unit 8: Data Sets and Distributions","lessons":[{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-1","title":"Lesson 1: Got Data?","steps":["Warm-up: Dots of Data","Activities: Surveying the Class; Numerical and Categorical Data","Practice problems for Lesson 1","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.1 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/1.html"},{"label":"Lesson 1 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-1"}],"questions":["Is ''favorite color'' numerical or categorical data?","Is ''height in cm'' numerical or categorical data?"],"answers":["Categorical","Numerical"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-2","title":"Lesson 2: Statistical Questions","steps":["Warm-up: Pencils on A Plot","Activities: What’s in the Data?; What Makes a Statistical Question?; Sifting for Statistical Questions","Practice problems for Lesson 2","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.2 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/2.html"},{"label":"Lesson 2 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-2"}],"questions":["Is ''How tall am I?'' a statistical question?","Is ''How tall are the students in my class?'' a statistical question?"],"answers":["No, it has only one answer and no variability","Yes"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-3","title":"Lesson 3: Representing Data Graphically","steps":["Warm-up: Curious about Caps","Activities: Estimating Caps; Been There, Done That!; Favorite Summer Sports","Practice problems for Lesson 3","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.3 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/3.html"},{"label":"Lesson 3 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-3"}],"questions":["Which graph suits categorical data, a bar graph or a dot plot?","What do we call information showing all data values and how often they occur?"],"answers":["A bar graph","A distribution"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-4","title":"Lesson 4: Dot Plots","steps":["Warm-up: Pizza Toppings (Part 1)","Activities: Pizza Toppings (Part 2); Homework Time","Practice problems for Lesson 4","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.4 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/4.html"},{"label":"Lesson 4 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-4"}],"questions":["A dot plot shows 1,1,2,2,2,3,5. Which value is most common?","How many data points are there?"],"answers":["2","7"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-5","title":"Lesson 5: Using Dot Plots to Answer Statistical Questions","steps":["Warm-up: Packs on Backs","Activities: On the Phone; Click-Clack","Practice problems for Lesson 5","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.5 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/5.html"},{"label":"Lesson 5 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-5"}],"questions":["Data: 2, 3, 3, 4, 8. Which value seems unusual?","Name two features you can describe for a distribution."],"answers":["8","Its center and its spread"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-6","title":"Lesson 6: Histograms","steps":["Warm-up: Dog Show (Part 1)","Activities: Dog Show (Part 2); Population of States","Practice problems for Lesson 6","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.6 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/6.html"},{"label":"Lesson 6 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-6"}],"questions":["A histogram bin is 10-20. Does it count a value of 20?","Are histograms used for numerical or categorical data?"],"answers":["No, 20 goes in the next bin (20-30)","Numerical"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-7","title":"Lesson 7: Using Histograms to Answer Statistical Questions","steps":["Warm-up: Which One Doesn’t Belong: Questions","Activities: Measuring Earthworms; Tall and Taller Players","Practice problems for Lesson 7","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.7 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/7.html"},{"label":"Lesson 7 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-7"}],"questions":["Weights range from 10 to 34 kg. Using bins of width 5 starting at 10, how many bins?","Which is better for showing exact values: dot plot or histogram?"],"answers":["5","Dot plot"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-8","title":"Lesson 8: Describing Distributions on Histograms","steps":["Warm-up: Which One Doesn’t Belong: Histograms","Activities: Sorting Histograms; Getting to School","Practice problems for Lesson 8","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.8 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/8.html"},{"label":"Lesson 8 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-8"}],"questions":["A histogram has one peak in the middle and looks the same on both sides. Describe its shape.","A histogram has a long tail to the right. What is that called?"],"answers":["Symmetric","Skewed right"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-9","title":"Lesson 9: Interpreting the Mean as Fair Share","steps":["Warm-up: Close to Four","Activities: Spread Out and Share; Getting to School","Practice problems for Lesson 9","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.9 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/9.html"},{"label":"Lesson 9 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-9"}],"questions":["Find the mean of 4, 6, 7, 3.","Five friends have 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 stickers and share fairly. How many does each get?"],"answers":["5","6"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-10","title":"Lesson 10: Finding and Interpreting the Mean as the Balance Point","steps":["Warm-up: Which One Doesn’t Belong: Division","Activities: Travel Times (Part 1); Travel Times (Part 2)","Practice problems for Lesson 10","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.10 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/10.html"},{"label":"Lesson 10 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-10"}],"questions":["Find the mean of 12, 15, 9, 20, 14.","Why is the mean called the balance point?"],"answers":["14","Distances to the values below it equal distances to the values above it"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-11","title":"Lesson 11: Deviation from the Mean","steps":["Warm-up: Shooting Hoops (Part 1)","Activities: Shooting Hoops (Part 2); Shooting Hoops (Part 3); Game of 22","Practice problems for Lesson 11","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.11 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/11.html"},{"label":"Lesson 11 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-11"}],"questions":["Data: 2, 4, 6, 8. Mean is 5. What is the MAD?","What does a small MAD tell you?"],"answers":["2","The values are close to the mean (little spread)"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-12","title":"Lesson 12: Using Mean and MAD to Make Comparisons","steps":["Warm-up: Number Talk: Decimal Division","Activities: Which Player Would You Choose?; Swimmers Over the Years","Practice problems for Lesson 12","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.12 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/12.html"},{"label":"Lesson 12 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-12"}],"questions":["Team A has mean 10, MAD 1. Team B has mean 10, MAD 4. Which is more consistent?","Find the mean of 1.2, 1.6, 2.0."],"answers":["Team A","1.6"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-13","title":"Lesson 13: The Median of a Data Set","steps":["Warm-up: The Plot of the Story","Activities: Siblings in the House; Finding the Middle","Practice problems for Lesson 13","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.13 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/13.html"},{"label":"Lesson 13 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-13"}],"questions":["Find the median of 3, 8, 1, 6, 9.","Find the median of 2, 4, 7, 10."],"answers":["6","5.5"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-14","title":"Lesson 14: Comparing Mean and Median","steps":["Warm-up: Heights of Presidents","Activities: The Tallest and the Smallest in the World; Mean or Median?","Practice problems for Lesson 14","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.14 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/14.html"},{"label":"Lesson 14 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-14"}],"questions":["Data: 2, 3, 3, 4, 40. Which better shows a typical value, mean or median?","What is the mean of that data?"],"answers":["The median (3)","10.4"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-15","title":"Lesson 15: Quartiles and Interquartile Range","steps":["Warm-up: Notice and Wonder: Two Parties","Activities: The Five-Number Summary; Range and Interquartile Range","Practice problems for Lesson 15","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.15 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/15.html"},{"label":"Lesson 15 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-15"}],"questions":["Data: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13. What are Q1, median, and Q3?","What is the IQR?"],"answers":["Q1 = 3, median = 7, Q3 = 11","8"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-16","title":"Lesson 16: Box Plots","steps":["Warm-up: Notice and Wonder: Puppy Weights","Activities: Human Box Plot; Studying Blinks","Practice problems for Lesson 16","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.16 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/16.html"},{"label":"Lesson 16 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-16"}],"questions":["Name the five numbers a box plot shows.","A box goes from 12 to 20. What is the IQR?"],"answers":["Minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum","8"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-17","title":"Lesson 17: Using Box Plots","steps":["Warm-up: Hours of Slumber","Activities: Info Gap: Sea Turtles; Paper Planes","Practice problems for Lesson 17","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.17 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/17.html"},{"label":"Lesson 17 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-17"}],"questions":["Box plot A has median 3.5, box plot B has median 5. Which group is typically larger?","Which is a measure of spread used with the median?"],"answers":["B","The IQR"]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-8-18","title":"Lesson 18: Using Data to Solve Problems","steps":["Warm-up: Wild Bears","Activities: Math Homework (Part 1); Math Homework (Part 2); Will the Yellow Perch Survive?","Practice problems for Lesson 18","Read the lesson summary and explain it back in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 8.18 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/18.html"},{"label":"Lesson 18 practice problems","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/8/practice_problems.html#practice-problems-lesson-plan-18"}],"questions":["Cookie weights have mean 21 g and MAD 2 g. Is a 30 g cookie typical?","For skewed data, which pair should you report: mean & MAD or median & IQR?"],"answers":["No, it is far from the mean","Median and IQR"]}]},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-u9","title":"Unit 9: Putting it All Together","lessons":[{"id":"im-grade-6-math-9-1","title":"Lesson 1: Fermi Problems","steps":["Activities: Ant Trek; Stacks and Stacks of Cereal Boxes; Covering the Washington Monument","Work through the project, showing your reasoning and assumptions","Share your findings with a parent or sibling"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 9.1 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/9/1.html"}],"questions":["Estimate how many steps it takes to walk 1 mile if one step is about 2.5 feet (1 mile = 5,280 ft)."],"answers":["About 2,100 steps"],"notes":"Culminating or exploration lesson: no summary or practice set; let it run long if the project takes off."},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-9-2","title":"Lesson 2: If Our Class Were the World","steps":["Activities: All 7.4 Billion of Us; About the People in the World; If Our Class Were the World","Work through the project, showing your reasoning and assumptions","Share your findings with a parent or sibling"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 9.2 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/9/2.html"}],"questions":["If 60% of the world lives in Asia and your class has 25 students, how many would represent Asia?"],"answers":["15"],"notes":"Culminating or exploration lesson: no summary or practice set; let it run long if the project takes off."},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-9-3","title":"Lesson 3: Rectangle Madness","steps":["Activities: Squares in Rectangles; More Rectangles, More Squares; Finding Equivalent Fractions; It’s All About Fractions","Work through the project, showing your reasoning and assumptions","Share your findings with a parent or sibling"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 9.3 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/9/3.html"}],"questions":["A 5-by-3 rectangle is cut into the largest squares possible, repeatedly. How many squares?"],"answers":["4 (one 3x3, one 2x2, two 1x1)"],"notes":"Culminating or exploration lesson: no summary or practice set; let it run long if the project takes off."},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-9-4","title":"Lesson 4: How Do We Choose?","steps":["Activities: Which Was “Yessier”?; Which Class Voted Purpler?; Supermajorities; Best Restaurant","Work through the project, showing your reasoning and assumptions","Share your findings with a parent or sibling"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 9.4 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/9/4.html"}],"questions":["Class A voted 18 yes of 30. Class B voted 14 yes of 20. Which class was ''yessier''?"],"answers":["Class B (70% vs 60%)"],"notes":"Culminating or exploration lesson: no summary or practice set; let it run long if the project takes off."},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-9-5","title":"Lesson 5: More than Two Choices","steps":["Activities: Field Day; School Lunches (Part 1); School Lunch (Part 2); Just Vote Once; Weekend Choices","Work through the project, showing your reasoning and assumptions","Share your findings with a parent or sibling"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 9.5 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/9/5.html"}],"questions":["Votes: pizza 9, tacos 7, pasta 6. Does pizza have a majority?"],"answers":["No, 9 of 22 is less than half"],"notes":"Culminating or exploration lesson: no summary or practice set; let it run long if the project takes off."},{"id":"im-grade-6-math-9-6","title":"Lesson 6: Picking Representatives","steps":["Activities: Computers for Kids; School Mascot (Part 1); Advising the School Board; School Mascot (Part 2); Fair and Unfair Districts","Work through the project, showing your reasoning and assumptions","Share your findings with a parent or sibling"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson 9.6 student page","url":"https://access.openupresources.org/curricula/our6-8math-v1/6/students/9/6.html"}],"questions":["A council has 5 seats for 300 people. About how many people should each seat represent?"],"answers":["60"],"notes":"Culminating or exploration lesson: no summary or practice set; let it run long if the project takes off."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"cc_by","version":"4.0"},"attribution":"Adapted from “Open Up Resources 6–8 Math, Grade 6” by Illustrative Mathematics, published by Open Up Resources and licensed under CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Download for free at openupresources.org. Organized into lessons, with added practice questions, by Freerange Library; not endorsed by Open Up Resources or Illustrative Mathematics."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-intermediate-language-lessons', 'Freerange Library', 'publisher', true, 'Intermediate Language Lessons', array['writing', 'reading']::text[], 4, 6, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Emma Serl''s classic 1914 language book for grades 4–6, the sequel to Primary Language Lessons. Each day mixes poems and stories to study, picture study, dictation, letter writing, and short grammar lessons, building up to the parts of speech. The full book is free to read online.', array['209 lessons covering all 301 of the book''s lessons in 23 units', 'Poems to memorize, picture study, dictation, letter writing, and debate', 'Grammar from capitals and commas up to all eight parts of speech', 'Answers for grammar, punctuation, and fact questions', 'A link to the free scanned book for every lesson']::text[], 209, 4, 30, 'medium', false, '{}'::text[], '✍️', '#2F6B5E', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-intermediate-language-lessons', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, 'Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “Intermediate Language Lessons” by Emma Serl (1914), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/intermediatelan00serlgoog', '[{"title":"Part One: Lessons 1–12","lessons":["Lesson 1: The Finding of Moses","Lesson 2: Names of Persons and Places","Lesson 3: The Stone in the Road","Lessons 4–5: Selection to Be Memorized; Composition — A Fairy Story","Lesson 6: The Wise Fairy","Lessons 7–8: Oral Composition; The Sentence","Lessons 9–10: Picture Study — The Flower Girl; Composition","Lesson 11: Use of the Dictionary","Lesson 12: Correct Use of Words"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 13–24","lessons":["Lesson 13: The Robin","Lessons 14–15: Correct Use of Words; Conversation — Birds","Lesson 16: Composition — Description of a Bird","Lesson 17: Correct Use of Words","Lesson 18: The Red-Headed Woodpecker","Lessons 19–20: An Imaginary Dialogue; Words of a Series","Lesson 21: Paragraphs — An Indian Legend","Lessons 22–23: Conversation — Indians; Composition — Indians","Lesson 24: Titles and Abbreviations"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 25–37","lessons":["Lesson 25: Helen Keller","Lesson 26: Letter Writing — Helen Keller to O. W. Holmes","Lesson 27: Letter Writing","Lessons 28–29: Letter Writing; Addressing an Envelope","Lessons 30–31: Selection to Be Memorized; Dictation — The Apple Tree","Lessons 32–33: Conversation — Fruits; Description — A Fruit Store","Lesson 34: Correct Use of Words — Sit and Set","Lesson 35: The Barefoot Boy","Lessons 36–37: Conversation — The Farmer; Possessive Form"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 38–50","lessons":["Lessons 38–39: Composition; Names","Lesson 40: Story of the Flax","Lessons 41–42: Composition — Description; Composition — The Thirsty Crow","Lesson 43: Composition — Dialogue","Lesson 44: Conversation — The Cat Family","Lessons 45–46: Composition — Description of an Animal; Dictation — Description of a Lion","Lesson 47: Reproduction — A Fable","Lessons 48–49: Composition; Reproduction — A Fable","Lesson 50: Imaginative Letter"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 51–62","lessons":["Lesson 51: Summary","Lessons 52–53: Dictation — Quotation Marks; Meaning of Words","Lesson 54: Don''t Give Up","Lesson 55: Contractions","Lessons 56–57: Quotations; Dictation","Lesson 58: Correct Use of Words","Lessons 59–60: Description of a Game; Letter Writing","Lesson 61: Picture Study — Gambols of Children","Lesson 62: Correct Use of Words — Present, Past, and Future"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 63–76","lessons":["Lessons 63–64: The Comma in Address; Oral Composition — A Story","Lesson 65: The Village Blacksmith","Lessons 66–67: Henry Wadsworth Longfellow; Singular and Plural Forms","Lesson 68: The Wind and the Moon","Lessons 69–70: Conversation — Clothing; Conversation — Cotton","Lessons 71–72: Composition — Cotton; Letter Writing","Lesson 73: Words That Rhyme","Lessons 74–75: Writing Sentences; Written Conversation","Lesson 76: Letter Writing"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 77–89","lessons":["Lesson 77: Possessive Form — Plurals","Lessons 78–79: Conversation — The Dog Family; Picture Study — The Wounded Companion","Lesson 80: Composition — Story about Wool","Lessons 81–82: Correct Use of Words — Can and May; Composition — Frisk and the Mirror","Lessons 83–84: Picture Study — Shepherd and Sheep; Correct Use of Words","Lesson 85: Singular and Plural — Irregular Plurals","Lesson 86: King Solomon and the Bees","Lesson 87: Composition — A Story","Lessons 88–89: Daybreak; Dictation"]},{"title":"Part One: Lessons 90–100","lessons":["Lesson 90: Exclamations","Lesson 91: An Autobiography","Lesson 92: Composition — An Original Fairy Story","Lessons 93–94: Composition — Two Squirrels; Conversation — Gnawers or Rodents","Lesson 95: Correct Use of Words — Comparing","Lessons 96–97: Conversation — Money; Composition — History of a Dime","Lesson 98: Picture Study — Return of the Fishing Boats","Lesson 99: Sweet and Low","Lesson 100: Summary"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 101–113","lessons":["Lesson 101: The Story of a Seed","Lessons 102–103: Dictation; Divided Quotations","Lesson 104: Divided Quotations — The Fox and the Crow","Lessons 105–106: Indirect Quotations; Composition — A Story","Lesson 107: Conversation — The Body","Lesson 108: Debate","Lesson 109: Letter Writing — Review","Lesson 110: Homonyms","Lesson 111: Picture Study — Departure of the Pilgrims","Lessons 112–113: Conversation — Foods; Composition — A Loaf of Bread"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 114–124","lessons":["Lessons 114–115: Thanksgiving; Singular and Plural","Lesson 116: Conversation — Cud Chewers","Lesson 117: Picture Study — The Deer Family","Lesson 118: Correct Use of Words","Lesson 119: The Windflower","Lesson 120: Prose Study — A Plant","Lessons 121–122: Conversation — Flowers; Composition — The Monarch Butterfly","Lesson 123: Dictation — The Green of England","Lesson 124: October''s Bright Blue Weather"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 125–138","lessons":["Lesson 125: Correct Use of Words — Don''t and Doesn''t","Lessons 126–127: Picture Study — The Balloon; Composition","Lessons 128–129: Conversation — Ships of the Air; Composition — A Trip in an Airship","Lesson 130: Abbreviations — The States","Lesson 131: Reproduction — A Fable","Lesson 132: Composition — A Fairy Story","Lessons 133–134: Echo; Explanatory Expressions","Lessons 135–136: Selection to Be Memorized; Singular and Plural","Lessons 137–138: Picture Study — A River Scene; Dictation"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 139–151","lessons":["Lesson 139: The Song of the Brook","Lesson 140: Composition — The Story a Drop of Water Told","Lessons 141–142: Business Letters; Letter Writing","Lessons 143–144: Autobiography of a Book; Correct Use of Words","Lesson 145: A Diary","Lesson 146: Longfellow''s Diary","Lesson 147: The Cloud","Lessons 148–149: Dictation — A Dry Season; Composition","Lessons 150–151: Summary; Correct Use of Words"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 152–167","lessons":["Lessons 152–153: Picture Study — Two Mothers; Composition — An Important Event","Lesson 154: The Tree","Lesson 155: Composition — Story of an Oak Desk","Lessons 156–157: Composition — A Story; A Dialogue","Lessons 158–159: Letter Writing; Formation of Sentences","Lessons 160–161: Composition — A Story; Letter Writing","Lessons 162–163: Picture Study — Summer Evening; Study of Words","Lessons 164–165: Woodman, Spare that Tree; Composition — A Plea for Life","Lessons 166–167: Review — Singular and Plural; Kinds of Sentences"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 168–179","lessons":["Lesson 168: Letter Writing","Lesson 169: Legend of the Arbutus","Lesson 170: Conversation — The Post Office","Lessons 171–172: Description — A Postage Stamp; Titles","Lessons 173–174: Letter Writing; Selection to Be Memorized","Lesson 175: Formation of Sentences — Combining","Lessons 176–177: Picture Study — Return of the Mayflower; Letter Writing","Lesson 178: Old Ironsides","Lesson 179: Study of Words — Synonyms"]},{"title":"Part Two: Lessons 180–195","lessons":["Lessons 180–181: Description of an Old Mill; Composition — A Description","Lessons 182–183: Description of a Person; Selection to Be Memorized","Lessons 184–185: Correct Use of Words; Letter Writing","Lessons 186–187: Composition — A Story; An Imaginary Diary","Lesson 188: Picture Study — The Spinner","Lesson 189: The Heritage","Lessons 190–191: Selection to Be Memorized; Composition","Lessons 192–193: Quotations; To-Day","Lessons 194–195: Composition; Summary"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 196–208","lessons":["Lesson 196: A Chinese Folk Story — How the Moon Became Beautiful","Lesson 197: Subject and Predicate","Lessons 198–199: Selection for Study — October; Review","Lessons 200–201: Selection to Be Memorized; Letter Writing","Lesson 202: Picture Study — The Gleaners","Lesson 203: Compound Subject and Predicate","Lesson 204: July","Lesson 205: Transposed Order","Lessons 206–207: Conversation — The School; Debate","Lesson 208: The Invention of Printing"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 209–221","lessons":["Lesson 209: Composition — News Items","Lessons 210–211: Dictation; Nouns","Lesson 212: The Music of Labor","Lessons 213–214: Michael Angelo; Pronouns","Lesson 215: Composition — Description of a Place","Lesson 216: Common and Proper Nouns","Lesson 217: \"Down to Sleep\"","Lessons 218–219: Composition — A Lost Article; Possessives","Lessons 220–221: Conversation — Coal; Composition"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 222–235","lessons":["Lesson 222: Conversation — Electricity","Lesson 223: Picture Study and Letter — The First Railway Train","Lesson 224: Conversation — Transportation","Lessons 225–226: Debate; Dictation — Transportation","Lessons 227–228: Conversation — Good Roads; Railroads","Lesson 229: Picture Study — The Breaking Wave","Lessons 230–231: Advertisement and Answer; Writing Advertisements","Lessons 232–233: The Blue Jay; Conversation — Birds'' Nests","Lessons 234–235: Dictation — The Oriole''s Nest; Biography of an Oriole"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 236–249","lessons":["Lesson 236: Winter","Lessons 237–238: Conversation — Proverbs; Dictation — The Bells","Lessons 239–240: Composition; Choice of Adjectives","Lesson 241: Selection for Study — Under the Stone","Lessons 242–243: Composition; Debate","Lesson 244: Picture Study — The Horse Fair","Lessons 245–246: The Horse''s Prayer; Composition","Lessons 247–248: Adjectives and Nouns; Summary","Lesson 249: Landing of the Pilgrim Fathers"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 250–262","lessons":["Lessons 250–251: Selection for Study; Verbs","Lessons 252–253: Verbs — Continued; Letter Writing","Lesson 254: Conversation — The Court","Lesson 255: The Burial of Sir John Moore","Lesson 256: Correct Use of Words — Shall and Will","Lesson 257: Composition — A Description","Lesson 258: The Seasons","Lessons 259–260: Review; Sentences like Model","Lessons 261–262: Quotations; The Year''s at the Spring"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 263–275","lessons":["Lesson 263: Adverbs","Lessons 264–265: Adverbs — Continued","Lesson 266: Composition — A Business Circular","Lessons 267–268: A Biography — Samuel Morse; Telegrams","Lessons 269–270: Prepositions; Composition — A Biography","Lesson 271: Correct Use of Words — Who and Whom","Lesson 272: The Coming of Spring","Lesson 273: Selection for Study — A Bird''s Real Home","Lessons 274–275: The Flag Goes By; Dictation"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 276–290","lessons":["Lesson 276: Conversation — The Soldier","Lessons 277–278: A Man Without a Country; Conjunctions","Lessons 279–280: Composition — The Flag; Conversation — The State","Lesson 281: Interjections","Lessons 282–283: Columbus; Picture Study — Columbus","Lesson 284: Selection for Study — Close of Day","Lessons 285–286: Words Derived from Proper Nouns; Invitations","Lessons 287–288: Letter Writing; May","Lessons 289–290: Conversation — The Nation; Quotations"]},{"title":"Part Three: Lessons 291–301","lessons":["Lesson 291: Daffodils","Lesson 292: Summer Rain","Lessons 293–294: Description — Plan of a House; Home, Sweet Home","Lesson 295: Letter Writing — A Reference","Lesson 296: Conversation — Proverbs","Lesson 297: The Day Is Done","Lesson 298: Selections for Study — Ruskin and Van Dyke","Lesson 299: Suggestions for Descriptions","Lessons 300–301: Selection for Study — Neighbor Mine; Summary"]}]'::jsonb)
