"Narrate: tell three things about Baby Kate"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00360"}],"questions":["What color are Kate''s eyes?","Why can''t you see her eyes?"],"answers":["Blue","They are shut"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-8-5","title":"Lesson XL: Review: Henry and Ned","steps":["Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Her name is Kate.”","Compare Henry and Ned: who keeps the school rule?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00367"}],"questions":["Who is first at school?","What did Ned try to do with a stone?"],"answers":["Henry","Kill a quail"],"notes":"Review lesson: no new words. Let your child read it as independently as possible and note any words to revisit. This page has a script (cursive) exercise to read and copy."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-u9","title":"Unit 9: Lessons XLI–XLV","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-primer-9-1","title":"Lesson XLI: The Lighthouse","steps":["New words: light, far, its, high, where, sea, tall, were; sound focus: igh","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “The light is seen far out at sea.”","Answer the lesson''s question: would you like to live in a lighthouse?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00373"}],"questions":["Where does the lighthouse stand?","How does its light help the ships?"],"answers":["On a high rock by the sea","Sailors can tell where to go and not run on the rocks"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-9-2","title":"Lesson XLII: The Sheep and Lambs","steps":["New words: wrong, wolf, us, my, took, sheep, more, watch, lambs; sound focus: wr; silent b (lambs)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Let us watch the sheep.”","Narrate: what happened to the other two lambs?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00379"}],"questions":["What do the sheep like to eat?","What took two of the lambs?"],"answers":["The new grass","An old wolf"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-9-3","title":"Lesson XLIII: The Snow Man","steps":["New words: laugh, snow, head, fun, mouth, made, pipe; sound focus: gh as f","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “The boys have made a big snow man.”","Draw the snow man with his hat and pipe"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00385"}],"questions":["What is on the snow man''s head?","What is in his mouth?"],"answers":["A tall hat","An old pipe"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-9-4","title":"Lesson XLIV: The Bee and Mary","steps":["New words: sweets, mean, please, bee, buzz, vine, could, said, once; sound focus: long e (bee, sweets); said and once are sight words","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Buzz! buzz!”","Act it out: your child buzzes, you play Mary"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00394"}],"questions":["What did the bee say to Mary?","What did the bee want?"],"answers":["Buzz! buzz!","Some sweets in the vine"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-9-5","title":"Lesson XLV: Review: By the Sea","steps":["Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “What fun it is to see lambs play!”","Narrate the whole review story in order"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00401"}],"questions":["Where did Nat and the speaker sit?","What did the old wolf do once?"],"answers":["On the high hill by the sea, near the lighthouse","Took a lamb and ran off with it"],"notes":"Review lesson: no new words. Let your child read it as independently as possible and note any words to revisit."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-u10","title":"Unit 10: Lessons XLVI–LII and Slate Exercises","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-1","title":"Lesson XLVI: Work While You Work","steps":["New words: while, might, time, things, done, right, your, halves; sound focus: long i (while, time, might)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Work while you work, play while you play.”","Memorize the first four lines together"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00407"}],"questions":["What should you do while you play?","Are things done by halves done right?"],"answers":["Play","No"],"notes":"The whole lesson is a script (cursive) poem; read it together and use it as copywork."},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-2","title":"Lesson XLVII: John and Watch","steps":["New words: went, fish, fell, safe, arms, sprang, was, thank, got; sound focus: sh; short e (went, fell)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Thank you, my brave old dog.”","Narrate the story: what went wrong, and who saved John?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00411"}],"questions":["What was John''s dog named?","What happened when John got up to go?"],"answers":["Watch","He fell off the log, and Watch sprang in to save him"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-3","title":"Lesson XLVIII: James Drives On","steps":["New words: James, asks, warm, town, then, drives, been, show; sound focus: ow (town, show)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “James has been to the mill.”","Talk about how James was kind to his horse and to the girl"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00418"}],"questions":["Where has James been?","What did the girl ask him?"],"answers":["To the mill","To show her the way to the town"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-4","title":"Lesson XLIX: My Dear Puss","steps":["New words: I''ll, she''ll, don''t, puss, pur, pat, fur, harm, deeds; sound focus: contractions (I''ll, she''ll, don''t)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “I love my dear puss.”","Read the poem twice and clap the rhythm"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00424"}],"questions":["What will puss do when she is patted?","If you don''t hurt her, what will she do?"],"answers":["Purr","Do you no harm"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-5","title":"Lesson L: Queen of the May","steps":["New words: now, wreaths, who, queen, woods, shall, crown; sound focus: wr; qu","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Who shall it be?”","Make a simple paper crown or flower wreath"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00429"}],"questions":["What day is it?","Who will be crowned Queen of the May?"],"answers":["The first of May","Kate"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-6","title":"Lesson LI: The Tall Tree","steps":["New words: God, small, from, world, moon, shine, nut, long, ago; sound focus: all; long i (shine)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “God made the world.”","Find an acorn or seed outside and talk about how big it could grow"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00436"}],"questions":["What did the tall tree grow from?","What was made to shine at night?"],"answers":["A small nut","The moon"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-7","title":"Lesson LII: The Lord Is Near","steps":["New words: Lord, smile, joys, tears, nigh, morn, griefs, woes, stars, say; sound focus: igh (nigh); oy (joys)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Think the Lord is near.”","Read the hymn slowly; explain nigh, morn, and woes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00444"}],"questions":["When does the poem say the stars watch you?","What does the Lord see and hear?"],"answers":["At set of sun","All you do and all you say"]},{"id":"mcguffey-primer-10-8","title":"Slate Exercises and Script Alphabet","steps":["Sound focus: spelling three-letter words letter by letter","Point to each letter of the slate words (nun, urn, sun, cow...) and blend them into the word","Slate work: copy “c a t  cat”","Trace the script alphabet and numbers 1 to 0 from the last page"],"links":[{"label":"Slate exercises","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00450"},{"label":"Script alphabet","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14642/pg14642-images.html#id00455"}],"questions":["What word do the letters s-u-n spell?","What word do the letters h-e-n spell?"],"answers":["sun","hen"],"notes":"This is the Primer''s closing slate and script page, not a story. Spread it over a few days if your child is still learning to write."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “McGuffey''s Eclectic Primer, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1881), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14642"}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-second-reader', 'Freerange Library', 'publisher', true, 'McGuffey''s Second Eclectic Reader', array['reading']::text[], 1, 2, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional', 'faith_based']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The classic Second Reader: 71 short stories, poems, and nature lessons about brave dogs, honest children, bees, owls, and snowstorms. Each day your child practices a few new words, reads the lesson aloud, copies a line, and narrates it back. The full text is free online.', array['72 lessons in 9 units: the introduction plus all 71 Second Reader lessons', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson', 'Key new words, a copywork line, and a narration activity each day', 'Simple comprehension questions with answers', 'Script (cursive) lessons and poems marked in the notes']::text[], 72, 4, 20, 'medium', false, '{}'::text[], '🐕', '#3D9970', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-mcguffey-second-reader', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, 'Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “McGuffey''s Second Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14668', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Introduction and Lessons I–VIII","lessons":["Getting Started: Sounds and Punctuation","Lesson I: Evening at Home","Lesson II: Bubbles","Lesson III: Willie''s Letter","Lesson IV: The Little Star","Lesson V: Two Dogs","Lesson VI: Afraid in the Dark","Lesson VII: Baby Bye","Lesson VIII: Puss and Her Kittens"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Lessons IX–XVI","lessons":["Lesson IX: Kitty and Mousie","Lesson X: At Work","Lesson XI: What a Bird Taught","Lesson XII: Susie Sunbeam","Lesson XIII: If I Were a Sunbeam","Lesson XIV: Henry, the Bootblack","Lesson XV: Don''t Wake the Baby","Lesson XVI: A Kind Brother"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Lessons XVII–XXIV","lessons":["Lesson XVII: My Good-for-Nothing","Lesson XVIII: The Kingbird","Lesson XIX: Evening Hymn","Lesson XX: The Quarrel","Lesson XXI: The Bee","Lesson XXII: The Song of the Bee","Lesson XXIII: The Torn Doll","Lesson XXIV: Sheep-Shearing"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXV–XXXII","lessons":["Lesson XXV: The Clouds","Lesson XXVI: Patty and the Squirrel","Lesson XXVII: The Sparrow","Lesson XXVIII: Sam and Harry","Lesson XXIX: The Little Rill","Lesson XXX: The Boat Upset","Lesson XXXI: Mary''s Letter","Lesson XXXII: The Tiger"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXIII–XL","lessons":["Lesson XXXIII: The Fireside","Lesson XXXIV: Birdie''s Morning Song","Lesson XXXV: Willie and Bounce","Lesson XXXVI: Willie and Bounce (Concluded)","Lesson XXXVII: The Kitchen Clock","Lesson XXXVIII: The New Scales","Lesson XXXIX: The Bear and the Children","Lesson XL: The Little Harebell"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–XLVIII","lessons":["Lesson XLI: The Fishhawk","Lesson XLII: What the Leaf Said","Lesson XLIII: The Wind and the Leaves","Lesson XLIV: Mamma''s Present","Lesson XLV: Mary''s Story","Lesson XLVI: Ralph Wick","Lesson XLVII: Coasting Down the Hill","Lesson XLVIII: The Fox and the Ducks"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Lessons XLIX–LVI","lessons":["Lesson XLIX: Pretty Is That Pretty Does","Lesson L: The Story-Teller","Lesson LI: The Story-Teller (Concluded)","Lesson LII: The Owl","Lesson LIII: The Owl (Concluded)","Lesson LIV: Grandfather''s Story","Lesson LV: God Is Great and Good","Lesson LVI: A Good Old Man"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Lessons LVII–LXIV","lessons":["Lesson LVII: The Greedy Girl","Lesson LVIII: A Place for Everything","Lesson LIX: My Mother","Lesson LX: The Broken Window","Lesson LXI: The Broken Window (Concluded)","Lesson LXII: Frank and the Hourglass","Lesson LXIII: March","Lesson LXIV: Jenny''s Call"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXV–LXXI","lessons":["Lesson LXV: Poor Davy","Lesson LXVI: Alice''s Supper","Lesson LXVII: A Snowstorm","Lesson LXVIII: Bessie","Lesson LXIX: Bessie (Concluded)","Lesson LXX: Cheerfulness","Lesson LXXI: Lullaby"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-second-reader', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u1","title":"Unit 1: Introduction and Lessons I–VIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-1","title":"Getting Started: Sounds and Punctuation","steps":["Read through the Articulation tables together, saying each sound clearly","Copywork: “Where is John going?”","Find a period, a question mark, and an exclamation mark in the Punctuation page and say what each one tells the reader"],"links":[{"label":"Articulation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#artic"},{"label":"Punctuation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#punct"}],"questions":["What mark goes at the end of a question?","What mark shows strong feeling?"],"answers":["A question mark (interrogation mark)","An exclamation mark"],"notes":"The book''s introductory pages. Use the sound tables for a few minutes of drill before lessons all year."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-2","title":"Lesson I: Evening at Home","steps":["New words: newspaper, stockings, branches, events, story","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “The fire burns brightly.”","Narrate: what is each person in the family doing?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonI"}],"questions":["What season is it?","What is the mother doing?"],"answers":["Winter","Mending Harry''s stockings"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-3","title":"Lesson II: Bubbles","steps":["New words: beautiful, rainbow, burst, sneeze, colors, soap","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “See how high it floats in the air!”","Blow bubbles and count the colors you see"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonII"}],"questions":["Where did Willie''s bubble land?","How many colors are in a bubble, according to Harry?"],"answers":["On the cat''s face, and it made her sneeze","As many as in the rainbow"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-4","title":"Lesson III: Willie''s Letter","steps":["New words: rubber, parlor, street, number, o''clock","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Now, please don''t forget to come.”","Write a short letter of your own to someone in the family"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonIII"}],"questions":["Who is Willie''s letter to?","What two things does Willie ask for?","What time will Willie go to bed?"],"answers":["Santa Claus","A gun that will shoot and a rubber ball","Eight o''clock"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-5","title":"Lesson IV: The Little Star","steps":["New words: above, world, diamond, twinkle, blazing, dew","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Twinkle, twinkle, little star”","Memorize the first verse; look for stars tonight"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonIV"}],"questions":["What does the star look like in the sky?","When does the star show its light?"],"answers":["A diamond","When the sun has set"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-6","title":"Lesson V: Two Dogs","steps":["New words: Newfoundland, terrier, noble, shaggy, friskily, cellar","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Sport is a good watchdog.”","Compare the two dogs: draw a line down a page and list facts about each"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonV"}],"questions":["What are the two dogs'' names?","Why is the terrier called Dodger?","What did Dodger spoil?"],"answers":["Sport and Dodger","He jumps about so friskily","The baby''s red stocking"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-7","title":"Lesson VI: Afraid in the Dark","steps":["New words: between, bureau, needle, afraid, shadow","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “The dark is only a big shadow.”","Make hand shadows on a wall with a lamp"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonVI"}],"questions":["What did mamma ask Willie to get?","How did mamma show him what the dark is?"],"answers":["Her needlebook","She made a shadow with her hand"],"notes":"Ends with a short slate exercise."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-8","title":"Lesson VII: Baby Bye","steps":["New words: spiders, tickling, crawls, speck, secret, believe","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Little fly, good day.”","Look closely at a fly or ant (or a picture) and count its legs"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonVII"}],"questions":["How many legs does the fly have?","What does the poem warn the fly about?"],"answers":["Six","Spiders are near by"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-1-9","title":"Lesson VIII: Puss and Her Kittens","steps":["New words: servant, attic, cellar, anxious, trouble, dozen","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Be ever ready to help one another.”","Narrate the story in order, using ''first, then, at last''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonVIII"}],"questions":["Where did Puss want her kittens?","Who finally carried the kittens up?"],"answers":["In the attic","A strange cat she brought"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u2","title":"Unit 2: Lessons IX–XVI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-1","title":"Lesson IX: Kitty and Mousie","steps":["New words: mousie, frolic, spied, pearl, teeth, slipped","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Long time ago.”","Clap along with the repeated line each time it comes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonIX"}],"questions":["What color was the kitty?","Did the kitty keep the mousie?"],"answers":["White as snow","No, she slipped away"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-2","title":"Lesson X: At Work","steps":["New words: precious, game, harm, washed, brushed","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “One thing at a time.”","Talk about how your child can finish work before play today"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonX"}],"questions":["What did the boy do after play?","What was the boy''s saying?"],"answers":["Washed up, then helped his mother, read, or wrote on his slate","One thing at a time"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-3","title":"Lesson XI: What a Bird Taught","steps":["New words: topmost, bough, spray, mate, rosy, answer","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Little bird so gray”","Read the questions, and let your child chirp the bird''s answer"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXI"}],"questions":["What color is the bird?","What was all the bird would say?"],"answers":["Gray","Twit-twit, twit-twit-twee"],"notes":"Poem by Alice Cary. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-4","title":"Lesson XII: Susie Sunbeam","steps":["New words: brightness, pleasant, ragged, playmates, unkind, gladness","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Susie was always pleasant in her play.”","Narrate: how did Susie help the poor little girl?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXII"}],"questions":["What was Susie''s real name?","Why was she called Sunbeam?","What did Susie give the poor girl?"],"answers":["Susan Brown","She had a sweet, smiling face and brought brightness","A nice dress and a pair of shoes"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-5","title":"Lesson XIII: If I Were a Sunbeam","steps":["New words: woodlands, lilies, drooping, hovels, upward, divine","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “I would shine and shine.”","Name one way your child can be a ''sunbeam'' to someone today"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXIII"}],"questions":["What would the sunbeam do for the lilies?"],"answers":["Shine on them until each raised its drooping head"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-6","title":"Lesson XIV: Henry, the Bootblack","steps":["New words: support, dollar, honest, blacking, gentlemen, earned","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Black your boots, sir, please?”","Narrate: how did Henry earn money to help his family?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXIV"}],"questions":["How did Henry get his first dollar?","What did he buy with it?"],"answers":["A man gave it to him for finding his lost pocketbook","A box, three brushes, and some blacking"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-7","title":"Lesson XV: Don''t Wake the Baby","steps":["New words: tread, whisper, softly, careful, cheerful","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “We must not talk, but whisper low.”","Read the poem in a whisper, as if a baby were sleeping"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXV"}],"questions":["Why must the children tread softly?","Who is coming home to tea?"],"answers":["The baby is asleep","Father"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-2-8","title":"Lesson XVI: A Kind Brother","steps":["New words: heavy, middle, handle, brother, deceived","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “I will not do what is wrong.”","Try carrying a bag on a broom handle with someone to feel the difference"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXVI"}],"questions":["What did the boys put under the basket''s handle?","Where did the older boy slide the basket?"],"answers":["A pole","Near his own end, so his side was heavier"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u3","title":"Unit 3: Lessons XVII–XXIV","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-1","title":"Lesson XVII: My Good-for-Nothing","steps":["New words: busy, mischief, ringlets, ponder, embrace","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Good to love you, mamma.”","Ask your child the poem''s question: what are you good for?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXVII"}],"questions":["What answer did the little boy give?"],"answers":["Good to love you, mamma"],"notes":"Poem by Emily Huntington Miller. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-2","title":"Lesson XVIII: The Kingbird","steps":["New words: berries, robin, eagle, hawk, foe, active","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Is not the little fellow a brave bird?”","Look up a picture of a kingbird and compare its size to a robin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXVIII"}],"questions":["How big is the kingbird?","Why is he called the kingbird?"],"answers":["Not bigger than a robin","He can drive off all other birds"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-3","title":"Lesson XIX: Evening Hymn","steps":["New words: nigh, shadows, darkness, angels","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Now the day is over”","Read or sing it together at bedtime"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXIX"}],"questions":["What will soon be asleep, according to the hymn?"],"answers":["Birds, beasts, and flowers"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-4","title":"Lesson XX: The Quarrel","steps":["New words: quarrel, agree, settle, kernel, equal, divided","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “This is the way in which quarrels are very apt to end.”","Act it out with a real nut"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXX"}],"questions":["Why did each boy think the nut was his?","Who got the kernel?"],"answers":["John saw it first; James picked it up first","The older boy"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-5","title":"Lesson XXI: The Bee","steps":["New words: creatures, drones, workers, queen, hive, cells, wax","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “The cells are not round, but have six sides.”","Draw a honeycomb of six-sided cells"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXI"}],"questions":["What are the three kinds of bees?","How many sides does each cell have?"],"answers":["Workers, drones, and queens","Six"],"notes":"Ends with a slate exercise."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-6","title":"Lesson XXII: The Song of the Bee","steps":["New words: blossoms, clover, fragrant, thistle, meadow, treasure","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Buzz! buzz! buzz!”","Buzz the refrain together each time it comes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXII"}],"questions":["What does the bee do on sunny days?","What does the bee do on cloudy days?"],"answers":["Gets his honey","Makes his wax"],"notes":"Poem by Marian Douglas. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-7","title":"Lesson XXIII: The Torn Doll","steps":["New words: heedless, careless, effects, annoy, blame, harshly","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Put your things away when you are through playing.”","Tidy one shelf or toy box together"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXIII"}],"questions":["Who tore Mary''s doll?","Why shouldn''t Mary blame the dog?"],"answers":["Dash, her dog","She left the doll where he could get it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-3-8","title":"Lesson XXIV: Sheep-Shearing","steps":["New words: thoroughly, shearer, dyed, spun, woven, wool","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “It is then dyed, spun, and woven into cloth.”","Look at a wool sweater or yarn and trace its steps back to the sheep"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXIV"}],"questions":["In what month are sheep sheared?","What is done to the wool after it is cut?"],"answers":["June","It is dyed, spun, and woven into cloth"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u4","title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXV–XXXII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-1","title":"Lesson XXV: The Clouds","steps":["New words: wander, bearers, sultry, grain, rays","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “We are bearers of the rain.”","Go outside and describe today''s clouds"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXV"}],"questions":["What do the clouds carry?","How do clouds help on hot summer days?"],"answers":["Rain","They guard us from the sun''s rays"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-2","title":"Lesson XXVI: Patty and the Squirrel","steps":["New words: forest, squirrel, tame, hollow, snug, shoulder","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “She made them her friends.”","Narrate: how did Patty tame the squirrels?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXVI"}],"questions":["How did Patty make the squirrels her friends?","Where do squirrels live in winter?"],"answers":["She was quiet and gentle and left crumbs for them","In a hollow in an old tree"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-3","title":"Lesson XXVII: The Sparrow","steps":["New words: sparrow, frightened, shocking, robbing, ripest, breakfast","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Come to see me every day.”","Put out crumbs for the birds"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXVII"}],"questions":["What does Thomas say the sparrow steals?","What does the child give the sparrow?"],"answers":["His wheat","Crumbs"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-4","title":"Lesson XXVIII: Sam and Harry","steps":["New words: afternoon, carriage, hedge, truly, supper","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Which of the boys do you think was truly kind?”","Answer the question at the end of the lesson and tell why"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXVIII"}],"questions":["Why didn''t Sam give the blind man money?","What did Harry do with the cents?","Who found the money for the blind man?"],"answers":["He had spent it all on his book","Threw them into the hedge","Sam"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-5","title":"Lesson XXIX: The Little Rill","steps":["New words: rill, village, rippling, fringe, brink, course","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Run, run, thou tiny rill.”","Explain ''rill'' (a tiny stream); find one on a walk or a map"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXIX"}],"questions":["What does the rill turn?","Who will join the rill''s song?"],"answers":["The village mill","The birds"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-6","title":"Lesson XXX: The Boat Upset","steps":["New words: hastened, possible, balance, boatman, danger, several","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Sit still, children.”","Talk about safety rules for boats and water"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXX"}],"questions":["Why did Edgar fall into the water?","Who saved everyone?"],"answers":["He lost his balance trying to get Thomas''s stick","A man in another boat"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-7","title":"Lesson XXXI: Mary''s Letter","steps":["New words: rowing, drowned, tripped, cousin","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “A man in another boat picked us up.”","Compare the letter with Lesson XXX: what is the same story?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXI"}],"questions":["Who is Mary writing to?","What happened to the boat?"],"answers":["Her cousin Fanny","It upset, and they were all in the water"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It retells the story of Lesson XXX."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-4-8","title":"Lesson XXXII: The Tiger","steps":["New words: giant, stripes, prey, whiskers, tigress, collar","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “The tiger is a giant cat.”","Find a cat''s whiskers (or a picture) and talk about what they are for"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXII"}],"questions":["What do a tiger''s whiskers help it know?","Who took the tiger kitten from the tent?"],"answers":["When something is in its way","A large tigress, its mother"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u5","title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXIII–XL","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-1","title":"Lesson XXXIII: The Fireside","steps":["New words: fireside, sewing, knitting, crackle, usual, instructive","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “We feel happy because we have been busy.”","Do a small useful task together and talk about how it feels"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXIII"}],"questions":["What were the girls doing by the fire?","Why did they feel happier tonight?"],"answers":["Sewing","They had been doing something useful"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-2","title":"Lesson XXXIV: Birdie''s Morning Song","steps":["New words: dewdrops, laziest, slumber, patience, instead","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Wake up, little darling, wake up!”","Say the refrain together each time it comes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXIV"}],"questions":["What finally wakes the child?"],"answers":["Mother''s kisses"],"notes":"Poem by George Cooper. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-3","title":"Lesson XXXV: Willie and Bounce","steps":["New words: strolled, ripples, circle, floating, however","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Two fast friends were Willie Brown and his little dog Bounce.”","Predict: how will Bounce ''tell'' Willie''s father?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXV"}],"questions":["Where did Willie go with Bounce?","Why did Willie fall in?"],"answers":["Down to the river","The log he stood on turned"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-4","title":"Lesson XXXVI: Willie and Bounce (Concluded)","steps":["New words: yelp, distress, scratched, barking, clothes","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Bounce started for the river.”","Narrate the whole two-part story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXVI"}],"questions":["Where did Bounce run for help?","Who saved Willie?"],"answers":["To Mr. Brown''s store","His father, Mr. Brown"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-5","title":"Lesson XXXVII: The Kitchen Clock","steps":["New words: talkative, patient, truthful, obliging, improve","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Life is short, improve it well.”","Practice reading the time on a clock"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXVII"}],"questions":["What does the clock always say?","What does it do when the hour hand reaches two?"],"answers":["Tick-tock","Strikes: ding-ding"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-6","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: The New Scales","steps":["New words: orange, scales, beam, groove, gimlet, ribbon","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “First, we must take the peel off.”","Make a simple balance with a hanger, string, and two cups"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXVIII"}],"questions":["What did Herbert use for the scale cups?","What did Lucy give him for making them?"],"answers":["Orange peel","All her orange"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-7","title":"Lesson XXXIX: The Bear and the Children","steps":["New words: inn, attic, shaggy, eldest, youngest, soldiers","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “See, we are playing soldiers!”","Narrate the story as if you were one of the children"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXXXIX"}],"questions":["What did the children think the bear was?","What did the bear do when the drum beat?"],"answers":["A great dog","Rose on his hind legs and danced"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-5-8","title":"Lesson XL: The Little Harebell","steps":["New words: harebell, lady, clinging, despair, nodding, blooming","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “God is everywhere.”","Find a picture of a harebell (bluebell)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXL"}],"questions":["Where is the harebell growing?","Why isn''t the harebell lonely?"],"answers":["On a dreary rock, in the shadow","God is everywhere"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It is a short poem."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u6","title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–XLVIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-1","title":"Lesson XLI: The Fishhawk","steps":["New words: osprey, beneath, fiercely, seaside, timidly, robber","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “The eagle is a robber.”","Look up a picture of an osprey"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLI"}],"questions":["What is another name for the fishhawk?","What does the eagle do to the fishhawk?"],"answers":["Osprey","Makes him drop his fish and takes it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-2","title":"Lesson XLII: What the Leaf Said","steps":["New words: sighing, twig, autumn, October, rustled, trembling","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Hold on tight.”","Collect fall leaves and sort them by color"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLII"}],"questions":["What was the leaf afraid of?","Why do the branches keep plain colors?"],"answers":["The wind would pull it off","Their work is not yet done"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-3","title":"Lesson XLIII: The Wind and the Leaves","steps":["New words: meadows, cricket, farewell, fleecy, whirling, content","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Come, little leaves.”","Dance like the leaves while you read it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLIII"}],"questions":["Who called the leaves to come and play?","What covered the leaves at the end?"],"answers":["The wind","The snow"],"notes":"Poem by George Cooper. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-4","title":"Lesson XLIV: Mamma''s Present","steps":["New words: Christmas, presents, leggings, jolly, expect","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Just the very thing I wanted most.”","Plan a homemade present for someone"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLIV"}],"questions":["What were the children getting in the woods?","What was mamma''s present?"],"answers":["Green branches for Christmas","Jessie in a basket of green branches"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-5","title":"Lesson XLV: Mary''s Story","steps":["New words: yesterday, seaweeds, shallow, waded, purple, plumes","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “We went to the beach yesterday.”","Tell a ''yesterday'' story of your own, like Mary"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLV"}],"questions":["What did Mary and Lucy gather?","What funny thing happened to Lucy?"],"answers":["Sea mosses (seaweeds)","She slipped and fell into the water"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-6","title":"Lesson XLVI: Ralph Wick","steps":["New words: forget, thorns, snatched, scream, pluck, sore","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “He at last learned to do as he was told.”","Narrate: what did Ralph learn and how?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLVI"}],"questions":["How old was Ralph?","What happened when he snatched the white rose?"],"answers":["Seven","The thorns tore his hand"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-7","title":"Lesson XLVII: Coasting Down the Hill","steps":["New words: coasting, frosty, landscape, laughter, beaming, trudging","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Coasting down the hill!”","Explain ''coasting'' (sledding); talk about sledding or another snow game"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLVII"}],"questions":["What is the weather like?","What do the children do after rushing down the slope?"],"answers":["Frosty, but the sun is bright","Trudge back up through the snow"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It is a poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-6-8","title":"Lesson XLVIII: The Fox and the Ducks","steps":["New words: drifting, circling, heed, slyly, cunning, patient","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “The sly old fox”","Narrate the fox''s trick step by step"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLVIII"}],"questions":["What did the fox send down the river first?","How did the fox catch the ducks?"],"answers":["Branches","He hid on a large branch and drifted among them"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u7","title":"Unit 7: Lessons XLIX–LVI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-1","title":"Lesson XLIX: Pretty Is That Pretty Does","steps":["New words: spinner, silken, proverb, poverty, splendor, steady","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Pretty is which pretty does.”","Talk about what the proverb means"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonXLIX"}],"questions":["What is the spider planning while she spins?"],"answers":["How to catch her dinner"],"notes":"Poem by Alice Cary. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-2","title":"Lesson L: The Story-Teller","steps":["New words: civil, appear, travelers, persons, alone","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Tell us a story we have never heard.”","Predict how the dogs will help someone"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonL"}],"questions":["What did Ned ask the story to be about?","What are the big dogs taught to do?"],"answers":["Boys and dogs","Hunt for people lost in the snow"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-3","title":"Lesson LI: The Story-Teller (Concluded)","steps":["New words: bleak, shrill, lain, stiff, rode","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “He felt something pull him by the coat.”","Narrate the rescue from the boy''s point of view"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLI"}],"questions":["How did the boy get to the men''s house?","When did they send him home?"],"answers":["He rode on the dog''s back","When the snow was gone"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-4","title":"Lesson LII: The Owl","steps":["New words: dusk, squeak, ruffled, awake, creeping","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Owls can''t see very well in the daytime.”","Read it as a conversation in two voices"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLII"}],"questions":["Where did Harry and Fred find the owl?","How did they catch him?"],"answers":["In the old hollow oak","He was napping in the daytime, and Fred slipped him into a bag"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-5","title":"Lesson LIII: The Owl (Concluded)","steps":["New words: mouser, ducklings, capture, comical, swallows","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “An owl is a good mouser.”","Tell two facts you learned about owls"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLIII"}],"questions":["What will Harry do with the owl?","Why can the owl surprise its prey?"],"answers":["Let him go","It flies so softly you can hardly hear it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-6","title":"Lesson LIV: Grandfather''s Story","steps":["New words: fig, trout, underneath, surprised, fought","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “So much for fighting!”","Compare this story with Lesson XLI, The Fishhawk"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLIV"}],"questions":["What fell from the fig tree?","Why did it fall?"],"answers":["A fish (a trout)","A fishhawk and an eagle fought over it and dropped it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-7","title":"Lesson LV: God Is Great and Good","steps":["New words: twinkling, steep, vale, rills, broad","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “For great and good is He.”","List things God made named in the poem"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLV"}],"questions":["What does the poem say God made to shine at night?"],"answers":["The twinkling stars"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-7-8","title":"Lesson LVI: A Good Old Man","steps":["New words: cottage, hoe, droll, hymn, prayed, grave","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Many happy days they spent together.”","Narrate: how did the family take care of one another?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLVI"}],"questions":["Who lived with the old man?","Who knocked at the door?"],"answers":["His two grandchildren, Mary and Jane","Their father, home from sea"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u8","title":"Unit 8: Lessons LVII–LXIV","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-1","title":"Lesson LVII: The Greedy Girl","steps":["New words: greedy, glutton, acorns, doctor, tempers","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “It eats just enough.”","Talk about how the kitten, bee, and squirrel know when to stop"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLVII"}],"questions":["What is a glutton?","What does the bee do with the honey it doesn''t eat?"],"answers":["One who eats too much because the food tastes good","Carries it to its hive"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-2","title":"Lesson LVIII: A Place for Everything","steps":["New words: thimble, borrow, comfort, offended, ashamed","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “I have a place for everything.”","Read it as a play: one of you is Mary, one is Sarah"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLVIII"}],"questions":["What did Mary want to borrow?","What is Sarah''s secret?"],"answers":["A thimble","She has a place for everything and puts everything in its place"],"notes":"Written as a dialogue."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-3","title":"Lesson LIX: My Mother","steps":["New words: constant, lull, rejoice, fretful, meek","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “None can love so well as thou.”","Make a card for mother with one line from the poem"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLIX"}],"questions":["What does the child give mother in return?"],"answers":["A fond heart"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It is a poem with old words (thou, thy, didst); explain them as you read."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-4","title":"Lesson LX: The Broken Window","steps":["New words: gift, engaged, skipping, mischief","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “I will go and pay for it.”","Predict: what will happen when George goes back?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLX"}],"questions":["What was George''s New-year gift?","How did the window break?"],"answers":["A bright silver dollar","His snowball missed James Mason and hit it"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-5","title":"Lesson LXI: The Broken Window (Concluded)","steps":["New words: merchant, honestly, partner, without","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “He called him an honest boy.”","Narrate the whole story and say why honesty paid off"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXI"}],"questions":["What did George find in his cap?","What did George become in a few years?"],"answers":["Two silver dollars","The merchant''s partner"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-6","title":"Lesson LXII: Frank and the Hourglass","steps":["New words: hourglass, figure, impatient, advice, perfectly","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Study all the time.”","Time a task with a sand timer or clock"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXII"}],"questions":["What shape is an hourglass?","How long did it take Frank to learn the hymn?"],"answers":["The figure 8","One hour and a half"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-7","title":"Lesson LXIII: March","steps":["New words: sleet, cruel, cheerly, taps","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Little flowers begin their growing.”","Look for the first spring shoots outside"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXIII"}],"questions":["Who taps on the flowers to wake them?","Where is Winter going?"],"answers":["Spring","On his way; his time is past"],"notes":"Poem by Mary Mapes Dodge. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-8","title":"Lesson LXIV: Jenny''s Call","steps":["New words: grazing, harness, rogue, snorted, caressed, service","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “She gave love for love.”","Narrate: why would Fanny come to Jenny but not to the men?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXIV"}],"questions":["What was the horse''s name?","Why did the horse come to Jenny?"],"answers":["Fanny","Jenny was always kind and gentle; the men had been rough"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u9","title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXV–LXXI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-1","title":"Lesson LXV: Poor Davy","steps":["New words: recess, violet, ferns, maple, dainty, arrange","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Now the sunshine and the bird''s songs make him glad.”","Pick or draw a little bouquet like Davy''s"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXV"}],"questions":["Why was Davy sad?","How did his teacher help?"],"answers":["Children said cruel words about his ragged clothes","She helped him become a little flower merchant"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-2","title":"Lesson LXVI: Alice''s Supper","steps":["New words: reapers, cradles, miller, flour, dough, kneading","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “And it is for Alice''s supper!”","Trace bread from wheat to plate: wheat, flour, dough, bread"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXVI"}],"questions":["What do the reapers cut?","What is Alice''s supper?"],"answers":["Wheat","Bread and butter"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-3","title":"Lesson LXVII: A Snowstorm","steps":["New words: flakes, plumes, picket, captains, fairies","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “The whole white world sparkled.”","Draw the snowy yard with the fence ''soldiers'' and gatepost ''captains''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXVII"}],"questions":["What did the snow turn the picket fence into?","What did the old corn basket become?"],"answers":["A row of soldiers","A little snow house with a round roof"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-4","title":"Lesson LXVIII: Bessie","steps":["New words: apron, closets, workbasket, dandelions, heaped, toad","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Waiting, waiting, waiting.”","Predict: who is ringing the bell for Bessie?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXVIII"}],"questions":["What did Aunt Annie give Bessie?","What jumped into Bessie''s garden bed?"],"answers":["A large workbasket","A big brown toad"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-5","title":"Lesson LXIX: Bessie (Concluded)","steps":["New words: visit, soaked, ruined, obedient","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “You did quite right.”","Narrate: why was coming home at once the right choice?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXIX"}],"questions":["Who wanted Bessie?","What happened to the basket?"],"answers":["Her mother, who was leaving for a long visit","It was left out in the rain"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-6","title":"Lesson LXX: Cheerfulness","steps":["New words: lightsome, cherished, lofty, maiden, introduce","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Her name is Cheerfulness.”","Guess the riddle before reading the last line"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXX"}],"questions":["What is the little maiden''s name?"],"answers":["Cheerfulness"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It is a riddle poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-7","title":"Lesson LXXI: Lullaby","steps":["New words: western, breathe, dying, babe, sails","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Sweet and low, sweet and low”","Read it softly as a lullaby; celebrate finishing the Second Reader"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXXI"}],"questions":["Who will come to the baby soon?"],"answers":["Father"],"notes":"Poem by Tennyson. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “McGuffey''s Second Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14668"}'::jsonb)
