 7?"],"answers":["40","24","56"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-4","title":"Lesson XLVII: Multiplication: Times 9","steps":["Use the finger trick or bean groups for 9s","Recite the 9s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVII (p. 50)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n56/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 50","questions":["How many are 9 times 4?","Melons cost 9 cents each. What do 6 cost?","How many are 9 times 9?"],"answers":["36","54 cents","81"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-5","title":"Lesson XLVIII: Multiplication: Times 10","steps":["Count dimes by 10s","Recite the 10s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVIII (p. 51)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n57/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 51","questions":["How many are 10 times 6?","Each bundle has 10 sticks. How many sticks in 8 bundles?","How many are 3 times 10?"],"answers":["60","80","30"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-6","title":"Lesson XLIX: Review of Multiplication","steps":["Build arrays with beans for any two numbers","Work the review, including three numbers multiplied together","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIX (p. 52)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n58/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 52","questions":["How many are 4 times 7?","How many are 2 times 3 times 4?","How many are 6 times 9?"],"answers":["28","24","54"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-7","title":"Lesson L: Review: Mixed Operations","steps":["Add, take away, and then make groups with counters","Solve each question step by step, saying each result","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson L (p. 54)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n60/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 54","questions":["How many are 3 and 5, less 2, multiplied by 4?","How many are 6 and 4, less 5, multiplied by 3?","How many are 9 less 7, multiplied by 8?"],"answers":["24","15","16"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-8","title":"Lesson LI: Promiscuous (Mixed) Questions","steps":["Play store with pennies and price tags","Read each mixed word problem and explain your steps","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LI (p. 55)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n61/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 55","questions":["Ben had 15 cents and bought 2 apples at 4 cents each. How much did he have left?","A girl bought 3 ribbons at 5 cents each. What did she pay?","Two boys had 9 marbles each; one lost 3. How many marbles do they have together now?"],"answers":["7 cents","15 cents","15"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u9","title":"Unit 9: Division, 2 In to 6 In (Lessons LII–LVII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-1","title":"Lesson LII: Division: Ducks and Geese","steps":["Share 12 crackers fairly among 3 plates","Look at the ducks-and-geese picture and answer the sharing questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LII (p. 56)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n62/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 56","questions":["Twelve ducks split into 3 equal flocks. How many in each flock?","How many times is 4 contained in 16?","Six geese have 12 feet. How many feet does each goose have?"],"answers":["4","4","2"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-2","title":"Lesson LIII: Division: 2 In","steps":["Split beans into groups of 2","Recite ''2 in 2, 1 time…'' and work the buying problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIII (p. 57)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n63/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 57","questions":["How many times is 2 contained in 14?","Apples are 2 cents each. How many can you buy for 10 cents?","How many times is 2 contained in 18?"],"answers":["7","5","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-3","title":"Lesson LIV: Division: 3 In","steps":["Make groups of 3 buttons from a pile of 21","Recite the 3s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIV (p. 58)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n64/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 58","questions":["How many times is 3 contained in 24?","Share 15 cherries among 3 children. How many each?","How many times is 3 contained in 9?"],"answers":["8","5","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-4","title":"Lesson LV: Division: 4 In","steps":["Put 4 beans in each cup until 28 are used","Recite the 4s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LV (p. 59)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n65/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 59","questions":["How many times is 4 contained in 32?","Lemons are 4 cents each. How many for 20 cents?","How many times is 4 contained in 36?"],"answers":["8","5","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-5","title":"Lesson LVI: Division: 5 In","steps":["Group pennies into nickels'' worth (5s)","Recite the 5s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVI (p. 60)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n66/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 60","questions":["How many times is 5 contained in 35?","Kites are 5 cents each. How many for 30 cents?","How many times is 5 contained in 45?"],"answers":["7","6","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-6","title":"Lesson LVII: Division: 6 In","steps":["Pack 6 eggs per carton from a pile","Recite the 6s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVII (p. 61)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n67/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 61","questions":["How many times is 6 contained in 42?","Share 24 nuts among 6 boys. How many each?","How many times is 6 contained in 54?"],"answers":["7","4","9"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u10","title":"Unit 10: Division, 7 In to 10 In and Review (Lessons LVIII–LXIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-1","title":"Lesson LVIII: Division: 7 In","steps":["Arrange 28 counters into rows of 7","Recite the 7s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVIII (p. 62)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n68/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 62","questions":["How many times is 7 contained in 56?","A man walked 21 miles in 7 hours. How far in 1 hour?","How many times is 7 contained in 49?"],"answers":["8","3 miles","7"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-2","title":"Lesson LIX: Division: 8 In","steps":["Make 8-legged paper spiders from 40 ''legs''","Recite the 8s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIX (p. 63)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n69/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 63","questions":["How many times is 8 contained in 48?","Eight boys share 64 marbles. How many each?","How many times is 8 contained in 72?"],"answers":["6","8","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-3","title":"Lesson LX: Division: 9 In","steps":["Make groups of 9 from 45 beans","Recite the 9s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LX (p. 64)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n70/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 64","questions":["How many times is 9 contained in 63?","Pears cost 9 cents each. How many for 36 cents?","How many times is 9 contained in 90?"],"answers":["7","4","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-4","title":"Lesson LXI: Division: 10 In","steps":["Change pennies into dimes (10 each)","Recite the 10s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXI (p. 65)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n71/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 65","questions":["How many times is 10 contained in 70?","How many dimes are in 50 cents?","How many times is 10 contained in 100?"],"answers":["7","5","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-5","title":"Lesson LXII: Review of Division","steps":["Share a pile of counters different ways","Work the review table and the ''and … divided by'' questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXII (p. 66)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n72/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 66","questions":["How many times is 6 contained in 30?","How many are 7 and 9, divided by 4?","How many are 10 and 8, divided by 3?"],"answers":["5","4","6"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-6","title":"Lesson LXIII: Finding the Cost of One","steps":["Price a few items in play money, then find the price of one","Work the ''if 4 pens cost…'' problems in two steps","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIII (p. 68)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n74/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 68","questions":["If 3 pencils cost 12 cents, what does 1 cost?","If 1 orange costs 5 cents, what do 6 cost?","If 5 tops cost 20 cents, what will 3 tops cost?"],"answers":["4 cents","30 cents","12 cents"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u11","title":"Unit 11: Signs and Larger Numbers (Lessons LXIV–LXXI)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-1","title":"Lesson LXIV: Signs: + − × ÷ =","steps":["Make cards for the five signs and build number sentences with counters","Read about the signs, then solve the + problems with numbers past 10","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIV (p. 69)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n75/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 69","questions":["11 + 6 = ?","What does the sign × mean?","12 + 7 = ?"],"answers":["17","Multiplied by","19"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-2","title":"Lesson LXV: Adding to Numbers 16–20","steps":["Use a ten-bundle plus loose straws to add","Work the rows of + problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXV (p. 70)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n76/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 70","questions":["17 + 5 = ?","8 + 18 = ?","19 + 9 = ?"],"answers":["22","26","28"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-3","title":"Lesson LXVI: Adding to Larger Numbers","steps":["Add to 2-digit numbers on a hundred chart","Work the rows, noticing the ones digits repeat","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVI (p. 71)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n77/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 71","questions":["34 + 5 = ?","46 + 7 = ?","68 + 9 = ?"],"answers":["39","53","77"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-4","title":"Lesson LXVII: Addition Word Problems","steps":["Act out each story with coins","Read each problem and answer in a full sentence","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVII (p. 72)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n78/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 72","questions":["I had 18 cents and was given 6 more. How many cents now?","A slate cost 15 cents and a book 12 cents. What did both cost?","Ann has 23 beads and buys 7. How many beads?"],"answers":["24 cents","27 cents","30"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-5","title":"Lesson LXVIII: Subtraction Word Problems","steps":["Act out losing and spending with real coins","Read each problem and answer in a full sentence","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVIII (p. 73)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n79/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 73","questions":["Tom had 17 marbles and lost 5. How many now?","A girl had 20 cents and spent 6. How many left?","There were 19 plums; 7 were given away. How many left?"],"answers":["12","14 cents","12"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-6","title":"Lesson LXIX: Using Several Signs","steps":["Line up sign cards and number cards and work left to right","Solve each + and − string, saying each step","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIX (p. 74)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n80/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 74","questions":["6 + 8 + 3 − 5 = ?","10 + 4 + 7 − 6 = ?","9 + 9 − 8 = ?"],"answers":["12","15","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-7","title":"Lesson LXX: Money Word Problems","steps":["Use real coins to spend and make change","Work each problem, saying what was spent and what is left","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXX (p. 75)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n81/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 75","questions":["Fred had 20 cents and spent 7. How many cents left?","Nell had 25 cents; she spent 5 for a top and 8 for a ball. How much left?","Mother gave me 10 cents and father enough more to make 18. How much did father give?"],"answers":["13 cents","12 cents","8 cents"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-8","title":"Lesson LXXI: Multiplying, Then Subtracting","steps":["Make an array, then take some counters away","Solve each × and − problem","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXI (p. 76)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n82/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 76","questions":["4 × 5 − 6 = ?","7 × 3 − 9 = ?","6 × 6 − 10 = ?"],"answers":["14","12","26"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u12","title":"Unit 12: Review Problems (Lessons LXXII–LXXVIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-1","title":"Lesson LXXII: How Many Times? (to 20)","steps":["Group counters to see how many 3s make 12","Answer ''Six are how many times 2?'' style questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXII (p. 77)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n83/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 77","questions":["Twelve are how many times 4?","Fifteen are how many times 5?","Eighteen are how many times 6?"],"answers":["3","3","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-2","title":"Lesson LXXIII: How Many Times? (larger numbers)","steps":["Use arrays of beans to check each answer","Answer the larger ''how many times'' questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIII (p. 78)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n84/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 78","questions":["Thirty-two are how many times 8?","Forty-two are how many times 7?","Forty-five are how many times 9?"],"answers":["4","6","5"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-3","title":"Lesson LXXIV: Promiscuous (Mixed) Questions","steps":["Play store with several items and prices","Work each mixed problem, telling which operation you used","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIV (p. 79)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n85/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 79","questions":["Kate bought 3 peaches at 2 cents and 1 orange at 5 cents. What did she pay?","Ed has 3 kites and Ted has 4 times as many. How many kites do both have?","A man had 30 dollars and spent 4 dollars a day for 5 days. How much was left?"],"answers":["11 cents","15","10 dollars"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-4","title":"Lesson LXXV: Review Problems","steps":["Trade marbles between two piles to act out the first problem","Read each problem and solve it in steps","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXV (p. 80)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n86/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 80","questions":["Ann and Bea each had 8 shells; Ann gave Bea 3. How many does each have now?","Jim owed me 15 cents and gave me 2 apples worth 4 cents each. How much does he still owe?","I bought 2 lemons at 3 cents and 2 pears at 4 cents. What did I spend?"],"answers":["Ann 5, Bea 11","7 cents","14 cents"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-5","title":"Lesson LXXVI: Trading Goods","steps":["Trade ''apples'' for ''oranges'' using price tags","Work each trading problem: find the cost first, then the number of things","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVI (p. 81)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n87/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 81","questions":["Max bought 4 pears at 3 cents each and paid with apples at 2 cents each. How many apples?","Ribbon is 5 cents a yard. What do 4 yards cost, and how many 10-cent coins pay for it?","How many 4-cent tops pay for 2 books at 6 cents each?"],"answers":["6","20 cents; 2 coins","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-6","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Paying in Other Goods","steps":["Use play money to compare prices","Solve the ''how many … will pay for …'' problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVII (p. 82)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n88/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 82","questions":["How many pencils at 2 cents each will pay for 3 erasers at 4 cents each?","How many kites at 5 cents each will pay for 2 balls at 10 cents each?","How many apples at 3 cents each will pay for 6 plums at 2 cents each?"],"answers":["6","4","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-7","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: Counting by 2s, 3s, 4s to 100","steps":["Skip-count aloud while bouncing a ball","Count up and back down by the steps the book gives","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVIII (p. 83)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n89/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 83","questions":["Count by 4s from 4 to 24.","Count back by 3s from 30 to 15.","Count by 2s from 86 to 100."],"answers":["4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24","30, 27, 24, 21, 18, 15","86, 88, 90, 92, 94, 96, 98, 100"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u13","title":"Unit 13: Tables of Money and Measures (Lessons LXXIX–LXXXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-1","title":"Lesson LXXIX: Tables: United States Money","steps":["Sort real pennies and dimes; trade 10 pennies for a dime","Learn the money table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIX (p. 84)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n90/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 84","questions":["How many cents in 4 dimes?","How many dimes in 70 cents?","How many dimes in 2 dollars?"],"answers":["40 cents","7 dimes","20 dimes"],"notes":"The book also lists the ''eagle'' (a $10 gold coin); just explain it as old money."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-2","title":"Lesson LXXX: Tables: English Money","steps":["Use buttons as ''pence'' and bundle 12 into a ''shilling''","Read the English money table and work a few problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXX (p. 85)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n91/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 85","questions":["How many farthings in 2 pence?","How many pence in 1 shilling?","How many shillings in 2 pounds?"],"answers":["8","12","40"],"notes":"Historical units; treat this lesson as a fun look at old British money."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-3","title":"Lesson LXXXI: Tables: Troy Weight","steps":["Weigh small things on a kitchen scale and talk about ounces","Read the Troy weight table and work a few problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXI (p. 86)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n92/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 86","questions":["How many ounces in 2 pounds Troy?","How many pennyweights in 3 ounces?","How many grains in 1 pennyweight?"],"answers":["24","60","24"],"notes":"Troy weight was used for gold and silver; keep it light."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-4","title":"Lesson LXXXII: Tables: Avoirdupois Weight","steps":["Weigh flour or apples on a scale in pounds and ounces","Learn the table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXII (p. 87)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n93/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 87","questions":["How many ounces in 3 pounds?","How many pounds in 2 hundred-weight?","How many pounds in 1 ton?"],"answers":["48","200","2000"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-5","title":"Lesson LXXXIII: Tables: Dry Measure","steps":["Measure dry beans with pint and quart containers","Learn the dry measure table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXIII (p. 88)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n94/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 88","questions":["How many pints in 4 quarts?","How many quarts in 2 pecks?","How many pecks in 3 bushels?"],"answers":["8","16","12"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-6","title":"Lesson LXXXIV: Tables: Liquid Measure","steps":["Pour water between cup, pint, quart and gallon containers","Learn the liquid measure table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXIV (p. 89)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n95/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 89","questions":["How many pints in 3 quarts?","How many quarts in 2 gallons?","How many gills in 2 pints?"],"answers":["6","8","8"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-7","title":"Lesson LXXXV: Tables: Long Measure","steps":["Measure the table with a ruler and a yardstick","Learn the long measure table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXV (p. 90)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n96/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 90","questions":["How many inches in 2 feet?","How many feet in 5 yards?","How many yards in 12 feet?"],"answers":["24","15","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-8","title":"Lesson LXXXVI: Tables: Square Measure","steps":["Cover a 3-by-3 square of floor tiles or paper squares","Learn the square measure table and work the area problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXVI (p. 91)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n97/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 91","questions":["How many square feet in 2 square yards?","A rug is 4 feet long and 3 feet wide. How many square feet?","How many square yards in 27 square feet?"],"answers":["18","12","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-9","title":"Lesson LXXXVII: Tables: Apothecaries'' Weight","steps":["Look at a medicine cup and talk about small measures","Read the apothecaries'' table and work a few problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXVII (p. 92)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n98/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 92","questions":["How many scruples in 2 drams?","How many drams in 1 ounce?","How many grains in 1 scruple?"],"answers":["6","8","20"],"notes":"An old druggist''s table; enjoy it as history."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-10","title":"Lesson LXXXVIII: Tables: Circles, Paper, Books, and Counting","steps":["Count a dozen eggs, then talk about a ''score'' and a ''gross''","Read the circular measure, paper, and miscellaneous tables","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXVIII (p. 93)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n99/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 93","questions":["How many things in 2 dozen?","How many things in a score?","How many sheets of paper in 2 quires?"],"answers":["24","20","48"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-11","title":"Lesson LXXXIX: Review of Tables","steps":["Play a quick quiz game with money and measuring tools","Work the review of all the tables","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXIX (p. 94)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n100/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 94","questions":["How many cents in 6 dimes?","How many inches in 3 feet?","How many quarts in 3 gallons?"],"answers":["60","36","12"]}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “Ray''s New Primary Arithmetic for Young Learners (Eclectic Educational Series)” by Joseph Ray (1877), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog"}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-greeks', 'Freerange Library', 'publisher', true, 'The Story of the Greeks', array['history']::text[], 3, 6, array['classical', 'charlotte_mason']::text[], 'online', 0, 'H. A. Guerber''s classic 1896 read-aloud tells the story of ancient Greece in short, lively chapters, from the old myths and the Trojan War through Marathon, Pericles, Socrates, and Alexander to the Roman conquest. It suits the whole family in grades 3–6: each day you read one chapter aloud, your child narrates it back, finds the place on a map, and adds it to a timeline. The full text is free online.', array['115 lessons in 15 units, one chapter per lesson', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Narration prompt, map work, and timeline entry for each lesson', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Parent notes flagging myth told as history, dated viewpoints, and mature content']::text[], 115, 4, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🏛️', '#E3A33B', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-story-of-the-greeks', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, 'Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “The Story of the Greeks” by H. A. Guerber (1896), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/23495', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Myths and Heroes (Chapters 1–12)","lessons":["Chapter 1: Early Inhabitants of Greece","Chapter 2: The Deluge of Ogyges","Chapter 3: The Founding of Many Important Cities","Chapter 4: Story of Deucalion","Chapter 5: Story of Dædalus and Icarus","Chapter 6: The Adventures of Jason","Chapter 7: Theseus Visits the Labyrinth","Chapter 8: The Terrible Prophecy","Chapter 9: The Sphinx''s Riddle","Chapter 10: Blindness and Death of Œdipus","Chapter 11: The Brothers'' Quarrel","Chapter 12: The Taking of Thebes"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Troy, Homer, and the End of the Heroic Age (Chapters 13–20)","lessons":["Chapter 13: The Childhood of Paris","Chapter 14: The Muster of the Troops","Chapter 15: The Sacrifice of Iphigenia","Chapter 16: The Wrath of Achilles","Chapter 17: Death of Hector and Achilles","Chapter 18: The Burning of Troy","Chapter 19: Heroic Death of Codrus","Chapter 20: The Blind Poet"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Sparta and the Laws of Lycurgus (Chapters 21–28)","lessons":["Chapter 21: The Rise of Sparta","Chapter 22: The Spartan Training","Chapter 23: The Brave Spartan Boy","Chapter 24: Public Tables in Sparta","Chapter 25: Laws of Lycurgus","Chapter 26: The Messenian War","Chapter 27: The Music of Tyrtæus","Chapter 28: Aristomenes'' Escape"]},{"title":"Unit 4: The Olympic Games and the Lawgivers of Athens (Chapters 29–35)","lessons":["Chapter 29: The Olympic Games","Chapter 30: Milo of Croton","Chapter 31: The Jealous Athlete","Chapter 32: The Girls'' Games","Chapter 33: The Bloody Laws of Draco","Chapter 34: The Laws of Solon","Chapter 35: The First Plays"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Tyrants of Athens and the Great King (Chapters 36–41)","lessons":["Chapter 36: The Tyrant Pisistratus","Chapter 37: The Tyrant''s Insult","Chapter 38: Death of the Conspirators","Chapter 39: Hippias Driven Out of Athens","Chapter 40: The Great King","Chapter 41: Hippias Visits Darius"]},{"title":"Unit 6: The Persian Wars (Chapters 42–53)","lessons":["Chapter 42: Destruction of the Persian Host","Chapter 43: The Advance of the Second Host","Chapter 44: The Battle of Marathon","Chapter 45: Miltiades'' Disgrace","Chapter 46: Aristides the Just","Chapter 47: Two Noble Spartan Youths","Chapter 48: The Great Army","Chapter 49: Preparations for Defense","Chapter 50: Leonidas at Thermopylæ","Chapter 51: Death of Leonidas","Chapter 52: The Burning of Athens","Chapter 53: The Battles of Salamis and Platæa"]},{"title":"Unit 7: The Golden Age of Athens (Chapters 54–59)","lessons":["Chapter 54: The Rebuilding of Athens","Chapter 55: Death of Pausanias","Chapter 56: Cimon Improves Athens","Chapter 57: The Earthquake","Chapter 58: The Age of Pericles","Chapter 59: The Teachings of Anaxagoras"]},{"title":"Unit 8: The Peloponnesian War (Chapters 60–68)","lessons":["Chapter 60: Beginning of the Peloponnesian War","Chapter 61: Death of Pericles","Chapter 62: The Philosopher Socrates","Chapter 63: Socrates'' Favorite Pupil","Chapter 64: Youth of Alcibiades","Chapter 65: Greek Colonies in Italy","Chapter 66: Alcibiades in Disgrace","Chapter 67: Death of Alcibiades","Chapter 68: The Overthrow of the Thirty Tyrants"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Socrates, Xenophon, and Agesilaus (Chapters 69–75)","lessons":["Chapter 69: Accusation of Socrates","Chapter 70: Death of Socrates","Chapter 71: The Defeat of Cyrus","Chapter 72: The Retreat of the Ten Thousand","Chapter 73: Agesilaus in Asia","Chapter 74: A Strange Interview","Chapter 75: The Peace of Antalcidas"]},{"title":"Unit 10: The Rise of Thebes (Chapters 76–80)","lessons":["Chapter 76: The Theban Friends","Chapter 77: Thebes Free Once More","Chapter 78: The Battle of Leuctra","Chapter 79: Death of Pelopidas","Chapter 80: The Battle of Mantinea"]},{"title":"Unit 11: The Tyrants of Syracuse (Chapters 81–86)","lessons":["Chapter 81: The Tyrant of Syracuse","Chapter 82: Story of Damon and Pythias","Chapter 83: The Sword of Damocles","Chapter 84: Dion and Dionysius","Chapter 85: Civil War in Syracuse","Chapter 86: Death of Dion"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Philip of Macedon and Young Alexander (Chapters 87–94)","lessons":["Chapter 87: Philip of Macedon","Chapter 88: Philip Begins His Conquests","Chapter 89: The Orator Demosthenes","Chapter 90: Philip Masters Greece","Chapter 91: Birth of Alexander","Chapter 92: The Steed Bucephalus","Chapter 93: Alexander as King","Chapter 94: Alexander and Diogenes"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Alexander the Great (Chapters 95–103)","lessons":["Chapter 95: Alexander''s Brilliant Beginning","Chapter 96: The Gordian Knot","Chapter 97: Alexander''s Royal Captives","Chapter 98: Alexander at Jerusalem","Chapter 99: The African Desert","Chapter 100: Death of Darius","Chapter 101: Defeat of Porus","Chapter 102: The Return to Babylon","Chapter 103: Death of Alexander the Great"]},{"title":"Unit 14: After Alexander (Chapters 104–109)","lessons":["Chapter 104: The Division of the Realm","Chapter 105: Death of Demosthenes","Chapter 106: The Last of the Athenians","Chapter 107: The Colossus of Rhodes","Chapter 108: The Battle of Ipsus","Chapter 109: Demetrius and the Athenians"]},{"title":"Unit 15: The Last Days of Free Greece (Chapters 110–115)","lessons":["Chapter 110: The Achæan League","Chapter 111: Division in Sparta","Chapter 112: Death of Agis","Chapter 113: The War of the Two Leagues","Chapter 114: The Last of the Greeks","Chapter 115: Greece a Roman Province"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-greeks', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u1","title":"Unit 1: Myths and Heroes (Chapters 1–12)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: Early Inhabitants of Greece","steps":["Read Chapter I, \"Early Inhabitants of Greece,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who lived in Greece long ago and who came by sea to teach them","Map work: Find Greece, the Mediterranean Sea, Egypt, and Phoenicia on a map; notice the islands like stepping-stones","Timeline: Add \"Pelasgians live in Greece\" at c. 2000 BC (the book''s estimate)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#I_EARLY_INHABITANTS_OF_GREECE"}],"questions":["How did the earliest Greeks, the Pelasgians, live?","Why did Egyptian and Phoenician sailors hop from island to island instead of crossing open sea?"],"answers":["In forests and caves, hunting with clubs and stone-tipped weapons and wearing animal skins","They had no compass, so they stayed in sight of land"],"notes":"The book dates events by Bible stories (\"when Isaac went to Egypt\"). Modern historians describe early Greece differently; treat the Egyptian-teacher story as an old tradition."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Deluge of Ogyges","steps":["Read Chapter II, \"The Deluge of Ogyges,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Inachus helped the Pelasgians and what happened in the great flood","Map work: Find Argos on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Deluge of Ogyges\" to the myths strip of your timeline (legend, no real date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#II_THE_DELUGE_OF_OGYGES"}],"questions":["Why did later Greeks call the huge old stone walls \"Cyclopean\"?","Which city did Inachus found?"],"answers":["They said one-eyed giants called Cyclopes must have built them","Argos"],"notes":"Inachus and Ogyges are legendary figures, though the giant \"Cyclopean\" walls are real and can still be seen."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: The Founding of Many Important Cities","steps":["Read Chapter III, \"The Founding of Many Important Cities,\" aloud together","Narration: name the founders in this chapter and what each one gave the Greeks","Map work: Find Athens, Thebes, Argos, Mycenae, and the Isthmus of Corinth on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Mycenae''s great walls and gold masks\" at c. 1600–1200 BC; put Cecrops and Cadmus on the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#III_THE_FOUNDING_OF_MANY_IMPORTANT_CITIES"}],"questions":["What very important gift did Cadmus bring to Greece?","Whose name was given to the city of Athens?","What surprising things were found in the old tombs at Mycenae?"],"answers":["The alphabet (writing)","The goddess Athene","Thin masks of pure gold, plus weapons, armor, and pottery"],"notes":"Cecrops, Cadmus, and Danaus are founders from legend. The Mycenae finds are real (dug up in 1876)."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: Story of Deucalion","steps":["Read Chapter IV, \"Story of Deucalion,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Deucalion and Pyrrha after the flood","Map work: Find Thessaly, Delphi, and the pass of Thermopylae on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Deucalion''s flood\" to the myths strip; add \"Amphictyonic Council meets at Delphi\" (a real league, its beginning is legendary)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#IV_STORY_OF_DEUCALION"}],"questions":["What did Deucalion decide \"your mother''s bones\" meant?","What does the name Thermopylae mean?"],"answers":["The stones of the Earth","The Hot Gateway (hot springs in a narrow pass)"],"notes":"The Deucalion story is a myth; the book says so."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: Story of Dædalus and Icarus","steps":["Read Chapter V, \"Story of Dædalus and Icarus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Daedalus and Icarus escaped from Crete","Map work: Find Crete and the Icarian Sea on a map","Timeline: Add \"Daedalus and Icarus\" and \"King Minos of Crete\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#V_STORY_OF_DAEDALUS_AND_ICARUS"}],"questions":["Which useful inventions does the book credit to Daedalus?","Why did Icarus fall into the sea?","Who were the Hellenes named after?"],"answers":["The saw, the potter''s wheel, and sails for ships","He flew too near the sun and the wax holding his wings melted","Hellen, son of Deucalion"],"notes":"The book suggests the wing story grew out of Daedalus inventing sails. That is the author''s guess; the Minoan civilization on Crete was real."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: The Adventures of Jason","steps":["Read Chapter VI, \"The Adventures of Jason,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who the heroes were and what Jason went looking for","Map work: Find the Peloponnesus, then trace the Argo''s route from Greece across the Black Sea to Colchis","Timeline: Add \"Voyage of the Argo\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#VI_THE_ADVENTURES_OF_JASON"}],"questions":["How many heroes sailed on the Argo?","What did the Golden Fleece really stand for, according to the book?","Where does the name Peloponnesus come from?"],"answers":["Fifty","The riches the Greeks brought home from trading in the East","Pelops, who ruled the peninsula"],"notes":"Jason''s voyage is legend; the book explains it as an early trading journey."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-7","title":"Chapter 7: Theseus Visits the Labyrinth","steps":["Read Chapter VII, \"Theseus Visits the Labyrinth,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Theseus beat the monster in the Labyrinth, and what happened to his father","Map work: Find Athens, Crete, the Aegean Sea, and the island of Scyros","Timeline: Add \"Theseus and the Labyrinth\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#VII_THESEUS_VISITS_THE_LABYRINTH"}],"questions":["How did Theseus find his way out of the Labyrinth?","Why did King Aegeus think his son was dead?","What festival did Theseus start for Athene?"],"answers":["By following a long thread the king''s daughter gave him","The ship came back with black sails instead of white","The Panathenaea"],"notes":"Theseus is a legendary hero. The Acropolis named here is real and still stands in Athens."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-8","title":"Chapter 8: The Terrible Prophecy","steps":["Read Chapter VIII, \"The Terrible Prophecy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why baby Oedipus was sent away and how he ended up in Corinth","Map work: Find Thebes, Delphi, and Corinth on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Oedipus\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#VIII_THE_TERRIBLE_PROPHECY"}],"questions":["Why did King Laius want to get rid of his baby son?","What does the name Oedipus mean?"],"answers":["An oracle said the son would kill his father and bring ruin to the city","Swollen-footed"],"notes":"The Oedipus myth spans three chapters and has dark themes (a man unknowingly marries his mother). Preview it and shorten or skip parts if needed for younger children."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-9","title":"Chapter 9: The Sphinx''s Riddle","steps":["Read Chapter IX, \"The Sphinx''s Riddle,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened at the crossroads and how Oedipus beat the Sphinx","Map work: Find Thebes in Boeotia and trace a road toward Delphi","Timeline: Add \"The Sphinx''s riddle\" to the myths strip next to Oedipus"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#IX_THE_SPHINXS_RIDDLE"}],"questions":["What was the Sphinx''s riddle, and what was the answer?","What did the Sphinx look like?"],"answers":["What walks on four feet in the morning, two at noon, and three at night? Man","A woman''s head, a lion''s body, and an eagle''s wings"],"notes":"Myth. The violence at the crossroads is told plainly; you may want to summarize it."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-10","title":"Chapter 10: Blindness and Death of Œdipus","steps":["Read Chapter X, \"Blindness and Death of Œdipus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the truth about Oedipus came out and what Antigone did","Map work: Find Thebes again and talk about how far a blind man could wander on foot"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#X_BLINDNESS_AND_DEATH_OF_OEDIPUS"}],"questions":["What did the oracle say would stop the plague in Thebes?","Which daughter stayed to guide her blind father?"],"answers":["Finding and punishing the man who killed King Laius","Antigone"],"notes":"Contains Jocasta''s suicide and Oedipus blinding himself. Read ahead and soften or summarize for younger listeners."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-11","title":"Chapter 11: The Brothers'' Quarrel","steps":["Read Chapter XI, \"The Brothers'' Quarrel,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Eteocles and Polynices fought and how their quarrel ended","Map work: Find Argos, Nemea, and Thebes and trace the march of the Seven","Timeline: Add \"Seven against Thebes\" to the myths strip; note the real Nemean Games"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XI_THE_BROTHERS_QUARREL"}],"questions":["What was the brothers'' agreement, and who broke it?","Which games did the seven chiefs start, and in whose honor?","How long did the first siege of Thebes last?"],"answers":["To rule a year each in turn; Eteocles broke it","The Nemean Games, honoring Hercules'' victory over the Nemean lion","Seven years"],"notes":"The book itself says this family story is a myth."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-12","title":"Chapter 12: The Taking of Thebes","steps":["Read Chapter XII, \"The Taking of Thebes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the sons of the Seven finally took Thebes","Map work: Find Thebes and Argos on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"War of the Epigoni\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XII_THE_TAKING_OF_THEBES"}],"questions":["What does \"Epigoni\" mean?","Who was put on the throne of Thebes after it was taken?"],"answers":["Descendants (the sons of the first warriors)","Thersander, son of Polynices"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u2","title":"Unit 2: Troy, Homer, and the End of the Heroic Age (Chapters 13–20)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-1","title":"Chapter 13: The Childhood of Paris","steps":["Read Chapter XIII, \"The Childhood of Paris,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Paris from his mother''s dream to his journey home with Helen","Map work: Find Troy in Asia Minor (modern Turkey), Sparta, and the port of Aulis","Timeline: Add \"Paris and Helen\" to the myths strip, beginning the Trojan War story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XIII_THE_CHILDHOOD_OF_PARIS"}],"questions":["What did Queen Hecuba dream about baby Paris?","How was Paris recognized when he came to Troy?","Where did Menelaus tell his allies to gather?"],"answers":["A flaming brand in his cradle that set the palace on fire","He won all the prizes at the games, looked like the royal family, and Cassandra named him","Aulis"],"notes":"The Trojan War comes from Homer''s poems. Troy was a real city, but the story with gods and heroes is legend, not proven history."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-2","title":"Chapter 14: The Muster of the Troops","steps":["Read Chapter XIV, \"The Muster of the Troops,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who the Greek chiefs were and how Ulysses found Achilles","Map work: Find Aulis and the island of Scyros","Timeline: Add \"Greek army gathers at Aulis\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XIV_THE_MUSTER_OF_THE_TROOPS"}],"questions":["Why was Achilles'' heel the only place he could be hurt?","How did Ulysses trick Achilles into showing who he was?","Who was chosen leader of the whole Greek army?"],"answers":["His mother held him by the heel when she dipped him in the river Styx","He put a fine sword among his trinkets, and only Achilles grabbed it","Agamemnon, King of Mycenae"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-3","title":"Chapter 15: The Sacrifice of Iphigenia","steps":["Read Chapter XV, \"The Sacrifice of Iphigenia,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why the Greek fleet could not sail and what happened once they reached Troy","Map work: Trace the route from Aulis across the Aegean Sea to Troy","Timeline: Add \"Siege of Troy begins\" to the myths strip (ancient Greeks placed it near 1200 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XV_THE_SACRIFICE_OF_IPHIGENIA"}],"questions":["Why was there no wind to carry the Greek ships?","How long had the war gone on before anyone was close to winning?"],"answers":["Agamemnon had offended the goddess Diana, and the oracle said she must be appeased","Nine years"],"notes":"The story of a father asked to sacrifice his daughter is upsetting; the book has the goddess rescue her. A good chance to talk about myths versus history."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-4","title":"Chapter 16: The Wrath of Achilles","steps":["Read Chapter XVI, \"The Wrath of Achilles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Achilles stopped fighting and what made him return","Map work: Find the plain between Troy and the sea where the armies camped","Timeline: Add \"The Wrath of Achilles (the Iliad)\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XVI_THE_WRATH_OF_ACHILLES"}],"questions":["Why did Achilles refuse to fight for Agamemnon?","Whose armor did Patroclus wear into battle, and who killed him?"],"answers":["Agamemnon unfairly took away the captive girl given to Achilles","Achilles'' armor; Hector killed him"],"notes":"The book mentions captives being made slaves. Gods sending a plague is part of the myth."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-5","title":"Chapter 17: Death of Hector and Achilles","steps":["Read Chapter XVII, \"Death of Hector and Achilles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the fight between Hector and Achilles and King Priam''s visit","Map work: Find Troy and imagine the walls the old king watched from","Timeline: Add \"Death of Hector and Achilles\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XVII_DEATH_OF_HECTOR_AND_ACHILLES"}],"questions":["Why did old King Priam secretly visit Achilles'' tent?","How did Achilles die?"],"answers":["To beg for the body of his son Hector so he could bury it","A poisoned arrow shot by Paris hit his heel"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-6","title":"Chapter 18: The Burning of Troy","steps":["Read Chapter XVIII, \"The Burning of Troy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the wooden horse tricked the Trojans","Map work: Find Troy, then Sicily and southern Italy (\"Great Greece\"), and the Tiber River where Aeneas landed","Timeline: Add \"Fall of Troy\" to the myths strip (Greek tradition said c. 1184 BC); add the real \"Troy excavated by Schliemann, 1870s\" to the modern end"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XVIII_THE_BURNING_OF_TROY"}],"questions":["Whose idea was the wooden horse?","Why did the Trojans tear down part of their own wall?","Which Trojan prince escaped to start a new kingdom in Italy?"],"answers":["Ulysses","The horse was too big to fit through the gates","Aeneas"],"notes":"Archaeologists did find a burned city at Troy, but the wooden horse and the heroes are legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-7","title":"Chapter 19: Heroic Death of Codrus","steps":["Read Chapter XIX, \"Heroic Death of Codrus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how King Codrus saved Athens","Map work: Find the Peloponnesus (the \"mulberry leaf\"), Sparta, Athens, and the Ionian cities of Ephesus and Miletus in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Dorians move into the Peloponnesus\" at c. 1100 BC and \"Death of Codrus\" at c. 1070 BC (traditional date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XIX_HEROIC_DEATH_OF_CODRUS"}],"questions":["What had the oracle promised about the coming battle?","Why did Codrus dress as an ordinary soldier?","What title did the Athenians give their rulers after Codrus instead of king?"],"answers":["The army whose king was killed would win","So the enemy would not recognize him and spare him","Archon"],"notes":"The book says real history begins here. Codrus is still partly legendary; written records came later."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-8","title":"Chapter 20: The Blind Poet","steps":["Read Chapter XX, \"The Blind Poet,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what we know about Homer and how his poems spread","Map work: Find the island of Chios and the coast of Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Homer''s Iliad and Odyssey\" at c. 750 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XX_THE_BLIND_POET"}],"questions":["What are Homer''s two great poems about?","How were Homer''s poems kept alive before they were written down?","Which island was most often named as Homer''s birthplace?"],"answers":["The Iliad tells of the Trojan War; the Odyssey of Ulysses'' ten-year voyage home","People memorized and recited them from place to place","Chios"],"notes":"The pirate-blinding story is a legend; historians are not sure Homer was one real person."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u3","title":"Unit 3: Sparta and the Laws of Lycurgus (Chapters 21–28)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-1","title":"Chapter 21: The Rise of Sparta","steps":["Read Chapter XXI, \"The Rise of Sparta,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Lycurgus protected the baby king","Map work: Find Sparta and Laconia on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Lycurgus gives Sparta its laws\" at c. 800 BC (traditional date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXI_THE_RISE_OF_SPARTA"}],"questions":["Who were the Helots?","Why did Sparta have two kings at once?","How did Lycurgus keep the baby king Charilaus safe?"],"answers":["Prisoners of war who became Spartan slaves","Aristodemus''s twin sons both became king, and each line continued","He pretended to agree to kill him, then took him to the council hall and had him named king"],"notes":"Many historians think Lycurgus may be partly or wholly legendary."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-2","title":"Chapter 22: The Spartan Training","steps":["Read Chapter XXII, \"The Spartan Training,\" aloud together","Narration: describe a Spartan boy''s life after age seven","Map work: Find the Eurotas River near Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Spartan training system\" alongside Lycurgus (c. 800–600 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXII_THE_SPARTAN_TRAINING"}],"questions":["At what age were Spartan children given over to the state?","What did Spartan boys sleep on?"],"answers":["Seven","A pile of rushes from the Eurotas River"],"notes":"The chapter describes leaving weak babies to die. Read ahead and decide how to handle it."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-3","title":"Chapter 23: The Brave Spartan Boy","steps":["Read Chapter XXIII, \"The Brave Spartan Boy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the boy and the fox, and what \"laconic\" means","Map work: Find Laconia, the land around Sparta"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXIII_THE_BRAVE_SPARTAN_BOY"}],"questions":["Why were Spartan boys trained to steal food?","What does \"laconic\" speech mean, and where does the word come from?"],"answers":["Spartan armies had no supply wagons and had to find their own food in war","Saying a lot in few words; from Laconia, the land of Sparta"],"notes":"The book praises harsh customs (flogging, the fox story). Ask your child whether these customs were good or wise."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-4","title":"Chapter 24: Public Tables in Sparta","steps":["Read Chapter XXIV, \"Public Tables in Sparta,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the Spartan common tables and what mothers told sons going to war","Map work: Find Sparta and Messenia next to it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXIV_PUBLIC_TABLES_IN_SPARTA"}],"questions":["What was the Spartan men''s favorite food?","What did a Spartan mother say when handing her son his shield, and what did she mean?"],"answers":["Black broth, a thick dark stew","\"Come back with it or on it\": come home with honor or die bravely"],"notes":"The book describes deliberately getting a slave drunk as a lesson, a cruel custom worth discussing."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-5","title":"Chapter 25: Laws of Lycurgus","steps":["Read Chapter XXV, \"Laws of Lycurgus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Lycurgus did about money and how he made the laws last","Map work: Find Sparta and talk about how hard it would be to cart heavy iron money there","Timeline: Add \"Sparta uses iron money\" next to Lycurgus"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXV_LAWS_OF_LYCURGUS"}],"questions":["Why did Lycurgus make the Spartans use iron money?","How did Lycurgus make sure the laws would be kept after he left?"],"answers":["It was so heavy that no one could hide it, steal it, or grow greedy for wealth","He made the citizens swear to obey them until he came back, then never returned"],"notes":"Story of the oath is a legend about a figure who may not have existed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-6","title":"Chapter 26: The Messenian War","steps":["Read Chapter XXVI, \"The Messenian War,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Sparta attacked Messenia and what happened at Ithome","Map work: Find Messenia and Mount Ithome west of Sparta","Timeline: Add \"First Messenian War\" at c. 743–724 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXVI_THE_MESSENIAN_WAR"}],"questions":["Why did the Spartans want Messenia''s land?","Why couldn''t the Spartans climb up to Ithome?"],"answers":["Its fields were more fertile than their own","It stood on a very high, steep rock and the defenders rolled stones down"],"notes":"Includes a king sacrificing his daughter and then taking his own life. Preview before reading aloud."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-7","title":"Chapter 27: The Music of Tyrtæus","steps":["Read Chapter XXVII, \"The Music of Tyrtæus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how a lame schoolmaster helped the Spartans win","Map work: Find Messenia, Sparta, and Athens","Timeline: Add \"Second Messenian War\" at c. 685–668 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXVII_THE_MUSIC_OF_TYRTAEUS"}],"questions":["What bold thing did Aristomenes do inside Sparta?","Whom did the Athenians send to lead the Spartans, and how did he help?"],"answers":["He hung a trophy of captured arms in the main temple and wrote his name under it","Tyrtaeus, a lame schoolmaster, who stirred them with war songs"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-8","title":"Chapter 28: Aristomenes'' Escape","steps":["Read Chapter XXVIII, \"Aristomenes'' Escape,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aristomenes got out of the pit","Map work: Find Messenia, then Sicily, the city of Messina, and the Strait of Messina","Timeline: Add \"Messenians found colonies in Italy and Sicily\" at c. 650 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXVIII_ARISTOMENES_ESCAPE"}],"questions":["What animal led Aristomenes out of the pit?","Which city in Sicily was named after the Messenians'' homeland?"],"answers":["A fox","Messina"],"notes":"The escape story is a legend told about a real war."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u4","title":"Unit 4: The Olympic Games and the Lawgivers of Athens (Chapters 29–35)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-1","title":"Chapter 29: The Olympic Games","steps":["Read Chapter XXIX, \"The Olympic Games,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the Olympic games: where, how often, and what the winners received","Map work: Find Elis and Olympia, northwest of Sparta","Timeline: Add \"First recorded Olympic games\" at 776 BC (the book says 774) and \"Modern Olympics revived in Athens\" at AD 1896"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXIX_THE_OLYMPIC_GAMES"}],"questions":["What prize did Olympic winners receive?","How did Greeks use the games to count time?","Where were the first modern Olympics held, and when?"],"answers":["A crown of wild olive leaves","They counted by Olympiads, the four-year spans between games","Athens, in 1896"],"notes":"Hercules founding the games is legend. The book was written in 1896, the year the modern games began, so it calls them brand new."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-2","title":"Chapter 30: Milo of Croton","steps":["Read Chapter XXX, \"Milo of Croton,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Milo became so strong and how he died","Map work: Find Croton in southern Italy (a Greek colony)","Timeline: Add \"Milo of Croton, Olympic wrestler\" at c. 540–510 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXX_MILO_OF_CROTON"}],"questions":["How did Milo train to grow stronger each day?","What trapped Milo in the forest?"],"answers":["He carried a calf every day as it grew into an ox","His hands got caught when a split tree trunk snapped shut"],"notes":"Milo was a real champion wrestler, but stories like eating a whole ox are exaggerated legends."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-3","title":"Chapter 31: The Jealous Athlete","steps":["Read Chapter XXXI, \"The Jealous Athlete,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened to the jealous man and to the statue","Map work: Find Olympia again on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Theagenes, champion athlete\" at c. 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXI_THE_JEALOUS_ATHLETE"}],"questions":["How did the jealous man die?","What punishment did the judges give the statue?"],"answers":["He shook the statue until it fell and crushed him","It was thrown into the sea"],"notes":"Good chance to discuss what the book calls superstition."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-4","title":"Chapter 32: The Girls'' Games","steps":["Read Chapter XXXII, \"The Girls'' Games,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the girls'' torch race and the chariot races","Map work: Find Olympia and sketch a stadium and a hippodrome"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXII_THE_GIRLS_GAMES"}],"questions":["How did a girl win the torch race?","Where were the horse and chariot races held?"],"answers":["By reaching the goal first with her torch still lit, or keeping it lit longest","In the hippodrome"],"notes":"Girls'' races at Olympia were held at a separate festival honoring the goddess Hera."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-5","title":"Chapter 33: The Bloody Laws of Draco","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIII, \"The Bloody Laws of Draco,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Draco''s laws and what happened to Cylon''s followers","Map work: Find Athens, the Acropolis, and the island of Aegina","Timeline: Add \"Cylon tries to seize Athens\" at c. 632 BC and \"Draco''s laws\" at c. 621 BC (the book says 602)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXIII_THE_BLOODY_LAWS_OF_DRACO"}],"questions":["Why do we still call very harsh rules \"draconian\"?","What punishment did Draco set for stealing a cabbage?","Why did Cylon''s men hold a cord tied to Athene''s statue?"],"answers":["Draco''s laws punished small wrongs as severely as great crimes","Death","They believed it kept them under the goddess''s protection"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-6","title":"Chapter 34: The Laws of Solon","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIV, \"The Laws of Solon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Solon changed in Athens","Map work: Find Athens and the Areopagus hill beside the Acropolis","Timeline: Add \"Solon''s laws\" at 594 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXIV_THE_LAWS_OF_SOLON"}],"questions":["Why were trials at the Areopagus held in the dark?","How long did Epimenides sleep in the cave?","How long did Solon ask the Athenians to keep his laws while he traveled?"],"answers":["So the jury would judge by the facts, not by how the accused looked","Fifty-eight years","Ten years"],"notes":"Epimenides'' long sleep is a legend (like Rip Van Winkle)."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-7","title":"Chapter 35: The First Plays","steps":["Read Chapter XXXV, \"The First Plays,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Greek plays began and what the Theater of Dionysus was like","Map work: Find Athens and the Theater of Dionysus below the Acropolis","Timeline: Add \"Thespis, first actor\" at c. 534 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXV_THE_FIRST_PLAYS"}],"questions":["What does \"tragedy\" mean in Greek?","Why did Solon dislike the plays?","Name one Greek writer of tragedies and one of comedies."],"answers":["Goat song","He said they taught people to love a lie","Tragedies: Aeschylus, Sophocles, or Euripides; comedies: Aristophanes"],"notes":"Actors are still called \"thespians\" after Thespis."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u5","title":"Unit 5: Tyrants of Athens and the Great King (Chapters 36–41)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-1","title":"Chapter 36: The Tyrant Pisistratus","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVI, \"The Tyrant Pisistratus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pisistratus tricked his way to power and what good he did","Map work: Find Athens and its Acropolis","Timeline: Add \"Pisistratus becomes tyrant\" at 561 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXVI_THE_TYRANT_PISISTRATUS"}],"questions":["What trick did Pisistratus use to get a bodyguard?","Which two English words come from the Greek \"aristoi\" and \"demos\"?","What did Pisistratus do with Homer''s poems?"],"answers":["He wounded himself and said nobles had attacked him","Aristocrat and democrat","Had them written down and put in a public library"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-2","title":"Chapter 37: The Tyrant''s Insult","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVII, \"The Tyrant''s Insult,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Harmodius and Aristogiton turned against the tyrants","Map work: Find Athens; Solon had been traveling in Asia Minor across the Aegean","Timeline: Add \"Death of Pisistratus; his sons rule\" at 527 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXVII_THE_TYRANTS_INSULT"}],"questions":["How did Solon feel when he came home and found Pisistratus ruling?","How did Hipparchus insult Harmodius?"],"answers":["Sorry at first, but he forgave him because he ruled wisely","He banned Harmodius''s sister from carrying flowers in the procession"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-3","title":"Chapter 38: Death of the Conspirators","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVIII, \"Death of the Conspirators,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened at the festival and how Leaena kept the secret","Map work: Find Athens on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Hipparchus killed\" at 514 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXVIII_DEATH_OF_THE_CONSPIRATORS"}],"questions":["Which tyrant did Harmodius kill?","What image was put on Leaena''s tomb, and why?"],"answers":["Hipparchus","A lioness without a tongue, because she kept silent under torture"],"notes":"Contains torture; you may want to summarize Leaena''s story."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-4","title":"Chapter 39: Hippias Driven Out of Athens","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIX, \"Hippias Driven Out of Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Hippias was driven out and how ostracism worked","Map work: Find Delphi, Athens, and Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Hippias driven out\" at 510 BC and \"Clisthenes'' reforms\" at 508 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXIX_HIPPIAS_DRIVEN_OUT_OF_ATHENS"}],"questions":["How did the Alcmaeonidae win the favor of the priests at Delphi?","What was written on the shells in an ostracism vote?","How many votes were needed to ostracize a man?"],"answers":["They rebuilt the temple in white marble, paying the extra cost themselves","The name of the man to be banished","Six thousand"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-5","title":"Chapter 40: The Great King","steps":["Read Chapter XL, \"The Great King,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the Great King and how he ruled his huge empire","Map work: Find Persia, Ecbatana, and Ionia on the coast of Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Darius I becomes king of Persia\" at 522 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XL_THE_GREAT_KING"}],"questions":["What were satraps?","What was special about the walls of Ecbatana?"],"answers":["Governors who ruled the provinces (satrapies) for the king","There were seven walls, each painted a different bright color"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-6","title":"Chapter 41: Hippias Visits Darius","steps":["Read Chapter XLI, \"Hippias Visits Darius,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how a Greek doctor helped make Darius want to conquer Greece","Map work: Trace a route from Persia across Asia Minor to Greece","Timeline: Add \"Democedes, Greek doctor at the Persian court\" at c. 520 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLI_HIPPIAS_VISITS_DARIUS"}],"questions":["How did Democedes become the king''s doctor?","Why did Darius decide to conquer Greece?"],"answers":["He healed the king''s hurt foot after the Persian doctors failed","His officers'' reports on Greece made him curious, and Hippias urged him on"],"notes":"The book tells this mostly from the historian Herodotus; scholars think the real reasons for the war were more political."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u6","title":"Unit 6: The Persian Wars (Chapters 42–53)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-1","title":"Chapter 42: Destruction of the Persian Host","steps":["Read Chapter XLII, \"Destruction of the Persian Host,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Darius was angry with the Athenians and what happened to his first army and fleet","Map work: Find Sardis, Ionia, the Hellespont, and Mount Athos","Timeline: Add \"Ionian Revolt; Sardis burned\" at 499–498 BC and \"Persian fleet wrecked off Mount Athos\" at 492 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLII_DESTRUCTION_OF_THE_PERSIAN_HOST"}],"questions":["What did Darius''s slave say to him every day at dinner?","What destroyed the Persian fleet near Mount Athos?"],"answers":["\"Master, remember the Athenians!\"","A terrible storm"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-2","title":"Chapter 43: The Advance of the Second Host","steps":["Read Chapter XLIII, \"The Advance of the Second Host,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Athens and Sparta answered Darius and how Miltiades came to command","Map work: Find Marathon, Athens, Sparta, and Plataea","Timeline: Add \"Persians demand earth and water\" at 491 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLIII_THE_ADVANCE_OF_THE_SECOND_HOST"}],"questions":["What did \"earth and water\" stand for?","Why did the Spartans say they could not come at once?","How did Miltiades become sole commander?"],"answers":["Accepting the Persian king as master of their land and sea","They believed they had to wait until the moon was full","Aristides and the other generals gave him their days of command"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-3","title":"Chapter 44: The Battle of Marathon","steps":["Read Chapter XLIV, \"The Battle of Marathon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the Battle of Marathon and the runner who carried the news","Map work: Find Marathon on a map of Greece and measure about how far it is to Athens","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Marathon\" at 490 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLIV_THE_BATTLE_OF_MARATHON"}],"questions":["Who attacked first at Marathon?","What did the runner say when he reached Athens?","Why did Miltiades march his tired army straight back to Athens?"],"answers":["The Greeks","\"Rejoice, we have conquered!\"","To defend the city in case the Persian fleet sailed there"],"notes":"The famous Marathon runner story is a later legend, the origin of today''s marathon race. Some of the gory details can be skipped."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-4","title":"Chapter 45: Miltiades'' Disgrace","steps":["Read Chapter XLV, \"Miltiades'' Disgrace,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Athens honored Miltiades and how he fell from favor","Map work: Find the Acropolis in Athens and the island of Paros","Timeline: Add \"Miltiades dies in disgrace\" at 489 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLV_MILTIADES_DISGRACE"}],"questions":["What was made from the captured Persian shields and arms?","Why was Miltiades put on trial?","Who was Miltiades'' son?"],"answers":["A huge statue of Athene on the Acropolis","He used the fleet to attack Paros over a personal quarrel and failed","Cimon"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-5","title":"Chapter 46: Aristides the Just","steps":["Read Chapter XLVI, \"Aristides the Just,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened when the farmer asked Aristides to write on his shell","Map work: Find Athens and the market place (agora)","Timeline: Add \"Aristides ostracized\" at 482 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLVI_ARISTIDES_THE_JUST"}],"questions":["What did Themistocles and Aristides disagree about?","Why did the farmer want Aristides banished?","What did Aristides write on the shell?"],"answers":["Themistocles wanted more ships; Aristides wanted a bigger land army","He was tired of hearing him called \"the Just\"","His own name"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-6","title":"Chapter 47: Two Noble Spartan Youths","steps":["Read Chapter XLVII, \"Two Noble Spartan Youths,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Bulis and Sperthias offered Xerxes and how he answered","Map work: Find Sparta and Persia","Timeline: Add \"Darius dies; Xerxes becomes king\" at 486 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLVII_TWO_NOBLE_SPARTAN_YOUTHS"}],"questions":["Why did Bulis and Sperthias go to Persia?","Why wouldn''t they bow before Xerxes?"],"answers":["To offer their lives to make up for the Persian messengers Sparta had killed","They said such honor belonged only to their gods"],"notes":"The army sizes here (two million) come from ancient writers; modern historians think it was far smaller."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-7","title":"Chapter 48: The Great Army","steps":["Read Chapter XLVIII, \"The Great Army,\" aloud together","Narration: describe Xerxes'' army crossing the Hellespont","Map work: Find the Hellespont and the canal at Mount Athos","Timeline: Add \"Xerxes crosses the Hellespont\" at 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLVIII_THE_GREAT_ARMY"}],"questions":["What did Xerxes do after a storm wrecked his bridges?","Why did Xerxes weep when he looked at his army?","Who were the Immortals?"],"answers":["Had the waves whipped and chains thrown into the strait","In a hundred years every one of those men would be dead","The king''s bodyguard of picked soldiers"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-8","title":"Chapter 49: Preparations for Defense","steps":["Read Chapter XLIX, \"Preparations for Defense,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Greeks prepared to meet Xerxes","Map work: Find Corinth, Thermopylae, and Artemisium","Timeline: Add \"Greek council at Corinth\" at 481 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLIX_PREPARATIONS_FOR_DEFENSE"}],"questions":["Why did so few soldiers go with Leonidas?","How many Spartans went to guard Thermopylae?"],"answers":["Most Greeks were at the Olympic games and believed it was wrong to fight during them","Three hundred"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-9","title":"Chapter 50: Leonidas at Thermopylæ","steps":["Read Chapter L, \"Leonidas at Thermopylæ,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Leonidas and his men held the pass","Map work: Find Thermopylae on a map of Greece and look at the narrow strip between mountains and sea","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Thermopylae\" at 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#L_LEONIDAS_AT_THERMOPYLAE"}],"questions":["What did Leonidas answer when told to give up his weapons?","What did he say about the arrows darkening the sun?"],"answers":["\"Come and take them!\"","\"Then we can fight in the shade.\""],"notes":"The \"fight in the shade\" line is usually credited to the Spartan Dieneces in Herodotus."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-10","title":"Chapter 51: Death of Leonidas","steps":["Read Chapter LI, \"Death of Leonidas,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Persians got around the Greeks at Thermopylae and what happened next","Map work: Find Thermopylae, Delphi, Artemisium, and the Isthmus of Corinth","Timeline: Add \"Death of Leonidas\" at 480 BC (same year as Thermopylae)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LI_DEATH_OF_LEONIDAS"}],"questions":["Who showed the Persians the secret path?","Which allies stayed to die with the Spartans?","What did the Greeks start building across the Isthmus of Corinth?"],"answers":["Ephialtes, a Greek shepherd","Seven hundred Thespians","A huge wall"],"notes":"The storm at Delphi is told as if the gods protected their temple; this comes from Herodotus."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-11","title":"Chapter 52: The Burning of Athens","steps":["Read Chapter LII, \"The Burning of Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what \"wooden walls\" meant and what happened to Athens","Map work: Find Athens and the Bay of Salamis","Timeline: Add \"Persians burn Athens\" at 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LII_THE_BURNING_OF_ATHENS"}],"questions":["What did Themistocles say the \"wooden walls\" were?","Where did the Athenian families go?","What did Themistocles say when Eurybiades raised his staff?"],"answers":["Their ships","To the Peloponnesus","\"Strike if you will, but hear me!\""]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-12","title":"Chapter 53: The Battles of Salamis and Platæa","steps":["Read Chapter LIII, \"The Battles of Salamis and Platæa,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the victories at Salamis and Plataea, and Pausanias''s two suppers","Map work: Find Salamis, Plataea, and Mycale (on the coast of Asia Minor)","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Salamis\" at 480 BC and \"Battles of Plataea and Mycale\" at 479 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LIII_THE_BATTLES_OF_SALAMIS_AND_PLATAEA"}],"questions":["Who rowed out to warn the Greek fleet of Xerxes'' plan?","Where did Xerxes watch the sea battle from?","What point did Pausanias make with the two suppers?"],"answers":["Aristides","A throne on a mountain","That the plain Spartans had beaten the luxurious Persians"],"notes":"The Greek commander at Mycale was actually the Spartan king Leotychides, not Eurybiades as the book says."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u7","title":"Unit 7: The Golden Age of Athens (Chapters 54–59)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-1","title":"Chapter 54: The Rebuilding of Athens","steps":["Read Chapter LIV, \"The Rebuilding of Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Athenians rebuilt their city and how Themistocles fooled the Spartans","Map work: Find Athens, Sparta, and Miletus","Timeline: Add \"Walls of Athens rebuilt\" at 478 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LIV_THE_REBUILDING_OF_ATHENS"}],"questions":["What \"good omen\" encouraged the Athenians?","How did Themistocles give Athens time to finish the walls?"],"answers":["A new shoot grew from the burned sacred olive tree","He drew out the talks in Sparta until the walls were high enough"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-2","title":"Chapter 55: Death of Pausanias","steps":["Read Chapter LV, \"Death of Pausanias,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened to Pausanias and to Themistocles","Map work: Find Sparta, then Persia, where Themistocles ended his life","Timeline: Add \"Pausanias dies\" at c. 470 BC and \"Themistocles ostracized\" at c. 471 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LV_DEATH_OF_PAUSANIAS"}],"questions":["What wrong did Pausanias do?","What did Themistocles refuse to do for the Persian king?"],"answers":["He secretly offered to help the Persians if they made him king of all Greece","Lead a Persian army against the Greeks"],"notes":"Mentions starvation and suicide; summarize if needed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-3","title":"Chapter 56: Cimon Improves Athens","steps":["Read Chapter LVI, \"Cimon Improves Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aristides and Cimon each served Athens","Map work: Find Athens, the island of Scyros, and the Theseum (Temple of Hephaestus) in Athens","Timeline: Add \"Theseus''s bones brought to Athens\" at c. 475 BC and \"Death of Aristides\" at c. 468 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LVI_CIMON_IMPROVES_ATHENS"}],"questions":["How rich was Aristides when he died?","What did the Athenians wrongly do with the allies'' money?","Where did Athenians go to hear philosophers?"],"answers":["So poor there was not enough to pay for his funeral","Spent it to beautify their own city instead of on the army and navy","The gardens of the Academy (and the Lyceum)"],"notes":"The bones were believed to be Theseus''s; the building called the Theseum is actually a temple of Hephaestus."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-4","title":"Chapter 57: The Earthquake","steps":["Read Chapter LVII, \"The Earthquake,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Cimon changed and what happened after the earthquake at Sparta","Map work: Find Sparta and Messenia, and Athens to the northeast","Timeline: Add \"Great earthquake at Sparta; Helots revolt\" at 464 BC and \"Cimon ostracized\" at 461 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LVII_THE_EARTHQUAKE"}],"questions":["Who scolded the young Cimon for being lazy?","How did Cimon help poor people who were too proud to ask?","Why did the Athenians ostracize Cimon?"],"answers":["Aristides the Just","His servants found out their needs and helped them secretly","They were angry that the Spartans had insulted the army Cimon urged them to send"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-5","title":"Chapter 58: The Age of Pericles","steps":["Read Chapter LVIII, \"The Age of Pericles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Pericles built and how Cimon served Athens one last time","Map work: Find the Acropolis, the Parthenon, and the port of Piraeus with the Long Walls","Timeline: Add \"Parthenon built\" at 447–432 BC and \"Thirty Years'' Peace\" at 446 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LVIII_THE_AGE_OF_PERICLES"}],"questions":["What did the Long Walls connect?","Who made the carvings and great statue for the Parthenon?","Why was Cimon''s death kept secret for a month?"],"answers":["Athens and its seaport, Piraeus","Phidias","So the army could get used to a new leader without losing courage"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-6","title":"Chapter 59: The Teachings of Anaxagoras","steps":["Read Chapter LIX, \"The Teachings of Anaxagoras,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Anaxagoras taught about the sun and moon and why he was exiled","Map work: Find Athens and the Peloponnesus (Anaxagoras guessed the sun was about as big as the Peloponnesus)","Timeline: Add \"Anaxagoras teaches in Athens\" at c. 460–430 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LIX_THE_TEACHINGS_OF_ANAXAGORAS"}],"questions":["What did Anaxagoras say the sun really was?","What did he think the moon was like?"],"answers":["A great glowing rock, not a god in a chariot","Much like the earth, with mountains, plains, and seas"],"notes":"The author writes from a 19th-century Christian viewpoint (Anaxagoras \"had never heard of the true God\"). Adjust the framing to suit your family."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u8","title":"Unit 8: The Peloponnesian War (Chapters 60–68)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-1","title":"Chapter 60: Beginning of the Peloponnesian War","steps":["Read Chapter LX, \"Beginning of the Peloponnesian War,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pericles explained the eclipse to his pilot","Map work: Find Attica, Athens, Piraeus, and the Peloponnesus","Timeline: Add \"Peloponnesian War begins\" at 431 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LX_BEGINNING_OF_THE_PELOPONNESIAN_WAR"}],"questions":["What was Pericles'' plan for fighting Sparta?","How did Pericles show his pilot what an eclipse is?"],"answers":["Bring everyone inside the city walls and fight with the navy at sea","He threw his cloak over the man''s head, blocking the sun the way the moon does"],"notes":"Try the cloak demonstration with a lamp and a ball."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-2","title":"Chapter 61: Death of Pericles","steps":["Read Chapter LXI, \"Death of Pericles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the plague in Athens and Pericles'' last words","Map work: Find Athens and the island of Cos, home of Hippocrates","Timeline: Add \"Plague in Athens\" at 430 BC and \"Death of Pericles\" at 429 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXI_DEATH_OF_PERICLES"}],"questions":["Why did Hippocrates refuse the Persian king''s offer?","What was Pericles proudest of as he died?"],"answers":["He said his duty was to save his own countrymen first","That he never caused any fellow citizen to put on mourning"],"notes":"Hippocrates was a real doctor, but the story of his coming to cure Athens is a later legend. Some plague descriptions are grim."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-3","title":"Chapter 62: The Philosopher Socrates","steps":["Read Chapter LXII, \"The Philosopher Socrates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what kind of man and teacher Socrates was","Map work: Find Athens on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Socrates teaching in Athens\" at c. 430–399 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXII_THE_PHILOSOPHER_SOCRATES"}],"questions":["What was Socrates'' trade?","What did Socrates say after being drenched with water?"],"answers":["A stonecutter","\"After the thunder comes the rain.\""],"notes":"The book''s view of Xanthippe as a scold comes from old anecdotes and is unkind; little is really known about her. The author also reads Christian ideas into Socrates."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-4","title":"Chapter 63: Socrates'' Favorite Pupil","steps":["Read Chapter LXIII, \"Socrates'' Favorite Pupil,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Alcibiades was like as a boy and how Socrates saved him","Map work: Find the Academy outside Athens where Socrates taught","Timeline: Add \"Socrates saves Alcibiades in battle\" at c. 432 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXIII_SOCRATES_FAVORITE_PUPIL"}],"questions":["Name Socrates'' three famous pupils from this chapter.","What did little Alcibiades do when a wagon would not stop?","Why was Alcibiades in danger in his first battle?"],"answers":["Plato, Xenophon, and Alcibiades","He lay down in the road and dared the driver to come on","His showy gold armor was weak and his flatterers left him"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-5","title":"Chapter 64: Youth of Alcibiades","steps":["Read Chapter LXIV, \"Youth of Alcibiades,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the tricks Alcibiades played to get people talking","Map work: Find Olympia, where Alcibiades raced his chariots","Timeline: Add \"Alcibiades wins at Olympia\" at 416 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXIV_YOUTH_OF_ALCIBIADES"}],"questions":["Why did Alcibiades cut off his dog''s tail?","How was Alcibiades different from Nicias?"],"answers":["To give the Athenians something to talk about","Alcibiades was bold and ambitious; Nicias was careful and slow"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-6","title":"Chapter 65: Greek Colonies in Italy","steps":["Read Chapter LXV, \"Greek Colonies in Italy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Sybarites and why Athens sent a fleet to Sicily","Map work: Find Sybaris, Croton, and Tarentum in southern Italy, and Syracuse and Messina in Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Athenian expedition to Sicily sails\" at 415 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXV_GREEK_COLONIES_IN_ITALY"}],"questions":["What does it mean to call someone a Sybarite?","What happened to the statues of Hermes just before the fleet sailed?"],"answers":["A person who loves luxury and ease too much","They were broken, and Alcibiades was blamed"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-7","title":"Chapter 66: Alcibiades in Disgrace","steps":["Read Chapter LXVI, \"Alcibiades in Disgrace,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alcibiades turned against Athens and then tried to help it again","Map work: Find Syracuse, Sparta, and Decelea near Athens","Timeline: Add \"Athenian defeat at Syracuse\" at 413 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXVI_ALCIBIADES_IN_DISGRACE"}],"questions":["Where did Alcibiades go instead of returning to Athens for trial?","What did the Spartans fortify only twelve miles from Athens?"],"answers":["Sparta","Decelea"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-8","title":"Chapter 67: Death of Alcibiades","steps":["Read Chapter LXVII, \"Death of Alcibiades,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the war ended and how Alcibiades died","Map work: Find the Chersonese (Gallipoli peninsula) by the Hellespont, then Athens","Timeline: Add \"Athenian fleet destroyed at Aegospotami\" at 405 BC, \"Athens surrenders\" at 404 BC, and \"Death of Alcibiades\" at 404 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXVII_DEATH_OF_ALCIBIADES"}],"questions":["What did the Spartans tear down to the sound of music?","Who were the Thirty Tyrants?"],"answers":["The Long Walls of Athens","Thirty cruel men the Spartans put in charge of Athens"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-9","title":"Chapter 68: The Overthrow of the Thirty Tyrants","steps":["Read Chapter LXVIII, \"The Overthrow of the Thirty Tyrants,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Thrasybulus freed Athens","Map work: Find Athens and Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Thrasybulus overthrows the Thirty Tyrants\" at 403 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXVIII_THE_OVERTHROW_OF_THE_THIRTY_TYRANTS"}],"questions":["Who drove out the Thirty Tyrants?","Why did Sparta have to pay for Lysander''s funeral?"],"answers":["Thrasybulus","He kept none of the booty and died poor"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u9","title":"Unit 9: Socrates, Xenophon, and Agesilaus (Chapters 69–75)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-1","title":"Chapter 69: Accusation of Socrates","steps":["Read Chapter LXIX, \"Accusation of Socrates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how a comedy helped turn people against Socrates","Map work: Find Delphi, whose oracle called Socrates the wisest man","Timeline: Add \"Aristophanes'' play The Clouds\" at 423 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXIX_ACCUSATION_OF_SOCRATES"}],"questions":["What did the oracle at Delphi say about Socrates?","Why did Socrates go to see the play that mocked him?","What two charges were made against Socrates?"],"answers":["That he was the most learned (wisest) man in Greece","To find out if any of the faults it showed were ones he could correct","Giving bad advice to young men and speaking ill of the gods"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-2","title":"Chapter 70: Death of Socrates","steps":["Read Chapter LXX, \"Death of Socrates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Socrates would not escape from prison","Map work: Find Athens and the island of Delos","Timeline: Add \"Death of Socrates\" at 399 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXX_DEATH_OF_SOCRATES"}],"questions":["Why was Socrates'' death delayed a month?","Why did Socrates refuse to escape?","Which pupil wrote down his last conversation?"],"answers":["No one could be executed while the sacred ship was away at Delos","It would be breaking the law, which he had never done","Plato"],"notes":"This chapter describes his death by poison calmly but in detail. The book again presents Socrates in Christian terms."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-3","title":"Chapter 71: The Defeat of Cyrus","steps":["Read Chapter LXXI, \"The Defeat of Cyrus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Cyrus fought his brother and what trouble the Greek soldiers were left in","Map work: Find Persia and the Euphrates River near Babylon, where Cunaxa was","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Cunaxa\" at 401 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXI_THE_DEFEAT_OF_CYRUS"}],"questions":["Why did Cyrus think he should be king instead of Artaxerxes?","What happened to the Greek officers after the battle?","Who came up with a plan to save the Greek army?"],"answers":["He was the first son born after their father became king","They were invited to a council and killed","Xenophon"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-4","title":"Chapter 72: The Retreat of the Ten Thousand","steps":["Read Chapter LXXII, \"The Retreat of the Ten Thousand,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the long march of the Ten Thousand","Map work: Trace the route from Babylon north through the mountains to the Black Sea and on to Pergamus","Timeline: Add \"Retreat of the Ten Thousand\" at 401–400 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXII_THE_RETREAT_OF_THE_TEN_THOUSAND"}],"questions":["What did Xenophon ask of the soldiers before agreeing to lead?","About how far did the Greeks march to reach the sea?","What is the name of Xenophon''s book about the march?"],"answers":["That each would promise to obey him and not quarrel","About a thousand miles","The Anabasis"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-5","title":"Chapter 73: Agesilaus in Asia","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIII, \"Agesilaus in Asia,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what kind of man and general Agesilaus was","Map work: Find Sparta, then Ionia on the coast of Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Agesilaus fights the Persians in Asia Minor\" at 396–394 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXIII_AGESILAUS_IN_ASIA"}],"questions":["How did Agesilaus look different from most Spartans?","Why did the Persians think Agesilaus was strange for a king?"],"answers":["He was small, weak, and lame","He dressed like his soldiers and led them himself, without pomp"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-6","title":"Chapter 74: A Strange Interview","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIV, \"A Strange Interview,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the meeting under the tree","Map work: Find Asia Minor (Phrygia) where Pharnabazus governed"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXIV_A_STRANGE_INTERVIEW"}],"questions":["What was Agesilaus eating when Pharnabazus arrived?","What did the Persian king try when his soldiers could not beat Agesilaus?"],"answers":["Bread and onions","Bribery: he paid Athens and its allies to rise against Sparta"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-7","title":"Chapter 75: The Peace of Antalcidas","steps":["Read Chapter LXXV, \"The Peace of Antalcidas,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Agesilaus had to come home and what the Peace of Antalcidas gave away","Map work: Find Coronea in Boeotia and the island of Cyprus","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Coronea\" at 394 BC and \"Peace of Antalcidas (King''s Peace)\" at 387 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXV_THE_PEACE_OF_ANTALCIDAS"}],"questions":["What did Agesilaus mean by \"ten thousand Persian archers\"?","What did the treaty hand over to the Persian king?"],"answers":["Ten thousand Persian coins stamped with an archer, used as a bribe","The Greek cities of Asia Minor and the island of Cyprus"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u10","title":"Unit 10: The Rise of Thebes (Chapters 76–80)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-1","title":"Chapter 76: The Theban Friends","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVI, \"The Theban Friends,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Sparta took Thebes and how Pelopidas and Epaminondas became friends","Map work: Find Thebes in Boeotia","Timeline: Add \"Sparta seizes Thebes\" at 382 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXVI_THE_THEBAN_FRIENDS"}],"questions":["How did the Spartans take Thebes without a fight?","How did Pelopidas and Epaminondas become friends?"],"answers":["They came during a festival when the Thebans were unarmed in the temple","Epaminondas saved the wounded Pelopidas in battle"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-2","title":"Chapter 77: Thebes Free Once More","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVII, \"Thebes Free Once More,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how twelve exiles freed Thebes","Map work: Trace the road from Athens to Thebes","Timeline: Add \"Thebes freed from Sparta\" at 379 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXVII_THEBES_FREE_ONCE_MORE"}],"questions":["How did the exiles get into the Spartan banquet?","What did Charon leave with Pelopidas to prove he was loyal?"],"answers":["Dressed as dancing girls","His only son, a baby"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-3","title":"Chapter 78: The Battle of Leuctra","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVIII, \"The Battle of Leuctra,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Sacred Battalion and the Battle of Leuctra","Map work: Find Leuctra in Boeotia, then Laconia","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Leuctra: Thebes defeats Sparta\" at 371 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXVIII_THE_BATTLE_OF_LEUCTRA"}],"questions":["What oath did the Sacred Battalion take?","What did Epaminondas say about the bad omens?","What terms did Epaminondas give Sparta?"],"answers":["Never to turn their backs on the enemy or surrender","That no omen forbids fighting to defend your country","Never attack Thebes again and set the Messenians free"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-4","title":"Chapter 79: Death of Pelopidas","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIX, \"Death of Pelopidas,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pelopidas was captured, freed, and finally killed","Map work: Find Thessaly in northern Greece","Timeline: Add \"Death of Pelopidas\" at 364 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXIX_DEATH_OF_PELOPIDAS"}],"questions":["Why was Pelopidas sent to Thessaly?","How did Epaminondas end up commanding when he was only a soldier?"],"answers":["To warn the cruel tyrant Alexander of Thessaly","The soldiers begged him to lead after their generals failed"],"notes":"The ending describes harsh treatment of the tyrant''s body; skip if needed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-5","title":"Chapter 80: The Battle of Mantinea","steps":["Read Chapter LXXX, \"The Battle of Mantinea,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Epaminondas''s last battle and his last words","Map work: Find Mantinea in the central Peloponnesus","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Mantinea; death of Epaminondas\" at 362 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXX_THE_BATTLE_OF_MANTINEA"}],"questions":["What two things did the wounded Epaminondas ask about first?","What did he call Leuctra and Mantinea?"],"answers":["How the battle was going and whether his shield was safe","His \"daughters,\" who would keep his name alive"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u11","title":"Unit 11: The Tyrants of Syracuse (Chapters 81–86)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-1","title":"Chapter 81: The Tyrant of Syracuse","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXI, \"The Tyrant of Syracuse,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Philoxenus answered the tyrant''s poems","Map work: Find Syracuse on the island of Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Dionysius the Elder rules Syracuse\" at 405–367 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXI_THE_TYRANT_OF_SYRACUSE"}],"questions":["Why did Dionysius send Philoxenus to the Quarries?","What did Philoxenus say the second time he was asked about the poems?"],"answers":["He honestly said the tyrant''s poems were trash","\"Take me back to the Quarries!\""]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-2","title":"Chapter 82: Story of Damon and Pythias","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXII, \"Story of Damon and Pythias,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Damon and Pythias","Map work: Find Syracuse on a map of Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Damon and Pythias\" near Dionysius (c. 4th century BC; a famous story)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXII_STORY_OF_DAMON_AND_PYTHIAS"}],"questions":["What did Damon offer to do for Pythias?","Name two things that delayed Pythias on his way back.","What did Dionysius do when Pythias returned?"],"answers":["Stay in prison and die in his place if he did not return","Robbers tied him to a tree; a flooded stream; a hot, dry plain","Set both of them free"],"notes":"A famous friendship tale, probably more legend than history."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-3","title":"Chapter 83: The Sword of Damocles","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIII, \"The Sword of Damocles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Damocles learned at the banquet","Map work: Find Syracuse in Sicily"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXIII_THE_SWORD_OF_DAMOCLES"}],"questions":["What was hanging over Damocles'' head?","What lesson did Dionysius teach him?"],"answers":["A sword hanging by a single hair","A person always living in fear cannot be truly happy"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-4","title":"Chapter 84: Dion and Dionysius","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIV, \"Dion and Dionysius,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Plato came to Syracuse and what happened to him and Dion","Map work: Find Athens, Syracuse, and Olympia","Timeline: Add \"Plato visits Syracuse\" at c. 367 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXIV_DION_AND_DIONYSIUS"}],"questions":["Who was Dion''s teacher in Athens?","How did the crowd at Olympia honor Plato?"],"answers":["Plato","They gave him an olive crown like a champion''s"],"notes":"Ancient sources place Plato being sold into slavery on his earlier visit to Dionysius the Elder; the book merges the stories."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-5","title":"Chapter 85: Civil War in Syracuse","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXV, \"Civil War in Syracuse,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Dion returned and trapped Dionysius in the citadel","Map work: Find Syracuse and its harbor","Timeline: Add \"Dion returns to Syracuse\" at 357 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXV_CIVIL_WAR_IN_SYRACUSE"}],"questions":["Why did Dion come back to Syracuse with an army?","Why did the people start to tire of Dion?"],"answers":["To punish Dionysius, who had forced Dion''s wife to marry another man","He was so strict and harsh in demanding virtue and obedience"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-6","title":"Chapter 86: Death of Dion","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVI, \"Death of Dion,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Dionysius''s letter turned the people against Dion, and how the tyrant ended up","Map work: Find Syracuse and Corinth","Timeline: Add \"Dion murdered\" at 354 BC and \"Dionysius the Younger exiled to Corinth\" at 344 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXVI_DEATH_OF_DION"}],"questions":["How did Dionysius trick the people into turning against Dion?","What job did Dionysius have at the end of his life in Corinth?"],"answers":["He wrote a letter that made it sound as if Dion wanted to be tyrant","Schoolteacher"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u12","title":"Unit 12: Philip of Macedon and Young Alexander (Chapters 87–94)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-1","title":"Chapter 87: Philip of Macedon","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVII, \"Philip of Macedon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Philip became king and how he built his army","Map work: Find Macedon north of Greece, and Thebes where Philip grew up","Timeline: Add \"Philip II becomes king of Macedon\" at 359 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXVII_PHILIP_OF_MACEDON"}],"questions":["Where did young Philip live as a hostage, and who looked after him?","What was the Macedonian phalanx?","What did the widow mean by appealing \"from Philip drunk to Philip sober\"?"],"answers":["In Thebes, in the house of Epaminondas","Soldiers packed in a solid block, shields locked, with long spears pointing out in rows","She wanted the king to judge her case again when his head was clear"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-2","title":"Chapter 88: Philip Begins His Conquests","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVIII, \"Philip Begins His Conquests,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Aster''s arrow and Demosthenes'' speeches","Map work: Find Olynthus and Thrace in the north","Timeline: Add \"Demosthenes'' first Philippic\" at 351 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXVIII_PHILIP_BEGINS_HIS_CONQUESTS"}],"questions":["How did Philip treat Nicanor, who spoke badly of him?","What was written on Aster''s arrow?","What is a \"Philippic\"?"],"answers":["Kindly, with a seat at his table and rich gifts","\"To Philip''s left eye\"","A fierce speech against someone, named after Demosthenes'' speeches against Philip"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-3","title":"Chapter 89: The Orator Demosthenes","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIX, \"The Orator Demosthenes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Demosthenes taught himself to be a great speaker","Map work: Find Olynthus, which Philip destroyed, and Athens","Timeline: Add \"Philip destroys Olynthus\" at 348 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXIX_THE_ORATOR_DEMOSTHENES"}],"questions":["Why did Demosthenes practice with pebbles in his mouth by the sea?","Why did he shave half his head?"],"answers":["To speak clearly and loudly enough to be heard over the waves","So he would be too embarrassed to go out and would stay home to study"],"notes":"Good chance to talk about practice and perseverance."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-4","title":"Chapter 90: Philip Masters Greece","steps":["Read Chapter XC, \"Philip Masters Greece,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Philip became master of Greece","Map work: Find Phocis, Delphi, and Chaeronea in Boeotia","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Chaeronea\" at 338 BC and \"Philip is murdered\" at 336 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XC","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XC_PHILIP_MASTERS_GREECE"}],"questions":["Why was the Sacred War fought?","How old was Alexander at Chaeronea, and what did he do there?","What did Demosthenes do during the battle?"],"answers":["The Phocians took land set aside for Apollo and robbed the temple at Delphi","Eighteen; he crushed the Theban Sacred Battalion","He ran away and begged for mercy when a bush caught his cloak"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-5","title":"Chapter 91: Birth of Alexander","steps":["Read Chapter XCI, \"Birth of Alexander,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the day Alexander was born and how he was raised","Map work: Find Pella in Macedon and Ephesus in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Alexander born; Temple of Diana at Ephesus burned\" at 356 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCI_BIRTH_OF_ALEXANDER"}],"questions":["What three events were said to happen on the day Alexander was born?","Which books did Alexander keep under his pillow?"],"answers":["Parmenio won a victory, Philip''s horses won at Olympia, and the Temple of Diana at Ephesus burned","The Iliad and the Odyssey"],"notes":"The three events falling on one day is an ancient tradition, not certain fact."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-6","title":"Chapter 92: The Steed Bucephalus","steps":["Read Chapter XCII, \"The Steed Bucephalus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander tamed Bucephalus","Map work: Find Pella and Macedon; Aristotle taught Alexander nearby at Mieza","Timeline: Add \"Aristotle becomes Alexander''s tutor\" at 343 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCII_THE_STEED_BUCEPHALUS"}],"questions":["What does \"Bucephalus\" mean?","What was frightening the horse?","What did Alexander send Aristotle from his travels?"],"answers":["Ox-head","His own shadow","Collections of animals and plants"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-7","title":"Chapter 93: Alexander as King","steps":["Read Chapter XCIII, \"Alexander as King,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Alexander did when the Greeks rebelled after Philip died","Map work: Find Thrace, Thebes, and Corinth","Timeline: Add \"Alexander becomes king\" at 336 BC and \"Thebes destroyed\" at 335 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCIII_ALEXANDER_AS_KING"}],"questions":["Why were Demosthenes and the Athenians happy when Philip died?","Which single house did Alexander spare in Thebes?"],"answers":["They thought a young boy could not hold onto power and they would be free","The house of the poet Pindar"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-8","title":"Chapter 94: Alexander and Diogenes","steps":["Read Chapter XCIV, \"Alexander and Diogenes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened when Alexander visited Diogenes","Map work: Find Corinth on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Alexander meets Diogenes\" at c. 336 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCIV_ALEXANDER_AND_DIOGENES"}],"questions":["Why did Diogenes throw away his wooden bowl?","What was Diogenes looking for with his lantern?","What did Diogenes ask Alexander to do for him?"],"answers":["He saw a child drink from its hand and decided he didn''t need it","An honest man","Stand out of his sunshine"],"notes":"The story that they died on the same night is an old tradition."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u13","title":"Unit 13: Alexander the Great (Chapters 95–103)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-1","title":"Chapter 95: Alexander''s Brilliant Beginning","steps":["Read Chapter XCV, \"Alexander''s Brilliant Beginning,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander began his war on Persia","Map work: Find the Hellespont, Troy, the Granicus River, Sardis, Ephesus, and Caria","Timeline: Add \"Battle of the Granicus\" at 334 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCV_ALEXANDERS_BRILLIANT_BEGINNING"}],"questions":["What did Alexander say he kept for himself after giving gifts away?","Who saved Alexander''s life at the Granicus?","What was Aristotle''s \"recipe\" for a good appetite?"],"answers":["His hopes","Clytus, son of his old nurse","A march before daybreak for dinner, and a light dinner for supper"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-2","title":"Chapter 96: The Gordian Knot","steps":["Read Chapter XCVI, \"The Gordian Knot,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Gordian knot and Alexander''s trust in his doctor","Map work: Find Gordium in Phrygia, Tarsus, and the Cydnus River","Timeline: Add \"Alexander cuts the Gordian knot\" at 333 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCVI_THE_GORDIAN_KNOT"}],"questions":["What was promised to whoever untied the Gordian knot?","What does \"cutting the Gordian knot\" mean today?","What did Alexander do with the warning letter about his doctor?"],"answers":["Rule over all Asia","Solving a hard problem by a bold, direct action","Handed it to the doctor while he drank the medicine"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-3","title":"Chapter 97: Alexander''s Royal Captives","steps":["Read Chapter XCVII, \"Alexander''s Royal Captives,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander treated the Persian royal family","Map work: Find Issus, at the corner of Asia Minor and Syria","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Issus\" at 333 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCVII_ALEXANDERS_ROYAL_CAPTIVES"}],"questions":["Why did the queen mother kneel to the wrong man?","What did Alexander say about Hephaestion?","Why did Sisygambis cry when Alexander offered to teach spinning?"],"answers":["Hephaestion was taller, and they wore plain armor","\"He is my other self\"","Persian royal ladies thought work was for slaves"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-4","title":"Chapter 98: Alexander at Jerusalem","steps":["Read Chapter XCVIII, \"Alexander at Jerusalem,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the siege of Tyre and Alexander''s visit to Jerusalem","Map work: Find Damascus, Sidon, Tyre, and Jerusalem","Timeline: Add \"Siege of Tyre\" at 332 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCVIII_ALEXANDER_AT_JERUSALEM"}],"questions":["How did Alexander reach the island city of Tyre without a fleet?","Who came out to meet Alexander at Jerusalem?"],"answers":["He built a causeway out to the island","The high priest Jaddua with the Levites"],"notes":"The Jerusalem visit comes from the Jewish historian Josephus; many historians doubt it happened. The book tells it as fact, from a religious viewpoint. Tyre''s punishment is grim."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-5","title":"Chapter 99: The African Desert","steps":["Read Chapter XCIX, \"The African Desert,\" aloud together","Narration: describe Alexander''s march across the desert and the battle at Arbela","Map work: Find Alexandria in Egypt, the Siwa oasis in the Libyan desert, the Euphrates and Tigris rivers, and Arbela","Timeline: Add \"Alexandria founded\" at 331 BC and \"Battle of Arbela (Gaugamela)\" at 331 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCIX_THE_AFRICAN_DESERT"}],"questions":["Which city did Alexander found at the mouth of the Nile?","What is a mirage?","How did the soldiers cross the Tigris?"],"answers":["Alexandria","Seeing water and trees in the desert that are not really there","They swam, holding their shields over their heads"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-6","title":"Chapter 100: Death of Darius","steps":["Read Chapter C, \"Death of Darius,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander changed after his victories and how Darius died","Map work: Find Babylon, Susa, and Persepolis","Timeline: Add \"Persepolis burned; death of Darius III\" at 330 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter C","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#C_DEATH_OF_DARIUS"}],"questions":["What did Alexander find at Susa and send back to Athens?","Why did Alexander refuse to share Persia with Darius?","Who killed Darius?"],"answers":["A bronze statue that Xerxes had carried off","He said the world could not have two masters any more than two suns","Bessus, one of his own followers"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-7","title":"Chapter 101: Defeat of Porus","steps":["Read Chapter CI, \"Defeat of Porus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander treated Porus","Map work: Find Persia, then northwest India and the Indus River region","Timeline: Add \"Death of Clytus\" at 328 BC and \"Battle with Porus\" at 326 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CI_DEFEAT_OF_PORUS"}],"questions":["Why did Alexander kill his friend Clytus?","What did Porus answer when asked how he wished to be treated?","What city did Alexander name after his horse?"],"answers":["Clytus scolded him for his drinking, and Alexander struck him in a drunken rage","\"Like a king!\"","Bucephala"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-8","title":"Chapter 102: The Return to Babylon","steps":["Read Chapter CII, \"The Return to Babylon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Alexander turned back and how his army and fleet returned","Map work: Find the Hyphasis and Indus rivers, the Indian Ocean coast, and the Persian Gulf","Timeline: Add \"Army refuses to go on at the Hyphasis\" at 326 BC and \"Nearchus''s voyage\" at 325 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CII_THE_RETURN_TO_BABYLON"}],"questions":["Why did Alexander have to stop his conquests?","What did Alexander do with the little water his men brought him?","Why was Nearchus''s voyage important?"],"answers":["His soldiers refused to go any farther","Poured it on the sand, since his men could not drink too","It opened a sea route for trade with the East"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-9","title":"Chapter 103: Death of Alexander the Great","steps":["Read Chapter CIII, \"Death of Alexander the Great,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Alexander''s last days and what he said about who should rule","Map work: Find Babylon, where Alexander died, and Alexandria, where he was buried","Timeline: Add \"Death of Alexander the Great\" at 323 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CIII_DEATH_OF_ALEXANDER_THE_GREAT"}],"questions":["How old was Alexander when he died?","Whom did Alexander say should have the throne?","Why did Ptolemy want Alexander''s body in Egypt?"],"answers":["Thirty-three","The strongest","An oracle said whoever buried Alexander would be master of his kingdom"],"notes":"The mass weddings actually took place at Susa, and Hephaestion died at Ecbatana; the book moves both to Babylon."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u14","title":"Unit 14: After Alexander (Chapters 104–109)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-1","title":"Chapter 104: The Division of the Realm","steps":["Read Chapter CIV, \"The Division of the Realm,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the generals tried to share Alexander''s empire","Map work: Find Macedon, Babylon, and Egypt, and look at how huge the empire was","Timeline: Add \"Generals divide Alexander''s empire\" at 323 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CIV_THE_DIVISION_OF_THE_REALM"}],"questions":["Who were named to follow Alexander as king?","Into how many provinces was the kingdom divided?"],"answers":["His half-brother Arridaeus and his baby son","Thirty-three"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-2","title":"Chapter 105: Death of Demosthenes","steps":["Read Chapter CV, \"Death of Demosthenes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Greeks revolted and how Demosthenes died","Map work: Find Lamia near Thermopylae and the island of Calauria (Poros), where Demosthenes died","Timeline: Add \"Lamian War\" at 323–322 BC and \"Death of Demosthenes\" at 322 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CV_DEATH_OF_DEMOSTHENES"}],"questions":["What did Phocion say when people rushed to revolt?","How did Demosthenes escape being taken prisoner?"],"answers":["If Alexander is dead today he will still be dead tomorrow, so there is time to think","He took poison hidden in his writing reed"],"notes":"Describes a suicide; summarize if needed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-3","title":"Chapter 106: The Last of the Athenians","steps":["Read Chapter CVI, \"The Last of the Athenians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened to Perdiccas, Phocion, and Alexander''s family","Map work: Find the Nile in Egypt, Athens, and Macedon","Timeline: Add \"Death of Phocion\" at 318 BC and \"Alexander''s son killed\" at c. 310 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CVI_THE_LAST_OF_THE_ATHENIANS"}],"questions":["How did Perdiccas die?","Why is Phocion called the \"Last of the Athenians\"?"],"answers":["His soldiers turned on him after many were eaten by crocodiles crossing the Nile","He was the last great Athenian statesman; no one after him tried to win back the city''s freedom"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-4","title":"Chapter 107: The Colossus of Rhodes","steps":["Read Chapter CVII, \"The Colossus of Rhodes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Colossus of Rhodes came to be built","Map work: Find the island of Rhodes, then mark the Seven Wonders on a map","Timeline: Add \"Siege of Rhodes\" at 305–304 BC and \"Colossus of Rhodes\" at c. 280–226 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CVII_THE_COLOSSUS_OF_RHODES"}],"questions":["What nickname did Demetrius earn at Rhodes, and what does it mean?","What paid for the Colossus?","How was the Colossus destroyed?"],"answers":["Poliorcetes, \"the city taker\"","Selling the war machines Demetrius left behind","An earthquake knocked it down"],"notes":"The idea that ships sailed between its legs is a later legend. The book''s list of wonders differs a little from the usual seven."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-5","title":"Chapter 108: The Battle of Ipsus","steps":["Read Chapter CVIII, \"The Battle of Ipsus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Battle of Ipsus decided who got which part of the empire","Map work: Find Ipsus in Asia Minor, Egypt, Syria and Antioch, Thrace, and Macedon","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Ipsus\" at 301 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CVIII_THE_BATTLE_OF_IPSUS"}],"questions":["Who ruled Egypt after Ipsus, and who was the last of his family?","How did the Athenians treat Demetrius after his defeat?"],"answers":["Ptolemy; Cleopatra","They turned against him and made it a crime to speak well of him"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-6","title":"Chapter 109: Demetrius and the Athenians","steps":["Read Chapter CIX, \"Demetrius and the Athenians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Demetrius surprised the Athenians, and how the Gauls were driven off","Map work: Find Athens, Delphi, and Galatia in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Demetrius takes Athens\" at 295 BC and \"Gauls attack Delphi\" at 279 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CIX_DEMETRIUS_AND_THE_ATHENIANS"}],"questions":["What did Demetrius do instead of punishing the Athenians?","Where did the defeated Gauls go?"],"answers":["He forgave them and sent grain to every house","To a part of Asia Minor later called Galatia"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u15","title":"Unit 15: The Last Days of Free Greece (Chapters 110–115)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-1","title":"Chapter 110: The Achæan League","steps":["Read Chapter CX, \"The Achæan League,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aratus freed Sicyon and captured the fortress of Corinth","Map work: Find Sicyon, Corinth, and Acrocorinth on the Isthmus","Timeline: Add \"Aratus frees Sicyon\" at 251 BC and \"Acrocorinth captured\" at 243 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CX_THE_ACHAEAN_LEAGUE"}],"questions":["Where did young Aratus hide from the tyrant?","How many years in a row was Aratus chosen leader?","Why did Aratus pawn his wife''s jewels?"],"answers":["In the house of the tyrant''s own sister","Thirty-five","To pay the man who showed the secret path up to Acrocorinth"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-2","title":"Chapter 111: Division in Sparta","steps":["Read Chapter CXI, \"Division in Sparta,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Agis tried to change in Sparta and how Chilonis stood by her family","Map work: Find Sparta in Laconia","Timeline: Add \"Agis IV tries to reform Sparta\" at c. 244 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXI_DIVISION_IN_SPARTA"}],"questions":["What did Agis want to do with the land and money?","How did Chilonis show loyalty?"],"answers":["Divide it equally among the Spartans","She stayed with whichever of her father or husband was in trouble and followed him into exile"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-3","title":"Chapter 112: Death of Agis","steps":["Read Chapter CXII, \"Death of Agis,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Agis was betrayed and how Cleomenes carried on his plan","Map work: Find Sparta and Macedon","Timeline: Add \"Death of Agis\" at 241 BC and \"Cleomenes'' reforms\" at 227 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXII_DEATH_OF_AGIS"}],"questions":["How was Agis caught?","Who taught Cleomenes about Agis''s ideas?","What does the nickname \"Doson\" mean?"],"answers":["Two false friends told the ephors he left the temple each night to bathe","Agiatis, Agis''s widow, who became Cleomenes'' wife","\"Who will give,\" because he promised gifts he never gave"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-4","title":"Chapter 113: The War of the Two Leagues","steps":["Read Chapter CXIII, \"The War of the Two Leagues,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aratus''s mistake let Macedon and then Rome into Greece","Map work: Find Sellasia in Laconia, Aetolia, and Rome in Italy","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Sellasia\" at 222 BC and \"Death of Aratus\" at 213 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXIII_THE_WAR_OF_THE_TWO_LEAGUES"}],"questions":["Whom did Aratus ask for help against Sparta, and why was it a mistake?","To whom did the Spartans and Aetolians turn for help?"],"answers":["The Macedonian king, who really wanted to rule Greece himself","Rome"],"notes":"Includes the cruel \"statue\" of Nabis and a murder-suicide; preview. The order of events in this chapter is compressed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-5","title":"Chapter 114: The Last of the Greeks","steps":["Read Chapter CXIV, \"The Last of the Greeks,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Philopoemen, the \"Last of the Greeks\"","Map work: Find Megalopolis, Messenia, and Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Death of Philopoemen\" at 183 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXIV_THE_LAST_OF_THE_GREEKS"}],"questions":["What happened when a hostess mistook Philopoemen for a servant?","Why is he called the \"Last of the Greeks\"?","What were his last words about?"],"answers":["He calmly split wood as she asked","He was the last to try to keep Greece independent","He was glad the Achaean army had escaped safely"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-6","title":"Chapter 115: Greece a Roman Province","steps":["Read Chapter CXV, \"Greece a Roman Province,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Greece went from being \"freed\" by Rome to being ruled by it","Map work: Find Corinth and the Isthmus, Macedon, and Rome","Timeline: Add \"Flamininus proclaims Greek freedom\" at 196 BC and \"Corinth destroyed; Greece under Rome\" at 146 BC. Look back over your whole timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXV_GREECE_A_ROMAN_PROVINCE"}],"questions":["What did the Roman consul announce at the Isthmian games?","What happened to Corinth?","What name did the Romans give the province of Greece?"],"answers":["That Rome had beaten the king of Macedon and the Greek states were free","It was burned and its art carried off to Rome","Achaia"],"notes":"Corinth was destroyed by the Roman general Mummius, not Aemilius Paulus, who had defeated Macedon in 168 BC."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “The Story of the Greeks” by H. A. Guerber (1896), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/23495"}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-this-country-of-ours', 'Freerange Library', 'publisher', true, 'This Country of Ours', array['history', 'civics', 'geography']::text[], 3, 7, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical']::text[], 'online', 0, 'H. E. Marshall''s classic story of America, told as one lively tale after another, from the Vikings and Columbus through the colonies, the Revolution, the Civil War, and up to 1917. Each day you read one chapter aloud, your children narrate it back, find the places on a map, and add a date to the family timeline. Written for children in a warm storytelling voice, and the full text is free online.', array['99 lessons in 12 units, one chapter each', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Map and timeline work in every lesson', 'Questions with answers for each chapter', 'Parent notes on 53 chapters where the 1917 telling is dated or one-sided']::text[], 99, 3, 30, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🗺️', '#2F6B5E', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-this-country-of-ours', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, 'Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “This Country of Ours: The Story of the United States” by H. E. Marshall (1917), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3761', '[{"title":"Part I: Stories of Explorers and Pioneers","lessons":["Chapter 1: How the Vikings of Old Sought and Found New Lands","Chapter 2: The Sea of Darkness and the Great Faith of Columbus","Chapter 3: How Columbus Fared Forth Upon The Sea of Darkness and Came to Pleasant Lands Beyond","Chapter 4: How Columbus Returned Home in Triumph","Chapter 5: How America Was Named","Chapter 6: How The Flag of England Was Planted on the Shores of the New World","Chapter 7: How The Flag of France Was Planted in Florida","Chapter 8: How The French Founded a Colony in Florida","Chapter 9: How the Spaniards Drove the French Out of Florida","Chapter 10: How a Frenchman Avenged the Death of His Countrymen","Chapter 11: The Adventures of Sir Humphrey Gilbert","Chapter 12: About Sir Walter Raleigh''s Adventures in the Golden West"]},{"title":"Part II: Stories of Virginia","lessons":["Chapter 13: The Adventures of Captain John Smith","Chapter 14: More Adventures of Captain John Smith","Chapter 15: How the Colony was Saved","Chapter 16: How Pocahontas Took a Journey Over the Seas","Chapter 17: How the Redmen Fought Against Their White Brothers","Chapter 18: How Englishmen Fought a Duel With Tyranny","Chapter 19: The Coming of the Cavaliers","Chapter 20: Bacon''s Rebellion","Chapter 21: The Story of the Knights of the Golden Horseshoe"]},{"title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Pilgrims to Harvard)","lessons":["Chapter 22: The Story of the Pilgrim Fathers","Chapter 23: The Founding of Massachusetts","Chapter 24: The Story of Harry Vane","Chapter 25: The Story of Anne Hutchinson and the Founding of Rhode Island","Chapter 26: The Founding of Harvard","Chapter 27: How Quakers First Came to New England","Chapter 28: How Maine and New Hampshire Were Founded"]},{"title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Connecticut to Salem)","lessons":["Chapter 29: The Founding of Connecticut and War with the Indians","Chapter 30: The Founding of New Haven","Chapter 31: The Hunt for the Regicides","Chapter 32: King Philip''s War","Chapter 33: How The Charter of Connecticut Was Saved","Chapter 34: The Witches of Salem"]},{"title":"Part IV: Stories of the Middle and Southern Colonies","lessons":["Chapter 35: The Founding of Maryland","Chapter 36: How New Amsterdam Became New York","Chapter 37: How a German Ruled New York","Chapter 38: Pirates!","Chapter 39: The Founding of New Jersey","Chapter 40: The Founding of Pennsylvania","Chapter 41: How Benjamin Franklin Came to Philadelphia","Chapter 42: The Founding of North and South Carolina","Chapter 43: War With the Indians in North and South Carolina","Chapter 44: The Founding of Georgia"]},{"title":"Part V: Stories of the French in America","lessons":["Chapter 45: How the Mississippi was Discovered","Chapter 46: King William''s War and Queen Anne''s War","Chapter 47: The Mississippi Bubble","Chapter 48: How a Terrible Disaster Befell the British Army","Chapter 49: The End of the French Rule in America","Chapter 50: The Rebellion of Pontiac"]},{"title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Tea Party to Saratoga)","lessons":["Chapter 51: The Boston Tea party","Chapter 52: Paul Revere''s Ride - The Unsheathing of the Sword","Chapter 53: The First Thrust-The Battle of Bunker Hill","Chapter 54: The War In Canada","Chapter 55: The Birth of A Great Nation","Chapter 56: The Darkest Hour - Trenton and Princeton","Chapter 57: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bennington and Oriskany","Chapter 58: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bemis Heights and Saratoga"]},{"title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Valley Forge to Yorktown)","lessons":["Chapter 59: Brandywine - Germantown - Valley Forge","Chapter 60: War on the Sea","Chapter 61: The Battle of Monmouth - The Story of Captain Molly","Chapter 62: The Story of a Great Crime","Chapter 63: A Turning Point in the World''s History"]},{"title":"Part VII: The Young Nation (Washington to Jackson)","lessons":["Chapter 64: Washington First In War, First In Peace","Chapter 65: Adams - How He Kept Peace with France","Chapter 66: Jefferson - How the Territory of the United States was Doubled","Chapter 67: Jefferson - How the Door Into the Far West was Opened","Chapter 68: Jefferson - About An American Who Wanted to be a King","Chapter 69: Madison - The Shooting Star and the Prophet","Chapter 70: Madison - War with Great Britain","Chapter 71: Monroe-The First Whispers of a Storm-Monroe''s Famous Doctrine","Chapter 72: Adams - The Tariff of Abominations","Chapter 73: Jackson - \"Liberty and Union, Now and Forever\" - Van Buren - Hard Times"]},{"title":"Part VII: Growth and Division (Harrison to Buchanan)","lessons":["Chapter 74: Harrison - The Hero of Tippecanoe","Chapter 75: Tyler - Florida Becomes a State","Chapter 76: Polk - How Much Land Was Added to the United States","Chapter 77: Polk - The Finding of Gold","Chapter 78: Taylor - Union or Disunion","Chapter 79: Fillmore - The Underground Railroad","Chapter 80: Pierce - The Story of \"Bleeding Kansas\"","Chapter 81: Buchanan - The Story of the Mormons","Chapter 82: Buchanan - The First Shots"]},{"title":"Part VII: The Civil War (Lincoln)","lessons":["Chapter 83: Lincoln - From Bull Run to Fort Donelson","Chapter 84: Lincoln - The Story of the First Battle Between Ironclads","Chapter 85: Lincoln - The Battle of Shiloh and the Taking of New Orleans","Chapter 86: Lincoln - The Slaves are Made Free","Chapter 87: Lincoln - Chancellorsville - The Death of Stonewall Jackson","Chapter 88: Lincoln - The Battle of Gettysburg","Chapter 89: Lincoln-Grant''s Campaign-Sheridan''s Ride","Chapter 90: Lincoln - Sherman''s March to the Sea - Lincoln Re-Elected President","Chapter 91: Lincoln - The End of the War - The President''s Death"]},{"title":"Part VII: Reunion and a New Century (Johnson to Wilson)","lessons":["Chapter 92: Johnson - How The President Was Impeached","Chapter 93: Grant - A Peaceful Victory","Chapter 94: Hayes - Garfield - Arthur","Chapter 95: Cleveland - Harrison - Cleveland","Chapter 96: McKinley - War and Sudden Death","Chapter 97: Roosevelt - Taft","Chapter 98: Wilson - Troubles With Mexico","Chapter 99: Wilson -The Great War"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-this-country-of-ours', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u1","title":"Part I: Stories of Explorers and Pioneers","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: How the Vikings of Old Sought and Found New Lands","steps":["Read chapter 1 aloud together; listen for the three names Leif gives the new lands","Oral narration: tell the story of Leif''s voyage, then what happened to Thorvald and Thorfinn","Find Greenland, Labrador, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia on a map","Timeline: Leif Ericson reaches Vineland (about 1000)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 1","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00019"}],"questions":["Who first saw the new land but never went ashore?","What did Tyrker find that gave Vineland its name?","What was the name of the first child of European parents born in Vineland?"],"answers":["Bjarni","Grapes (wild vines)","Snorri"],"notes":"The book calls the Native people ''savages'' and describes them as ugly; the Norse sagas only tell the newcomers'' side. Talk about how the people already living there might have seen armed strangers arriving."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Sea of Darkness and the Great Faith of Columbus","steps":["Read chapter 2 aloud together; listen for the dangers people believed lived in the Sea of Darkness","Oral narration: tell how Columbus finally won the help of Spain","Find Portugal, Spain, Granada and Palos on a map","Timeline: Columbus sails from Palos (August 3, 1492)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 2","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00091"}],"questions":["Why did Columbus think he could reach India by sailing west?","Which king first turned Columbus down and then secretly sent out his own ships?","What did Queen Isabella say she would do to pay for the voyage?"],"answers":["Because the world is round","King John of Portugal","Pawn her jewels"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: How Columbus Fared Forth Upon The Sea of Darkness and Came to Pleasant Lands Beyond","steps":["Read chapter 3 aloud together; listen for how Columbus kept his crew from losing heart","Oral narration: tell about the first sight of land and the landing","Find the Bahamas (San Salvador), Cuba and the West Indies on a map","Timeline: Columbus lands on San Salvador (October 12, 1492)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 3","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00122"}],"questions":["Why did Columbus keep two different records of how far the ships had sailed?","Why are these islands called the West Indies?","What two new things did the Spaniards see but not bother to bring home?"],"answers":["So the frightened sailors would not know how far from home they were","Columbus thought he had reached islands near India","Tobacco and potatoes"],"notes":"The Taíno people are called ''naked savages'' here and are seen only as sources of gold. A good moment to talk about who already lived on these islands and what the coming of the Spaniards meant for them."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: How Columbus Returned Home in Triumph","steps":["Read chapter 4 aloud together; listen for the story of the egg","Oral narration: tell about Columbus''s welcome in Spain and what he found when he went back to his fort","Find Haiti (Española), Barcelona and the mouth of the Orinoco River on a map","Timeline: Columbus returns to Palos (March 15, 1493)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 4","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00158"}],"questions":["How was the Santa Maria wrecked?","What point did Columbus make with the egg?","What had happened to the fort of La Navidad when Columbus returned?"],"answers":["A boy was left at the tiller and ran her onto a sandbank","Once a thing has been done, anyone can see how easy it is","It had been burned and all the men were dead"],"notes":"The chapter mentions ''dark-skinned savages'' Columbus brought to Spain; they were Taíno people taken across the ocean. Worth a short talk about how they came to be there."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: How America Was Named","steps":["Read chapter 5 aloud together; listen for who first wrote down the word ''America''","Oral narration: explain how the New World got its name","Find Florence, Venezuela, South America and the District of Columbia on a map","Timeline: The name ''America'' first appears in print (1507)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 5","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00185"}],"questions":["What work did Amerigo Vespucci do in Florence before he went to sea?","Was the name America first given to North or South America?","Name one place that was named to honour Columbus."],"answers":["He was a clerk for the Medici merchant house","South America","The District of Columbia (also British Columbia or Colombia)"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: How The Flag of England Was Planted on the Shores of the New World","steps":["Read chapter 6 aloud together; listen for the name of Cabot''s ship","Oral narration: tell the story of John Cabot''s voyage from Bristol","Find Bristol, Ireland, Cape Breton and Labrador on a map","Timeline: John Cabot reaches North America (June 24, 1497)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 6","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00209"}],"questions":["Which English king gave Cabot permission to sail?","How did Cabot''s men catch fish in the new land?","How much did the king give Cabot as a reward?"],"answers":["Henry VII","They lowered a basket weighted with a stone","Ten pounds"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-7","title":"Chapter 7: How The Flag of France Was Planted in Florida","steps":["Read chapter 7 aloud together; listen for why the Huguenots wanted to leave France","Oral narration: tell what happened to the men left at Charlesfort","Find Florida and Beaufort, South Carolina, on a map","Timeline: Ribaut''s Huguenots land in Florida (1562)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 7","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00235"}],"questions":["Who were the Huguenots?","Who kept the Charlesfort men from starving?","What were the sails of their homemade ship made from?"],"answers":["French Protestants","The local Indian chiefs who shared their food","Shirts and bedclothes"],"notes":"Uses ''Redmen'' and ''Pale-faces'', but also shows how generous the local people were. The voyage home ends with the starving men killing one of their own for food; preview before reading to young children."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-8","title":"Chapter 8: How The French Founded a Colony in Florida","steps":["Read chapter 8 aloud together; listen for how many times ships arrive at Fort Caroline","Oral narration: tell the troubles of Fort Caroline from its building to Ribaut''s arrival","Find the St. Johns River in Florida and the West Indies on a map","Timeline: Fort Caroline is built (1564)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 8","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00282"}],"questions":["What did the French name their fort, and why?","Why did the colonists starve when there was rich land all around them?","Which English admiral helped the starving French?"],"answers":["Fort Caroline, in honour of King Charles","They never ploughed or planted; they waited for food from France","John Hawkins"],"notes":"Chief Satouriona and his people are called ''savages'' with ''fiendish'' yells. Notice that the French broke their promise to him; talk about the Timucua side of the story."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-9","title":"Chapter 9: How the Spaniards Drove the French Out of Florida","steps":["Read chapter 9 aloud together; listen for the question Menendez asks the French every time he meets them","Oral narration: tell how Menendez took Fort Caroline and what happened to Ribaut''s men","Find St. Augustine and the St. Johns River in Florida on a map","Timeline: Menendez founds St. Augustine and destroys Fort Caroline (1565)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 9","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00354"}],"questions":["What town did Menendez found, and why is it famous today?","Why were there almost no guards on the walls when the Spaniards attacked Fort Caroline?","Why did Menendez say he was killing the Frenchmen?"],"answers":["St. Augustine, the oldest town in the United States","It was raining so hard the captain sent the guards to bed","Because they were Protestants (''Lutherans''), not because they were French"],"notes":"A grim chapter of massacres driven by religious hatred between Catholics and Protestants; preview it for younger children. A chance to talk about religious freedom."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-10","title":"Chapter 10: How a Frenchman Avenged the Death of His Countrymen","steps":["Read chapter 10 aloud together; listen for who joins Gourges in the attack","Oral narration: tell how Dominique de Gourges took revenge","Find Fort Caroline (Jacksonville, Florida), Cuba and France on a map","Timeline: Gourges destroys the Spanish forts on the St. Johns River (1568)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 10","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00432"}],"questions":["How did Gourges pay for his expedition?","Which Indian chief helped the French against the Spaniards?","What did the King of Spain do when he heard what Gourges had done?"],"answers":["He sold everything he had and borrowed money","Satouriona","He offered a large reward for his head"],"notes":"Revenge is shown as heroic here, and the Native warriors are described as ''mad with the desire of blood.'' Ask your child whether revenge solved anything, and why Satouriona''s people hated the Spaniards."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-11","title":"Chapter 11: The Adventures of Sir Humphrey Gilbert","steps":["Read chapter 11 aloud together; listen for the names of Sir Humphrey''s ships","Oral narration: tell about Sir Humphrey''s last night at sea","Find St. John''s, Newfoundland, and Plymouth, England, on a map","Timeline: Sir Humphrey Gilbert claims Newfoundland for England (August 5, 1583)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 11","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00470"}],"questions":["What did Sir Humphrey hold in his hands to show he was taking the land?","Why were so many ships from different countries at St. John''s?","What did Sir Humphrey call out from the little Squirrel during the storm?"],"answers":["A hazel twig and a piece of sod","They came every summer to fish","That we are as near to heaven by sea as by land"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-12","title":"Chapter 12: About Sir Walter Raleigh''s Adventures in the Golden West","steps":["Read chapter 12 aloud together; listen for the word carved on the post","Oral narration: tell the story of the Lost Colony of Roanoke","Find Roanoke Island, Chesapeake Bay and North Carolina on a map","Timeline: Virginia Dare is born on Roanoke Island (August 18, 1587)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 12","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00498"}],"questions":["Who was the land of Virginia named for?","What two things did Lane''s colonists bring back to England?","What word did John White find carved on a post?"],"answers":["Queen Elizabeth, the ''Virgin Queen''","Tobacco and potatoes","Croatoan"],"notes":"The book calls the Native people ''cruel and revengeful'' while also showing the English burning their cornfields over a stolen cup. Talk about how the Roanoke and Croatoan people experienced these visits."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u2","title":"Part II: Stories of Virginia","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-1","title":"Chapter 13: The Adventures of Captain John Smith","steps":["Read chapter 13 aloud together; listen for how Smith used his compass","Oral narration: tell how the Jamestown settlers lived their first summer and how Smith was captured","Find Chesapeake Bay, Cape Henry, the James River and the Chickahominy River on a map","Timeline: Jamestown is founded (1607)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 13","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00554"}],"questions":["What were the names of the three ships?","Why was Jamestown short of food so soon?","Who saved John Smith''s life, according to his story?"],"answers":["Susan Constant, Godspeed and Discovery","Most men were gentlemen who would not work, and they could not hunt or farm well","Pocahontas"],"notes":"The book calls the Powhatan people ''savages'' and ''wily.'' Historians question whether the Pocahontas rescue happened the way Smith told it; he wrote it years later, and it may have been a ceremony he did not understand."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-2","title":"Chapter 14: More Adventures of Captain John Smith","steps":["Read chapter 14 aloud together; listen for what kind of workers Smith asked England to send","Oral narration: tell about the crowning of Powhatan and how Pocahontas warned Smith","Find Jamestown, Werowocomoco and the Bermudas on a map","Timeline: The Powhatan is ''crowned'' by order of King James (1608)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 14","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00605"}],"questions":["Why would the Powhatan not kneel to be crowned?","What rule did Smith make for the lazy settlers?","Why did Smith have to leave Virginia?"],"answers":["He had never seen a crown and did not see why a king should kneel","He who will not work shall not eat","He was badly hurt when a bag of gunpowder exploded"],"notes":"The Powhatan is called ''wily'' and ''savage,'' and his wish to see the English leave his land is treated as treachery. Ask: whose land was it, and why might he want the newcomers gone?"},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-3","title":"Chapter 15: How the Colony was Saved","steps":["Read chapter 15 aloud together; listen for the names of the two ships built on Bermuda","Oral narration: tell how the colony was almost abandoned and how it was saved","Find the Bermudas, the James River and the Appomattox River on a map","Timeline: Lord Delaware arrives and saves Jamestown (1610)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 15","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00662"}],"questions":["What was the terrible winter at Jamestown called?","How did Sir Thomas Dale get the settlers to work harder?","What did the chief receive for helping Argall capture Pocahontas?"],"answers":["The Starving Time","He gave each man three acres of his own land to farm","A copper kettle"],"notes":"The book treats the kidnapping of Pocahontas as a clever plan and calls the Powhatan people ''wily Redmen.'' Mention that she was taken as a hostage against her will. The Starving Time includes a brief mention of cannibalism."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-4","title":"Chapter 16: How Pocahontas Took a Journey Over the Seas","steps":["Read chapter 16 aloud together; listen for how the Indian counselor tried to count the English","Oral narration: tell about Pocahontas''s visit to England","Find London, Plymouth and Gravesend in England on a map","Timeline: Pocahontas dies at Gravesend (1617)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 16","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00732"}],"questions":["What crop made the Virginia farmers money?","What did King James think of smoking?","Where did Pocahontas die?"],"answers":["Tobacco","He hated it and wrote a book against it","Gravesend, England"],"notes":"Pocahontas (Matoaka) is called a ''half naked savage'' and ''heathen'' here. Point out that she was a real young woman far from home, and that she died very young."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-5","title":"Chapter 17: How the Redmen Fought Against Their White Brothers","steps":["Read chapter 17 aloud together; listen for the three important things that happened in 1619","Oral narration: tell about the first House of Burgesses","Find Jamestown and Richmond, Virginia, on a map","Timeline: First House of Burgesses meets; first enslaved Africans arrive in Virginia (1619)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 17","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00767"}],"questions":["What was Virginia''s first parliament called?","Where did it meet?","Who warned his master and saved many settlers in 1622?"],"answers":["The House of Burgesses","In the little wooden church at Jamestown","Chanco"],"notes":"The 1622 attack is told only from the settlers'' side, with the Powhatan as ''treacherous''; the brutal English revenge is described too. The book rightly calls the arrival of enslaved Africans in 1619 the sad beginning of slavery; a good place to stop and talk."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-6","title":"Chapter 18: How Englishmen Fought a Duel With Tyranny","steps":["Read chapter 18 aloud together; listen for what the company kept shouting at the meeting","Oral narration: tell how the company members stood up to King James","Find London and Virginia on a map","Timeline: Virginia becomes a royal (crown) colony (1624)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 18","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00786"}],"questions":["Why was Sir Walter Raleigh put to death?","Whom did King James not want the company to choose as treasurer?","Whom did the company elect instead?"],"answers":["The King of Spain demanded it after Raleigh failed to find a gold mine","Sir Edwin Sandys","The Earl of Southampton"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-7","title":"Chapter 19: The Coming of the Cavaliers","steps":["Read chapter 19 aloud together; listen for why Virginia was called the Old Dominion","Oral narration: tell how Virginia took the King''s side and what happened when Cromwell''s ships came","Find Jamestown, Virginia, and England on a map","Timeline: Cavaliers flee to Virginia after King Charles I is beheaded (1649)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 19","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00806"}],"questions":["What did Governor Berkeley think of free schools and printing?","Who were the Cavaliers?","What did the Navigation Law say?"],"answers":["He was glad Virginia had none and hoped it never would","Supporters of the King who fled England after Charles I was beheaded","Colonies could only ship goods in British ships and trade mainly with England"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-8","title":"Chapter 20: Bacon''s Rebellion","steps":["Read chapter 20 aloud together; listen for what Queen Pamunky kept crying out","Oral narration: tell the story of Bacon''s Rebellion from beginning to end","Find Jamestown and the Eastern Shore of Virginia (Accomac) on a map","Timeline: Bacon''s Rebellion; Jamestown is burned (1676)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 20","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00831"}],"questions":["Why were the settlers angry at Governor Berkeley at first?","What happened to Jamestown during the rebellion?","How did Bacon''s Rebellion come to an end?"],"answers":["He would not protect them or let them fight the Indians","Bacon burned it to the ground","Bacon died, and the rebellion fell apart"],"notes":"The book calls Native people ''Indian wolves'' (quoting Bacon) and celebrates the ''Battle of Bloody Run.'' It does show Native anger at losing their land, and Queen Cockacoeske of the Pamunkey grieving her husband; talk about her side."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-9","title":"Chapter 21: The Story of the Knights of the Golden Horseshoe","steps":["Read chapter 21 aloud together; listen for the Latin words on the golden horseshoes","Oral narration: tell about Spotswood''s journey over the mountains","Find the Blue Ridge Mountains, the Shenandoah Valley and Williamsburg on a map","Timeline: Spotswood''s Knights of the Golden Horseshoe cross the Blue Ridge (1716)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 21","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00903"}],"questions":["What did Spotswood want to name the Shenandoah River?","Why did he give his friends golden horseshoes?","Which town became the capital of Virginia after Jamestown?"],"answers":["The Euphrates","Their horses had been shod with iron for the trip, which was unusual in Virginia","Williamsburg"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u3","title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Pilgrims to Harvard)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-1","title":"Chapter 22: The Story of the Pilgrim Fathers","steps":["Read chapter 22 aloud together; listen for why the Pilgrims left Holland","Oral narration: tell the Pilgrims'' journey from Scrooby to Plymouth","Find Scrooby, Leyden (Leiden), Plymouth in England and Cape Cod on a map","Timeline: The Mayflower lands at Plymouth (1620)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 22","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00923"}],"questions":["Why did the Speedwell not cross the ocean?","Who taught the Pilgrims to plant corn?","What did the Governor send back to Canonicus in the snakeskin?"],"answers":["Her captain said she leaked too badly, so she was sent back","Squanto","Bullets and gunpowder"],"notes":"Native people are ''wild Redmen'' and ''savages,'' and the land is called empty. Squanto''s own village had just been wiped out by disease brought by Europeans; worth explaining why the coast seemed so empty."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-2","title":"Chapter 23: The Founding of Massachusetts","steps":["Read chapter 23 aloud together; listen for what made the Massachusetts charter different","Oral narration: tell how Boston was founded and why Roger Williams was sent away","Find Salem, Boston, the Charles River and Providence on a map","Timeline: John Winthrop''s Puritans found Boston (1630)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 23","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00977"}],"questions":["Who was the first governor of the Massachusetts Bay colony?","Who could vote in early Massachusetts?","What did Roger Williams say about the land in America?"],"answers":["John Winthrop","Only church members","It belonged to the Native people, so settlers should buy it from them"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-3","title":"Chapter 24: The Story of Harry Vane","steps":["Read chapter 24 aloud together; listen for how old Harry Vane was when he became governor","Oral narration: tell the story of young Governor Vane","Find Boston and London on a map","Timeline: Harry Vane is elected Governor of Massachusetts (1636)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 24","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00997"}],"questions":["Why did Harry Vane leave England for New England?","How did Vane show his grandness as Governor?","What did Winthrop, his rival, say about him later?"],"answers":["For conscience'' sake; he was a Puritan","He had soldiers march before him and made a royal tour","That he was a true friend to New England with a noble, generous mind"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-4","title":"Chapter 25: The Story of Anne Hutchinson and the Founding of Rhode Island","steps":["Read chapter 25 aloud together; listen for what Anne Hutchinson''s husband said about her","Oral narration: tell why Anne Hutchinson was sent away and what she founded","Find Portsmouth, Newport and Providence in Rhode Island on a map","Timeline: Anne Hutchinson is banished and settles in Rhode Island (1638)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 25","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01011"}],"questions":["Why did Anne Hutchinson start her meetings?","Which two towns did she and her friends found?","What made Rhode Island''s charter special?"],"answers":["Women were not allowed at the men''s meetings","Portsmouth and Newport","No one could be punished for their religious opinions"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-5","title":"Chapter 26: The Founding of Harvard","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how the college got its name","Ask your child to narrate the story of John Harvard and his gift","Find Cambridge and Charlestown, Massachusetts, near Boston on a map","Timeline: Massachusetts votes money to found a college (1636)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 26","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01022"}],"questions":["What did John Harvard leave to the new college when he died?","What was the town of Newtown renamed, and why?","What happened to John Harvard''s books about a hundred years later?"],"answers":["Half his money and all his books","Cambridge, after the English university many settlers came from","They burned in a library fire; only one book survived"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-6","title":"Chapter 27: How Quakers First Came to New England","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for what made the Quakers different","Ask your child to narrate how Boston treated the Quakers and how it finally changed","Find Boston and England on a map; trace the voyage the Quakers were sent back on","Timeline: First Quakers arrive in Boston (1656)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 27","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01033"}],"questions":["Name two customs that set the Quakers apart from other people.","Who was the old man who paid for food for the first two Quaker women?","Which king wrote to tell Massachusetts to stop persecuting the Quakers?"],"answers":["Any two: refused to fight, swear oaths, use titles, or take off their hats; said thee and thou","Nicholas Upshal","King Charles (Charles II)"],"notes":"This chapter includes harsh punishments and hangings; a good chance to talk about religious freedom and how the Puritans, who fled persecution, persecuted others."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-7","title":"Chapter 28: How Maine and New Hampshire Were Founded","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why these colonies were founded","Ask your child to narrate how Beacon Hill got its name","Find Maine, New Hampshire, and Boston on a map","Timeline: Mason and Gorges divide their land; New Hampshire is named (1629)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 28","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01058"}],"questions":["Who were the two men chiefly behind the founding of Maine and New Hampshire?","Why did Mason call his land New Hampshire?","Until what year was Maine part of Massachusetts?"],"answers":["Sir Ferdinando Gorges and Captain John Mason","After Hampshire, the English county he was fond of","1820"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u4","title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Connecticut to Salem)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-1","title":"Chapter 29: The Founding of Connecticut and War with the Indians","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Thomas Hooker left Massachusetts","Ask your child to narrate Hooker''s journey through the wilderness","Find Boston, Hartford, and the Connecticut River on a map","Timeline: Hooker''s congregation settles Hartford (1636); Pequot War (1637)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 29","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01076"}],"questions":["Why did Thomas Hooker and his people want to leave Massachusetts?","What town did they build on the Connecticut River?","How did the Fundamental Orders differ from the rules of Massachusetts?"],"answers":["He disliked rule by a few, and the land around Boston was crowded and poor","Hartford","A man no longer had to be a church member to vote (and the King was not mentioned)"],"notes":"The Pequots are called \"savages\" and \"wily,\" and the burning of the Pequot fort at Mystic, where hundreds of people including women and children died, is told as just vengeance. Talk about how the Pequots might tell this story."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-2","title":"Chapter 30: The Founding of New Haven","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how New Haven was governed","Ask your child to narrate how the New Haven settlers got their land","Find New Haven, Connecticut, on a map along Long Island Sound","Timeline: New Haven founded (1638); United Colonies of New England formed (1643)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 30","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01112"}],"questions":["Who founded New Haven?","Why were no juries allowed in New Haven?","Which colony was not invited to join the United Colonies of New England?"],"answers":["John Davenport","Juries are not mentioned in the Bible","Rhode Island"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-3","title":"Chapter 31: The Hunt for the Regicides","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the hiding places Goffe and Whalley used","Ask your child to narrate how the people of New Haven and Guilford kept the regicides safe","Find New Haven and Guilford, Connecticut, and Hadley, Massachusetts, on a map","Timeline: Charles II restored; regicides Whalley and Goffe flee to New England (1660)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 31","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01123"}],"questions":["Why were Whalley and Goffe hunted by the King''s messengers?","Why couldn''t the Royalist messengers ride on to New Haven on Sunday?","Where did the two men hide during their last years?"],"answers":["They had helped condemn King Charles I to death","Puritan law forbade riding on Sunday except to church","In a secret room in minister John Russell''s house in Hadley"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-4","title":"Chapter 32: King Philip''s War","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Philip turned to war","Ask your child to narrate the mysterious stranger who saved Hadley","Find Plymouth, Swansea, and Hadley, Massachusetts, and Rhode Island on a map","Timeline: King Philip''s War begins (1675)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 32","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01145"}],"questions":["What was King Philip''s real name, and who was his father?","Who was the strange leader who appeared at Hadley?","How did King Philip die?"],"answers":["Metacomet; his father was Massasoit","Colonel Goffe, the hidden regicide","He was killed by one of his own followers"],"notes":"The Wampanoag and their allies are described as \"savages\" and compared to wild animals, and the war is told only from the colonists'' side. Discuss Philip''s real grievance: settlers taking more and more of his people''s land."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-5","title":"Chapter 33: How The Charter of Connecticut Was Saved","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for what happened when the candles went out","Ask your child to narrate the story of the Charter Oak","Find Hartford, Connecticut, and Boston on a map","Timeline: Andros takes over Connecticut''s government; Charter Oak legend (1687)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 33","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01171"}],"questions":["Who was the royal Governor sent to rule all of New England?","Where was the Connecticut charter said to be hidden?","How did the sentry recognize Andros when he tried to escape in woman''s clothes?"],"answers":["Sir Edmund Andros","In a hollow oak tree, the Charter Oak","By his boots"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-6","title":"Chapter 34: The Witches of Salem","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how the accusations started","Ask your child to narrate how the people of Salem came back to their senses and what the judge and jury did afterward","Find Salem, Massachusetts, on a map","Timeline: Salem witch trials (1692)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 34","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01201"}],"questions":["Whose daughter and niece first began acting strangely in Salem village?","How many people were put to death by hanging?","What did Judge Sewall do to show he was sorry?"],"answers":["The minister Samuel Parris","Twenty","He confessed his guilt before the congregation and kept a yearly day of fasting and prayer"],"notes":"The servant first accused (Tituba) was an enslaved woman, likely of Indigenous Caribbean or South American origin, which the book does not mention. The chapter also links old age and ugliness with suspicion; worth a gentle discussion."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u5","title":"Part IV: Stories of the Middle and Southern Colonies","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-1","title":"Chapter 35: The Founding of Maryland","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Lord Baltimore wanted a colony","Ask your child to narrate the landing of the Ark and the Dove","Find Chesapeake Bay, the Potomac River, and the Isle of Kent on a map","Timeline: Maryland Toleration Act (1649)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 35","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01222"}],"questions":["What two ships carried the first Maryland colonists?","What did Lord Baltimore have to give the King each year for Maryland?","What did the Toleration Act promise?"],"answers":["The Ark and the Dove","Two Indian arrows (plus a fifth of any gold or silver found)","That Christians would not be troubled for their religion"],"notes":"The book says King James granted Maryland in 1632; it was actually King Charles I, whose wife Henrietta Maria gave the colony its name."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-2","title":"Chapter 36: How New Amsterdam Became New York","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for what kind of man Peter Stuyvesant was","Ask your child to narrate how New Amsterdam became New York without a battle","Find the Hudson River, Manhattan Island, Albany, and the Delaware River on a map","Timeline: The English take New Amsterdam and rename it New York (1664)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 36","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01245"}],"questions":["Who sailed up the river that now bears his name, and for which country?","What was special about Peter Stuyvesant''s wooden leg?","Why did Stuyvesant finally surrender the town?"],"answers":["Henry Hudson, sailing for the Dutch","It was bound with silver","Everyone, even his own son, begged him to; the fort had little powder, food, or water"],"notes":"The book repeats the story that Manhattan was bought for trinkets; the Lenape likely understood the deal as sharing use of the land, not giving it up forever."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-3","title":"Chapter 37: How a German Ruled New York","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why people feared Nicholson","Ask your child to narrate Leisler''s rise and fall","Find New York City and Germany on a map","Timeline: Leisler takes control of New York (1689)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 37","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01296"}],"questions":["Why did Jacob Leisler refuse to pay a tax?","What did Leisler demand before he would let Ingoldsby''s soldiers into the fort?","How did people feel about Leisler after he was hanged?"],"answers":["The tax collector was a Catholic (\"Papist\")","Written orders from the King or the Governor","Many were sorry and saw him as a martyr who had meant well"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-4","title":"Chapter 38: Pirates!","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the difference between a privateer and a pirate","Ask your child to narrate how Captain Kidd went from pirate hunter to pirate","Find New York, Boston, Long Island Sound, the West Indies, and Madagascar on a map","Timeline: Captain Kidd hanged as a pirate (1701)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 38","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01318"}],"questions":["What was a letter of marque?","What was the name of Captain Kidd''s first ship?","Why did people search for treasure along Long Island Sound for years afterward?"],"answers":["A paper allowing a private ship to attack enemy ships in wartime","The Adventure","Little treasure was found on Kidd''s sloop, so people thought he had buried it"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-5","title":"Chapter 39: The Founding of New Jersey","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how New Jersey got its name","Ask your child to narrate how Philip Carteret arrived and why the settlers rebelled","Find the Isle of Jersey in the English Channel and Elizabeth, New Jersey, on a map","Timeline: Philip Carteret lands and founds Elizabeth (1665)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 39","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01340"}],"questions":["Why was the colony named New Jersey?","What did Philip Carteret carry on his shoulder when he landed, and why?","Into which two parts was New Jersey divided?"],"answers":["To honor Sir George Carteret, who had defended the Isle of Jersey for the King","A hoe, to show he would live and work among the settlers","East Jersey and West Jersey"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-6","title":"Chapter 40: The Founding of Pennsylvania","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how Pennsylvania got its name","Ask your child to narrate Penn''s meeting with the Lenape chiefs under the great elm","Find Philadelphia, the Delaware River, Delaware Bay, and the Mason-Dixon Line on a map","Timeline: William Penn receives his charter for Pennsylvania (1681)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 40","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01349"}],"questions":["How did William Penn come to own Pennsylvania?","What does the name Philadelphia mean?","What name did the Indians give Penn?"],"answers":["King Charles II gave him the land to pay a debt owed to Penn''s father","City of brotherly love","Onas"],"notes":"The treaty scene is told in a romantic way and calls Native people \"Redmen\" with \"wild dances.\" Penn''s fair dealing was real, but later Pennsylvania leaders broke its spirit (for example, the Walking Purchase of 1737)."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-7","title":"Chapter 41: How Benjamin Franklin Came to Philadelphia","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how young Ben got his writing printed","Ask your child to narrate Ben Franklin''s arrival in Philadelphia","Trace Franklin''s trip from Boston to New York to Philadelphia on a map","Timeline: Benjamin Franklin arrives in Philadelphia (1723)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 41","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01385"}],"questions":["How did Ben get his first article into his brother''s newspaper?","What did he buy at the bakery when he arrived in Philadelphia?","Name two things Franklin did for the city of Philadelphia."],"answers":["He disguised his handwriting and slipped it under the door at night","Three big rolls","Any two: a library, fire brigade, police force, the post office, a hospital, the University of Pennsylvania"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-8","title":"Chapter 42: The Founding of North and South Carolina","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the kinds of people who came to Carolina","Ask your child to narrate why the settlers rejected the Grand Model","Find North Carolina, South Carolina, and the Carolina coast on a map; compare latitudes 30 and 36 degrees","Timeline: Charles II grants Carolina to eight Lords Proprietors (1663)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 42","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01399"}],"questions":["Whom was Carolina named for by the French, and by the English?","Why did the Huguenots leave France?","Why did the Virginians call Carolina \"Rogues'' Harbour\"?"],"answers":["Charles IX of France; Charles II of England","Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes and forbade their worship","Many people came to escape debts they owed elsewhere"],"notes":"The book says white men could not endure rice-field work and that \"no one saw anything wrong\" with slavery; neither is accurate. Enslaved Africans were forced into the work, many brought rice-growing skill from West Africa, and some people did object at the time."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-9","title":"Chapter 43: War With the Indians in North and South Carolina","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why the Tuscaroras grew angry","Ask your child to narrate how Sanute warned his friend Fraser","Find Albemarle Sound, New Bern, Charleston, and St. Augustine on a map","Timeline: Tuscarora War begins (1711); Yamasee War (1715)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 43","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01415"}],"questions":["Why did the Tuscaroras come to hate the settlers?","Whom did the Tuscaroras join after their defeat, and what did the Five Nations become?","Who warned the Fraser family to flee?"],"answers":["They were being pushed farther and farther from their lands","The Five Nations in New York, which became the Six Nations","The chief Sanute"],"notes":"Native people are called \"silent and crafty\" and \"raging demons,\" and only the settlers'' losses are described; many captured Tuscaroras were sold into slavery. The book also says King William made South Carolina royal in 1719; the king then was George I."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-10","title":"Chapter 44: The Founding of Georgia","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Oglethorpe wanted a new colony","Ask your child to narrate how Oglethorpe tricked the Spaniards with a letter","Find the Savannah and Altamaha rivers, Savannah, St. Simons Island, and St. Augustine on a map","Timeline: Oglethorpe''s colonists found Savannah, Georgia (1733)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 44","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01442"}],"questions":["What kind of people did Oglethorpe first want to help with his colony?","Which two things were forbidden in early Georgia?","Who was the old Creek chief who welcomed the settlers?"],"answers":["Poor debtors who had been in prison (and people persecuted for religion)","Slavery and rum","Tomo-chi-chi"],"notes":"The chapter uses the word \"squaw,\" now considered offensive, and quotes a Creek speaker saying the Great Spirit gave white people more wisdom; that speech comes from English records. Georgia''s ban on slavery ended in 1749."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u6","title":"Part V: Stories of the French in America","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-1","title":"Chapter 45: How the Mississippi was Discovered","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the troubles that kept stopping La Salle","Ask your child to narrate La Salle''s thousand-mile walk and how he finally reached the sea","Trace the Mississippi River from the Great Lakes to the Gulf of Mexico; find the Missouri, Arkansas, and Illinois rivers","Timeline: La Salle reaches the mouth of the Mississippi and claims it for France (1682)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 45","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01486"}],"questions":["Who were the first Frenchmen to travel down the Mississippi?","Why did the Indians call Tonty \"Iron Hand\"?","What happened to La Salle''s ship, the Griffin?"],"answers":["Marquette and Joliet (1673)","He had an iron hand, hidden under a glove, from a war wound","It sailed away with furs and was never heard of again"],"notes":"Native people are called \"almost as wild\" as animals and \"wondering savages.\" Also, only Marquette was a priest; Joliet was a fur trader and mapmaker."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-2","title":"Chapter 46: King William''s War and Queen Anne''s War","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how Frontenac treated the English messenger","Ask your child to narrate Frontenac''s answer to Phips","Find Quebec, the St. Lawrence River, Schenectady, and Nova Scotia on a map","Timeline: Phips''s attack on Quebec fails (1690); Treaty of Utrecht (1713)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 46","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01519"}],"questions":["Why did King Louis want Frontenac to capture New York?","How did Frontenac reply to the demand to surrender Quebec?","What lands did Britain gain at the Treaty of Utrecht?"],"answers":["To reach the Hudson and get a port that did not freeze in winter","He would answer only with the mouths of his cannon","Nova Scotia, Newfoundland, and the Hudson Bay Territory"],"notes":"Indian allies of both sides are mentioned mainly in raids and massacres; discuss that Native nations had their own reasons for choosing sides in these European wars."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-3","title":"Chapter 47: The Mississippi Bubble","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the tall tales told about Louisiana","Ask your child to narrate how the Mississippi Bubble grew and then burst","Find New Orleans, the mouth of the Mississippi, and Cape Breton Island (Louisbourg) on a map","Timeline: Bienville founds New Orleans (1718); New Englanders capture Louisbourg (1745)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 47","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01557"}],"questions":["How did Bienville get the English ship to turn back on the Mississippi?","What did John Law tell the French people about Louisiana?","Why were the New Englanders angry after the Peace of Aix-la-Chapelle?"],"answers":["He told the captain the river belonged to France and was not the Mississippi","That it had endless gold and silver and was an earthly paradise","Louisbourg, which they had captured, was given back to France"],"notes":"The book says John Law fled France in 1790; the correct year is 1720."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-4","title":"Chapter 48: How a Terrible Disaster Befell the British Army","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Braddock would not take advice","Ask your child to narrate the ambush near the Monongahela","Find the forks of the Ohio (Pittsburgh), the Monongahela River, Williamsburg, and Albany on a map","Timeline: Braddock''s defeat near Fort Duquesne (1755)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 48","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01584"}],"questions":["Why was young George Washington sent to the French fort?","Why did Braddock''s redcoats lose so badly?","How did Washington''s clothes show how close he came to danger?"],"answers":["To tell the French they were on British land and must leave","They stood in tight lines in bright coats while hidden enemies fired from the trees","They were riddled with bullet holes, and two horses were shot under him"],"notes":"Native people are called \"savages\" throughout, and the Indian-hunter Captain Jack is admired. A good chance to notice that Braddock''s own contempt for Native allies helped cause his defeat."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-5","title":"Chapter 49: The End of the French Rule in America","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how Wolfe''s men got past the sentries","Ask your child to narrate the climb up the cliffs and the battle on the Plains of Abraham","Find Quebec, the St. Lawrence River, Montreal, and Pittsburgh on a map","Timeline: Battle of the Plains of Abraham; Quebec falls (1759)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 49","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01634"}],"questions":["What new name was Fort Duquesne given, and in whose honor?","How did the Highland officer trick the French sentries?","What did the Treaty of Paris of 1763 do?"],"answers":["Pittsburgh, for William Pitt","He answered in French that the boats were French provision boats","Gave Britain nearly all of France''s lands in North America (Spain got Louisiana)"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-6","title":"Chapter 50: The Rebellion of Pontiac","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why the Indians preferred the French","Ask your child to narrate how Major Gladwin learned of Pontiac''s plan","Find Detroit, Michilimackinac (Mackinac), Fort Pitt, and Fort Niagara on a map","Timeline: Pontiac''s War begins (1763)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 50","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01689"}],"questions":["Why did the Indians become unhappy after the British took Canada?","Who was Pontiac?","How did the warriors get into the fort at Michilimackinac?"],"answers":["The British gave no gifts, looked down on them, and cleared their hunting lands","Chief of the Ottawas and leader of the uprising","They chased a ball game toward the open gate and grabbed hidden weapons"],"notes":"Pontiac''s side is called \"treachery\" and the chiefs \"hideously painted,\" and \"squaw\" is now considered offensive. The chapter does admit British contempt and land-clearing caused the war; discuss Pontiac as a leader defending his people''s lands."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u7","title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Tea Party to Saratoga)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-1","title":"Chapter 51: The Boston Tea party","steps":["Read chapter 51 aloud together. Listen for the two taxes Britain tried to put on the colonies.","Oral narration: Narrate how Boston answered the tea ships.","On a map, find Boston and its harbor; Salem.","Add to your timeline: Boston Tea Party (1773)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 51","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01735"}],"questions":["What was the colonists'' cry against the Stamp Act?","Which tax did King George keep after removing the others?","How did the King punish Boston after the Tea Party?"],"answers":["No taxation without representation","The tax on tea","He closed its port and moved the capital to Salem"],"notes":"The tea raiders'' disguise as \"Red Indians\" is described in stereotyped terms; a good moment to talk about why costumes of real peoples can be hurtful."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-2","title":"Chapter 52: Paul Revere''s Ride - The Unsheathing of the Sword","steps":["Read chapter 52 aloud together. Listen for the signal Paul Revere waited for.","Oral narration: Narrate Paul Revere''s ride from start to finish.","On a map, find Boston, Lexington, and Concord, Massachusetts.","Add to your timeline: Battles of Lexington and Concord (1775)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 52","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01763"}],"questions":["Where did the signal lights for Paul Revere appear?","Which two patriot leaders were the British sent to arrest?","What were ''minute men''?"],"answers":["On the tower of the Old North Church","Samuel Adams and John Hancock","Men ready to fight at a minute''s notice"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-3","title":"Chapter 53: The First Thrust-The Battle of Bunker Hill","steps":["Read chapter 53 aloud together. Listen for why the battle has the ''wrong'' name.","Oral narration: Narrate Ethan Allen''s capture of Fort Ticonderoga.","On a map, find Fort Ticonderoga and Lake Champlain; Breed''s Hill near Boston.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Bunker Hill (1775)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 53","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01796"}],"questions":["On which hill was the Battle of Bunker Hill really fought?","Why did the Americans finally leave the hill?","Who did Congress choose as commander-in-chief?"],"answers":["Breed''s Hill","They ran out of ammunition","George Washington"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-4","title":"Chapter 54: The War In Canada","steps":["Read chapter 54 aloud together. Listen for the hardships of Arnold''s march through Maine.","Oral narration: Narrate the attack on Quebec in the snowstorm.","On a map, find Maine, the St. Lawrence River, Quebec, and Lake Champlain.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Valcour Island on Lake Champlain (1776)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 54","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01837"}],"questions":["Which American general was killed in the attack on Quebec?","How did Captain Douglas get his ship to Quebec through the ice?","How did Arnold''s fleet escape the British the first night?"],"answers":["General Richard Montgomery","He rammed through the ice at full speed","They slipped through the British line in the dark and fog"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-5","title":"Chapter 55: The Birth of A Great Nation","steps":["Read chapter 55 aloud together. Listen for how Washington got cannon to Boston.","Oral narration: Narrate how the British were forced out of Boston.","On a map, find Dorchester Heights, Boston; Philadelphia.","Add to your timeline: Declaration of Independence (1776)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 55","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01855"}],"questions":["Who dragged the captured cannon to Boston?","Who wrote the Declaration of Independence?","Who signed the Declaration first?"],"answers":["Colonel Henry Knox","Thomas Jefferson","John Hancock"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-6","title":"Chapter 56: The Darkest Hour - Trenton and Princeton","steps":["Read chapter 56 aloud together. Listen for how the weather helped the Americans more than once.","Oral narration: Narrate the crossing of the Delaware and the surprise at Trenton.","On a map, find Long Island, Manhattan, the Delaware River, Trenton and Princeton.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Trenton (1776)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 56","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01871"}],"questions":["What hid the Americans'' retreat from Long Island?","What was Washington''s password the night he crossed the Delaware?","Who were the hired German soldiers at Trenton called?"],"answers":["A thick fog","Victory or death","Hessians"],"notes":"The book misprints the year of the Battle of Trenton. It was December 26, 1776."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-7","title":"Chapter 57: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bennington and Oriskany","steps":["Read chapter 57 aloud together. Listen for how the first American flag was made at Fort Stanwix.","Oral narration: Narrate General Herkimer''s stand at Oriskany.","On a map, find Ticonderoga, Bennington (Vermont), and Fort Stanwix in New York.","Add to your timeline: Battles of Bennington and Oriskany (1777)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 57","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01907"}],"questions":["What was the British plan for the Hudson-St. Lawrence waterway?","How did Schuyler slow Burgoyne''s army?","What did Herkimer do after his leg was shattered?"],"answers":["To take it and cut the colonies in two","He blocked the roads with felled trees and burned bridges","He sat against a tree on his saddle and kept giving orders"],"notes":"Oriskany is framed as ''savage'' warfare by Brant''s men; note that the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) were divided, with Oneida warriors fighting on the American side. Ticonderoga fell to Burgoyne in July 1777, not August as the book says."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-8","title":"Chapter 58: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bemis Heights and Saratoga","steps":["Read chapter 58 aloud together. Listen for Arnold''s trick to scatter the enemy at Fort Stanwix.","Oral narration: Narrate Arnold''s charge at the second battle.","On a map, find Saratoga and Bemis Heights on the Hudson River.","Add to your timeline: Burgoyne surrenders at Saratoga (1777)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 58","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01930"}],"questions":["Who commanded the Americans at Saratoga instead of Schuyler?","Which leg was Arnold wounded in at Saratoga?","About how many men surrendered with Burgoyne?"],"answers":["General Horatio Gates","The same leg wounded at Quebec","Nearly six thousand"],"notes":"The Fort Stanwix story uses dated words (''redskin'', ''fire water'') and mocks both a mentally disabled man and Native allies; worth pausing to discuss."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u8","title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Valley Forge to Yorktown)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-1","title":"Chapter 59: Brandywine - Germantown - Valley Forge","steps":["Read chapter 59 aloud together. Listen for why Washington nearly resigned.","Oral narration: Narrate what the winter at Valley Forge was like.","On a map, find Brandywine Creek, Germantown, Philadelphia, and Valley Forge in Pennsylvania.","Add to your timeline: Winter at Valley Forge (1777-1778)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 59","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01966"}],"questions":["Which city did Howe capture?","Who drilled the army at Valley Forge?","What made the Battle of Germantown confusing?"],"answers":["Philadelphia","Baron von Steuben","A thick fog, so Americans fired on each other"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-2","title":"Chapter 60: War on the Sea","steps":["Read chapter 60 aloud together. Listen for John Paul Jones''s famous answer.","Oral narration: Narrate the fight between the Bonhomme Richard and the Serapis.","On a map, find Narragansett Bay (Rhode Island); the North Sea off England.","Add to your timeline: Bonhomme Richard defeats the Serapis (1779)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 60","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01986"}],"questions":["Where was John Paul Jones born?","Who was the ship Bonhomme Richard named to honor?","What did Jones answer when asked if he had struck his flag?"],"answers":["Scotland","Benjamin Franklin (and France)","\"I haven''t begun to fight yet\""]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-3","title":"Chapter 61: The Battle of Monmouth - The Story of Captain Molly","steps":["Read chapter 61 aloud together. Listen for why Washington was so angry with General Lee.","Oral narration: Narrate Captain Molly''s deeds at Monmouth.","On a map, find Philadelphia, Monmouth (New Jersey), and New York.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Monmouth (1778)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 61","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02037"}],"questions":["Why did Clinton leave Philadelphia?","What did Captain Molly do when her husband fell?","What reward did Washington give her?"],"answers":["He thought holding it was worthless and moved his army to New York","She fired his cannon herself all day","He made her a sergeant"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-4","title":"Chapter 62: The Story of a Great Crime","steps":["Read chapter 62 aloud together. Listen for where Major Andre hid the papers.","Oral narration: Narrate how Andre was caught.","On a map, find West Point on the Hudson River.","Add to your timeline: Benedict Arnold''s treason discovered (1780)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 62","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02066"}],"questions":["Which fort did Arnold plan to hand to the British?","Where did the three Americans find the secret papers?","How did Arnold escape?"],"answers":["West Point","In Andre''s boots","He rode off and fled to the British ship Vulture"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-5","title":"Chapter 63: A Turning Point in the World''s History","steps":["Read chapter 63 aloud together. Listen for the tune the British band played at the surrender.","Oral narration: Narrate how Cornwallis was trapped at Yorktown.","On a map, find Yorktown and Chesapeake Bay, Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Cornwallis surrenders at Yorktown (1781)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 63","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02098"}],"questions":["Which country''s fleet and soldiers helped at Yorktown?","Why couldn''t Cornwallis escape by sea?","What became the western boundary of the United States in the peace?"],"answers":["France","The French fleet had beaten the British fleet at Chesapeake Bay","The Mississippi River"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u9","title":"Part VII: The Young Nation (Washington to Jackson)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-1","title":"Chapter 64: Washington First In War, First In Peace","steps":["Read chapter 64 aloud together. Listen for how many states had to accept the Constitution.","Oral narration: Narrate Washington''s first inauguration.","On a map, find Philadelphia, New York City, Vermont, Kentucky, and the Potomac River.","Add to your timeline: Washington becomes first President (1789)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 64","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02114"}],"questions":["Which state was first to accept the Constitution?","Who straightened out the nation''s money matters?","Which treaty with Britain made people angry at Washington?"],"answers":["Delaware","Alexander Hamilton","The Jay Treaty"],"notes":"Kentucky settlement is told only from the settlers'' side, and enslaved people carried off in the war appear only as property owed; worth a short discussion."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-2","title":"Chapter 65: Adams - How He Kept Peace with France","steps":["Read chapter 65 aloud together. Listen for the nicknames given to the new capital.","Oral narration: Narrate how Adams kept peace with France.","On a map, find France; Tennessee; Washington, D.C..","Add to your timeline: Government moves to Washington, D.C. (1800)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 65","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02183"}],"questions":["What song was written during the trouble with France?","What did Adams want written on his tomb?","What was Tennessee called when it was a separate republic?"],"answers":["Hail Columbia","That he took responsibility for peace with France","Franklin"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-3","title":"Chapter 66: Jefferson - How the Territory of the United States was Doubled","steps":["Read chapter 66 aloud together. Listen for why the Mississippi mattered so much to western farmers.","Oral narration: Narrate how the Louisiana Purchase happened.","On a map, find The Ohio River, Marietta, Cleveland, New Orleans, and the Louisiana Territory.","Add to your timeline: Louisiana Purchase (1803)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 66","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02200"}],"questions":["How did Jefferson arrive at his inauguration?","Why did Napoleon sell Louisiana?","What price was agreed?"],"answers":["He rode alone and tied up his horse himself","He needed money for his war with Britain","Fifteen million dollars"],"notes":"The Ohio wars are told only from the settlers'' side, with dated terms like ''Redmen''; the nations there were defending their homelands. (The book''s ''1893'' for Ohio is a misprint for 1803.)"},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-4","title":"Chapter 67: Jefferson - How the Door Into the Far West was Opened","steps":["Read chapter 67 aloud together. Listen for Lewis''s narrow escape from the grizzly bear.","Oral narration: Narrate one adventure of the journey west.","On a map, find The Missouri River, Fort Mandan (North Dakota), the Rocky Mountains, and the Columbia River.","Add to your timeline: Lewis and Clark Expedition (1804-1806)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 67","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02226"}],"questions":["Which river did the explorers follow from St. Louis?","Where did they spend their first winter?","Which river carried them to the Pacific?"],"answers":["The Missouri","Fort Mandan","The Columbia"],"notes":"Native peoples are called ''Redskins'' and said to understand nothing; the chapter leaves out Sacagawea and the many nations who guided and fed the party."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-5","title":"Chapter 68: Jefferson - About An American Who Wanted to be a King","steps":["Read chapter 68 aloud together. Listen for what title Burr hoped to take.","Oral narration: Narrate how Perkins caught and brought Burr in.","On a map, find New Orleans, the Mississippi River, and Richmond, Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Burr-Hamilton duel (1804)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 68","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02261"}],"questions":["Whom did Aaron Burr kill in a duel?","What did Burr want to make of the West?","Was Burr found guilty of treason?"],"answers":["Alexander Hamilton","An empire with himself as emperor","No, he was set free"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-6","title":"Chapter 69: Madison - The Shooting Star and the Prophet","steps":["Read chapter 69 aloud together. Listen for what the Prophet taught his people.","Oral narration: Narrate the Battle of Tippecanoe.","On a map, find Indiana and Tippecanoe.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Tippecanoe (1811)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 69","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02298"}],"questions":["What was Tecumseh''s great goal?","Who was the Prophet?","Why was Tecumseh angry with the Prophet after the battle?"],"answers":["To unite all the Indian nations and stop white settlement","Tecumseh''s brother, Tenskwatawa","He fought too soon and lost, ruining Tecumseh''s plans"],"notes":"The chapter shows Tecumseh''s cause with some sympathy but also speaks of Indian ''treachery''; talk about Harrison''s 1809 land treaty from the Shawnee point of view."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-7","title":"Chapter 70: Madison - War with Great Britain","steps":["Read chapter 70 aloud together. Listen for how the White House may have got its name.","Oral narration: Narrate how ''The Star-Spangled Banner'' was written.","On a map, find Lake Erie, the Thames River (Ontario), Washington, D.C., and Baltimore.","Add to your timeline: Star-Spangled Banner written at Fort McHenry (1814)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 70","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02321"}],"questions":["What nickname did the ship Constitution earn?","What was Perry''s message after Lake Erie?","Which treaty ended the War of 1812?"],"answers":["Old Ironsides","\"We have met the enemy, and they are ours\"","The Treaty of Ghent"],"notes":"Native warriors are called ''savage'' and ''fiery Redmen''; Tecumseh is shown protecting prisoners, which is a good counterpoint to discuss."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-8","title":"Chapter 71: Monroe-The First Whispers of a Storm-Monroe''s Famous Doctrine","steps":["Read chapter 71 aloud together. Listen for what the Missouri Compromise decided.","Oral narration: Narrate the Monroe Doctrine in your own words.","On a map, find Florida, Missouri, Maine, Mississippi, Illinois, and Alabama.","Add to your timeline: Missouri Compromise (1820)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 71","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02365"}],"questions":["Which country sold Florida to the United States?","Which state came in as a free state to balance Missouri?","What did the Monroe Doctrine tell Europe?"],"answers":["Spain","Maine","Not to start new colonies or reconquer free nations in the Americas"],"notes":"The Seminole war is told only from the American side, and the claim that white men could not work in southern heat is an old excuse for slavery, not a fact."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-9","title":"Chapter 72: Adams - The Tariff of Abominations","steps":["Read chapter 72 aloud together. Listen for why the North and South disagreed about tariffs.","Oral narration: Narrate what a tariff is and why the South disliked it.","On a map, find The industrial North and the cotton South (point to regions).","Add to your timeline: Tariff of Abominations (1828)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 72","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02394"}],"questions":["Whose son was John Quincy Adams?","What new inventions did Adams want built?","Why did the North want high tariffs?"],"answers":["John Adams, the second President","Canals, railroads, and bridges","To protect its factories from foreign goods"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-10","title":"Chapter 73: Jackson - \"Liberty and Union, Now and Forever\" - Van Buren - Hard Times","steps":["Read chapter 73 aloud together. Listen for Jackson''s famous toast.","Oral narration: Narrate Webster''s ''Liberty and Union'' speech or the nullification crisis.","On a map, find South Carolina, Arkansas, and Michigan.","Add to your timeline: South Carolina''s nullification crisis (1832)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 73","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02406"}],"questions":["Why was Jackson called Old Hickory?","What was the ''spoils system''?","What hard times began under Van Buren?"],"answers":["For his tough, unbending will","Firing government workers to give their jobs to the President''s friends","Banks and businesses failed and food grew dear"],"notes":"The chapter never mentions Indian Removal, a major act of Jackson''s presidency (the Trail of Tears followed in 1838); consider adding it."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u10","title":"Part VII: Growth and Division (Harrison to Buchanan)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-1","title":"Chapter 74: Harrison - The Hero of Tippecanoe","steps":["Read chapter 74 aloud together. Listen for the campaign slogans.","Oral narration: Narrate the Log Cabin and Hard Cider campaign.","On a map, find Indiana (Tippecanoe) and Washington, D.C..","Add to your timeline: President Harrison dies after one month (1841)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 74","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02452"}],"questions":["What slogan did Harrison''s supporters use?","What did Harrison refuse to wear at his inauguration?","How long was Harrison President?"],"answers":["Log Cabin and Hard Cider (also ''Tippecanoe and Tyler too'')","An overcoat","About one month"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-2","title":"Chapter 75: Tyler - Florida Becomes a State","steps":["Read chapter 75 aloud together. Listen for who led the Seminoles.","Oral narration: Narrate how the Maine boundary quarrel was settled.","On a map, find Maine, Florida, and the lands west of the Mississippi.","Add to your timeline: Florida becomes a state (1845)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 75","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02469"}],"questions":["Who became President when Harrison died?","Who settled the Maine boundary for the United States?","How long did the war with the Seminoles last?"],"answers":["John Tyler","Daniel Webster","Seven years"],"notes":"Seminole removal is presented approvingly; the Seminoles resisted being forced from their homes, and many escaped slaves lived among them."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-3","title":"Chapter 76: Polk - How Much Land Was Added to the United States","steps":["Read chapter 76 aloud together. Listen for three ways the country grew: Texas, Oregon, and the lands won from Mexico.","Narrate how Santa Anna was captured after San Jacinto (oral narration).","On a map, find Texas, the Oregon Country, the 49th parallel, the Rio Grande, and Mexico City.","Add to your timeline: Mexican War ends; U.S. gains the Southwest (1848)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 76","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02479"}],"questions":["Why was Texas called the Lone Star State?","What did the cry \"Fifty-four Forty or Fight\" mean?","Where was the Oregon boundary finally set?"],"answers":["Its flag as a republic had a single star","People wanted all of Oregon up to that line, or war with Britain","At the 49th parallel"],"notes":"Marshall tells the Texas story and Mexican War mostly from the American side (though it admits Polk wanted an excuse for war); a good chance to talk about how Mexicans and Tejanos saw these events."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-4","title":"Chapter 77: Polk - The Finding of Gold","steps":["Read chapter 77 aloud together. Listen for how the news of gold changed everyday life.","Narrate how Marshall tested the shining grains to see if they were gold (oral narration).","On a map, find the Sacramento Valley and the routes to California by land and sea.","Add to your timeline: Gold found at Sutter''s Mill (1848)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 77","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02527"}],"questions":["Who found the first gold, and where?","What were the big covered wagons called?","Did Sutter and Marshall get rich from the gold?"],"answers":["James Marshall, in the stream at Sutter''s sawmill","Prairie schooners","No, both died poor"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-5","title":"Chapter 78: Taylor - Union or Disunion","steps":["Read chapter 78 aloud together. Listen for the three great speeches in the Senate.","Narrate why the South did not want California to come in as a free state (oral narration).","On a map, find California and the Missouri Compromise line.","Add to your timeline: Laying the Washington Monument cornerstone; President Taylor dies (1850)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 78","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02560"}],"questions":["What nickname did Zachary Taylor''s soldiers give him?","Which old senator spoke for the South even though he was too ill to read his own speech?","How did President Taylor become ill?"],"answers":["Old Rough and Ready","John C. Calhoun","After sitting in the hot sun at a July 4th ceremony and eating cherries and iced milk"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-6","title":"Chapter 79: Fillmore - The Underground Railroad","steps":["Read chapter 79 aloud together. Listen for how the Underground Railroad worked.","Narrate the story of Harriet Tubman''s escapes (oral narration).","On a map, find the route north from the slave states to Canada, and San Francisco.","Add to your timeline: California becomes a free state; Fugitive Slave Law (1850)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 79","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02588"}],"questions":["Was the Underground Railroad really a railroad?","Why did people call Harriet Tubman \"Moses\"?","What famous book made many people hate slavery?"],"answers":["No, it was a chain of safe houses helping people escape north","She led so many enslaved people to freedom","Uncle Tom''s Cabin"],"notes":"The chapter claims many enslaved people with kind masters lived happily; that was a common view in 1917 but historians reject it. Worth discussing alongside first-person accounts such as Frederick Douglass."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-7","title":"Chapter 80: Pierce - The Story of \"Bleeding Kansas\"","steps":["Read chapter 80 aloud together. Listen for why Kansas became a battleground.","Narrate what happened on election day in Kansas in 1855 (oral narration).","On a map, find Kansas, Nebraska, Missouri, and the Gadsden Purchase in Arizona and New Mexico.","Add to your timeline: Kansas-Nebraska Act (1854)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 80","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02612"}],"questions":["What did the Kansas-Nebraska Bill let the people of a territory decide?","Why were there more votes than voters in Kansas?","Was Kansas finally admitted free or slave?"],"answers":["Whether to be a free state or a slave state","Thousands of Missourians crossed the border and voted illegally","Free, in 1861"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-8","title":"Chapter 81: Buchanan - The Story of the Mormons","steps":["Read chapter 81 aloud together. Listen for where the Mormons traveled and why.","Narrate the Mormon journey to the Great Salt Lake (oral narration).","On a map, find Nauvoo, Illinois, and the Great Salt Lake in Utah.","Add to your timeline: Building of Salt Lake City begins (1847)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 81","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02646"}],"questions":["Who became the Mormon leader after Joseph Smith''s death?","What does the name Utah mean, according to the book?","Was a battle fought in the \"Mormon War\"?"],"answers":["Brigham Young","Mountain Home","No"],"notes":"The author''s portrait of Joseph Smith and the Latter-day Saints is openly scornful in a way modern histories avoid; you may want to note that it reflects one outsider''s opinion."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-9","title":"Chapter 82: Buchanan - The First Shots","steps":["Read chapter 82 aloud together. Listen for Lincoln''s early life and what turned him against slavery.","Narrate how Lincoln grew from a poor boy into a leader (oral narration).","On a map, find Kentucky, Illinois, South Carolina, and Charleston harbor with Fort Sumter.","Add to your timeline: Lincoln elected; South Carolina secedes (1860)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 82","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02672"}],"questions":["Why was Lincoln called Honest Abe?","Which state was the first to leave the Union?","What did Lincoln say about a house divided against itself?"],"answers":["He was so upright in his dealings","South Carolina","It cannot stand"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u11","title":"Part VII: The Civil War (Lincoln)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-1","title":"Chapter 83: Lincoln - From Bull Run to Fort Donelson","steps":["Read chapter 83 aloud together. Listen for how Stonewall Jackson and Grant each got their nicknames.","Narrate the fight for Fort Donelson (oral narration).","On a map, find Fort Sumter, Bull Run, West Virginia, and Fort Donelson on the Cumberland River.","Add to your timeline: Fort Sumter falls; Battle of Bull Run (1861)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 83","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02691"}],"questions":["How did Thomas Jackson get the name Stonewall?","What did U. S. Grant''s initials come to stand for?","Which new state was formed during the war from part of Virginia?"],"answers":["His men stood firm \"like a stone wall\" at Bull Run","Unconditional Surrender","West Virginia"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-2","title":"Chapter 84: Lincoln - The Story of the First Battle Between Ironclads","steps":["Read chapter 84 aloud together. Listen for what made the two strange ships different from wooden ships.","Narrate the battle between the Monitor and the Merrimac (oral narration).","On a map, find Hampton Roads and Norfolk, Virginia, and the Southern coastline under blockade.","Add to your timeline: Monitor fights the Merrimac (1862)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 84","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02731"}],"questions":["Why was the blockade so harmful to the South?","What did people say the Monitor looked like?","Who invented the Monitor?"],"answers":["It cut off the cotton trade and supplies from abroad","A cheesebox on a raft","John Ericsson"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-3","title":"Chapter 85: Lincoln - The Battle of Shiloh and the Taking of New Orleans","steps":["Read chapter 85 aloud together. Listen for why the Mississippi River mattered so much.","Narrate the first morning of the battle of Shiloh (oral narration).","On a map, find Pittsburg Landing on the Tennessee River, New Orleans, and Vicksburg.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Shiloh; New Orleans taken (1862)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 85","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02751"}],"questions":["How did the battle of Shiloh get its name?","Which naval commander took New Orleans?","Which river city still held out for the South?"],"answers":["From a little log meeting house near the fighting","David Farragut","Vicksburg"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-4","title":"Chapter 86: Lincoln - The Slaves are Made Free","steps":["Read chapter 86 aloud together. Listen for Lincoln''s reasons for waiting to free the slaves.","Narrate what happened at Fredericksburg (oral narration).","On a map, find Antietam in Maryland and Fredericksburg in Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Emancipation Proclamation signed (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 86","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02784"}],"questions":["After which battle did Lincoln announce the Emancipation Proclamation?","When did the proclamation take effect?","What did many freed men do after emancipation?"],"answers":["Antietam","January 1, 1863","Joined the Union army"],"notes":"Marshall writes that the proclamation ended slavery; it actually freed people only in states in rebellion, and slavery ended everywhere with the 13th Amendment in 1865."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-5","title":"Chapter 87: Lincoln - Chancellorsville - The Death of Stonewall Jackson","steps":["Read chapter 87 aloud together. Listen for Jackson''s daring march through the Wilderness.","Narrate how Stonewall Jackson was wounded (oral narration).","On a map, find Chancellorsville and the Wilderness in Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Chancellorsville (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 87","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02814"}],"questions":["What first warned the Federals that Jackson''s men were coming?","Who wounded Stonewall Jackson?","What nickname did Hooker''s men give him?"],"answers":["Frightened birds and animals rushing out of the woods","His own men, by mistake in the dark","Fighting Joe"],"notes":"The book praises Confederate leaders warmly, as was common in 1917; you might talk about honoring courage while remembering what the Confederacy fought to keep."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-6","title":"Chapter 88: Lincoln - The Battle of Gettysburg","steps":["Read chapter 88 aloud together. Listen for what happened on each of the three days.","Narrate Pickett''s Charge (oral narration).","On a map, find Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, with Seminary Ridge and Cemetery Hill.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Gettysburg and the Gettysburg Address (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 88","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02854"}],"questions":["Who commanded the Union army at Gettysburg?","Which Confederate general led the great charge on the third day?","Why did Lincoln give a speech at Gettysburg?"],"answers":["George Meade","George Pickett","To dedicate a cemetery for the soldiers who died there"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-7","title":"Chapter 89: Lincoln-Grant''s Campaign-Sheridan''s Ride","steps":["Read chapter 89 aloud together. Listen for how the people of Vicksburg lived during the siege.","Narrate Sheridan''s ride to Cedar Creek (oral narration).","On a map, find Vicksburg, the Wilderness, Cold Harbor, Petersburg, and the Shenandoah Valley.","Add to your timeline: Vicksburg surrenders (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 89","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02878"}],"questions":["On what day did the Union army enter Vicksburg?","Where did families in Vicksburg hide from the shells?","What did Lincoln say when people wanted Grant removed?"],"answers":["July 4th","In cellars and caves dug in the hillsides","\"I cannot spare that man; he fights.\""]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-8","title":"Chapter 90: Lincoln - Sherman''s March to the Sea - Lincoln Re-Elected President","steps":["Read chapter 90 aloud together. Listen for what Sherman''s army destroyed and what it spared.","Narrate Sherman''s march from Atlanta to Savannah (oral narration).","On a map, find Atlanta, Savannah, and the Carolinas.","Add to your timeline: Sherman''s March to the Sea (1864)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 90","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02925"}],"questions":["What Christmas gift did Sherman send Lincoln?","What does Nevada mean?","Why did Lincoln say people should keep him as President?"],"answers":["The city of Savannah","Snowy","It was not wise to swap horses while crossing a stream"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-9","title":"Chapter 91: Lincoln - The End of the War - The President''s Death","steps":["Read chapter 91 aloud together. Listen for how Grant treated Lee''s army at the surrender.","Narrate the meeting at Appomattox (oral narration).","On a map, find Petersburg, Richmond, Appomattox Court House, and Washington, D.C..","Add to your timeline: Lee surrenders; Lincoln is assassinated (1865)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 91","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02946"}],"questions":["Where did Lee surrender to Grant?","Why did Grant let the soldiers keep their horses?","Who shot President Lincoln?"],"answers":["In a house at Appomattox Court House","They would need them for spring plowing","John Wilkes Booth"],"notes":"The closing lines quote formerly enslaved people in a mocking dialect; you may want to pause there and talk about how freedpeople themselves described Lincoln''s death."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u12","title":"Part VII: Reunion and a New Century (Johnson to Wilson)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-1","title":"Chapter 92: Johnson - How The President Was Impeached","steps":["Read chapter 92 aloud together. Listen for why Congress turned against President Johnson.","Narrate the quarrel over the Secretary of War''s office (oral narration).","On a map, find the former Confederate states, Nebraska, and Alaska with Sitka.","Add to your timeline: Alaska bought from Russia (1867); Johnson impeached (1868)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 92","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02974"}],"questions":["By how many votes was Johnson saved from removal?","Which country sold Alaska to the United States?","What did Nebraska''s Native name mean, according to the book?"],"answers":["One vote","Russia","Shallow water"],"notes":"The Reconstruction section reflects the old view that Black voters were ignorant and Northern \"carpet-baggers\" ruined the South; modern historians see Reconstruction as a real, if short-lived, effort at equal rights that was ended largely by violence. The book says Johnson was found guilty. In fact the Senate acquitted him in 1868, by a single vote."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-2","title":"Chapter 93: Grant - A Peaceful Victory","steps":["Read chapter 93 aloud together. Listen for how a quarrel with Britain was settled without war.","Narrate what the Alabama Claims were about (oral narration).","On a map, find Geneva in Switzerland, the Canadian border, and Colorado.","Add to your timeline: Geneva arbitration settles the Alabama Claims (1872)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 93","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03002"}],"questions":["What was the Alabama?","How was the quarrel settled?","Which state was admitted in 1876?"],"answers":["A Confederate warship built in Britain","By arbitration; Britain paid the U.S. money","Colorado"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-3","title":"Chapter 94: Hayes - Garfield - Arthur","steps":["Read chapter 94 aloud together. Listen for what the spoils system was.","Narrate how President Garfield was shot (oral narration).","On a map, find Washington, D.C., and the Ohio Canal where Garfield worked as a boy.","Add to your timeline: Civil service law passed (1883)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 94","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03013"}],"questions":["What jobs did Garfield do as a young man?","Why did Guiteau shoot Garfield?","What did the 1883 law require for many government jobs?"],"answers":["Barge boy, farmer, carpenter, teacher, lawyer, and soldier","He was angry at being refused a government job","Competitive examinations"],"notes":"The book presents the end of Reconstruction as a happy return to order; it also meant Black Southerners lost much of their voting power for decades."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-4","title":"Chapter 95: Cleveland - Harrison - Cleveland","steps":["Read chapter 95 aloud together. Listen for the six new states and what their names mean.","Narrate why Cleveland was called the Veto Mayor (oral narration).","On a map, find North and South Dakota, Montana, Washington, Idaho, Wyoming, Utah, and Chicago.","Add to your timeline: Columbian World''s Fair in Chicago (1893)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 95","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03035"}],"questions":["Which state first gave women the vote?","What made Cleveland''s presidency unusual?","What anniversary did the Chicago World''s Fair celebrate?"],"answers":["Wyoming","He served two terms with another President in between","400 years since Columbus reached America"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-5","title":"Chapter 96: McKinley - War and Sudden Death","steps":["Read chapter 96 aloud together. Listen for the islands that came under American rule.","Narrate how President McKinley was shot and how he acted afterward (oral narration).","On a map, find Cuba, Havana, Puerto Rico, the Philippines with Manila, Hawaii, and Buffalo.","Add to your timeline: Spanish-American War; Hawaii annexed (1898)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 96","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03059"}],"questions":["What happened to the battleship Maine?","Who was the last queen of Hawaii?","Which islands in the Pacific did Spain give up?"],"answers":["It was blown up in Havana harbor","Queen Liliuokalani","The Philippines"],"notes":"The book calls the Filipino fight for independence a \"rebellion\" and says Filipinos grew content with American rule; the Philippine-American War was long and costly, and Hawaii''s queen was overthrown largely by American businessmen."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-6","title":"Chapter 97: Roosevelt - Taft","steps":["Read chapter 97 aloud together. Listen for how the Panama Canal came to be built.","Narrate the Oklahoma land rush (oral narration).","On a map, find Panama, Colombia, Oklahoma, New Mexico, and Arizona.","Add to your timeline: Oklahoma becomes a state (1907)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 97","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03103"}],"questions":["Which country first tried to build a canal at Panama and gave up?","How old was Roosevelt when he became President?","What happened at noon on April 22, 1889, in Oklahoma?"],"answers":["France","Forty-two","Settlers rushed over the border to claim land"],"notes":"The land rush opened Indian Territory that had been promised to Native nations; worth noting whose land was being \"opened.\""},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-7","title":"Chapter 98: Wilson - Troubles With Mexico","steps":["Read chapter 98 aloud together. Listen for the changes Wilson made at home.","Narrate the trouble at Tampico and Vera Cruz (oral narration).","On a map, find Mexico, Tampico, Vera Cruz, and Niagara Falls.","Add to your timeline: Income tax begins under Wilson (1913)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 98","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03127"}],"questions":["What new tax did Wilson''s government begin?","What did the Child Labour Act try to do?","Who were the A.B.C. Powers?"],"answers":["An income tax","Keep young children from being made to work too hard","Argentina, Brazil, and Chile"],"notes":"Mexico is described as a \"troublous neighbor\" in a revolution the author only sees from the outside; the Mexican Revolution had real causes worth learning about."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-8","title":"Chapter 99: Wilson -The Great War","steps":["Read chapter 99 aloud together. Listen for why America first stayed neutral.","Narrate why President Wilson finally asked Congress to declare war (oral narration).","On a map, find Europe, Belgium, Germany, Britain, and France across the Atlantic.","Add to your timeline: United States enters the Great War (1917)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 99","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03152"}],"questions":["When did Wilson proclaim U.S. neutrality?","What German weapon at sea angered Americans most?","On what date did Wilson ask Congress for war?"],"answers":["August 1914","Submarines attacking ships","April 2, 1917"],"notes":"This chapter was written in the middle of World War I and reads like wartime persuasion against Germany; the book ends there, so you may want to tell how the war ended in 1918."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"Lesson plan by Freerange Library for “This Country of Ours: The Story of the United States” by H. E. Marshall (1917), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3761"}'::jsonb)
